    41	### Distances and geography — holds
    42	
    43	Thornwater's gate-to-stone distance is 110 yards and the follower keeps forty-five yards (ch6:9, :107–137; ch5:189–201). The Ring has a twenty-six-yard-stride floor, eleven banded uprights, its den beneath the eastern lip, a sixty-two-cord-and-twenty-stride perimeter, and the western line stone 220 yards from the middle (ch27:165–167; ch28:189–237; ch30:57–61, :121–145, :177–181, :211–217). The apparently different 207 yards is measured from the crossed floor's western edge to the stone and therefore agrees with the thirteen-yard radius (ch32:3–9).
    44	
    45	The Stair is 220 yards inside its line, forty steps down, with five courses per wall and the lone radius line between courses (ch49:3–5, :159–193; ch52:61–101, :291–311). The Hall lies on the recovered bearing and adds a third perimeter of the same size (ch52:177–239; ch53:3–35, :95–157; ch57:57–79). The fourth site is paced only from outside, is twice the radius, and lies on the continued bearing; the ending's four-site line is therefore supported without claiming identical interiors (ch58:117–229; ch60:237–265).
