    17	
    18	**Keep (canon — binding):**
    19	- Every book's spine: the major events, their order and outcomes, who lives and dies,
    20	  the ending state, ages, the calendar where later books depend on it.
    21	- Cael's fragment acquisitions — which fragment, in which book, under what engagement —
    22	  and the counts at each book's close. The system notice texts that later books quote.
    23	- The SECRET reveal schedule in `universe/CANON_RULES.md` and every reserved truth. A
    24	  rewrite may plant more; it may never disclose earlier than the schedule.
    25	- The universe rules, costs and limits in `universe/UNIVERSE_BIBLE.md`.
    26	- Relationships and their turning points as the existing books establish them.
    27	- Lines a later book quotes or calls back (log entries, notices, signature exchanges).
    28	  The book planner lists these as protected wording.
    29	
    30	**Change (the point of the edition):**
    31	- Length: about 300,000 words per book (owner). The existing books are 100–125k. The
    32	  extra ~190k comes from: developed fights given room (1,500–2,500+ words when the
    33	  changing problem earns it); learning encounters and training that show effort turning
    34	  into capability; events the current edition summarizes, put on the page; supporting
    35	  cast given private wants, independent decisions and arcs across the formula's 8–10
    36	  person cast; humor and warmth between people; brief POV cutaways; new episodes that
    37	  fit canon and do not alter any book's end state.
    38	- Shape: open-range movements of six or more chapters, one compact brief per movement,
    39	  no per-chapter cards or per-chapter gates. Roughly 55–65 chapters of ~5,000 words per
    40	  book in 7–9 movements; the author owns chapter boundaries.
    41	- Rhythm: the owner-selected numerical formula (compiled into every prompt). Baseline
    42	  of the current edition, measured by `tools/formula_metrics.py`: sentence mean ~17.5
    43	  (target 14.6), paragraph median ~44 words (target ~18), Flesch RE ~67 (target 72.3),
    44	  FK grade ~8.3 (target 6.8). The edition should move toward the targets.
    45	- POV: about 87% Cael, about 13% brief purposeful cutaways spread over 4–5 named
    46	  characters. Cutaways may never reveal what a reserved-truth boundary withholds.
    47	
    48	## Non-negotiable
    49	
    50	- **Reader Standard (Gate 27, owner):** written for a thirteen-year-old. Clean language
    51	  (no profanity, obscenity, crude slang or blasphemy, including "damn", "hell" as an
    52	  oath, "bastard" and softer cousins); moral goodness (honesty, courage, loyalty,
    53	  restraint, care for the weak, at a cost; wrong named as wrong; cruelty never
    54	  rewarded); violence with cost, fear and consequence and no gore; no sexual content
    55	  or innuendo. Outranks every voice preference. See `craft/VOICE_CHARTER.md` end section.
    56	- **Names:** keep every existing canon name. The registry's flagged collisions
    57	  (Vell/Velmere, the -vane cluster, Wray/Greyvane, Bracken/Brom) are the owner's call
    58	  and are NOT renamed in this edition. New minor characters the expansion needs may be
    59	  named by the planner, screened by ear against `craft/NAME_REGISTRY.md`, and listed
    60	  under "Names pending owner approval" in the book map. Never invent a name for an
    61	  existing canon role.
    62	- **Audio-first:** this ships as an audiobook. Clear referents, clear attribution,
    63	  names distinct by ear, punctuation that exposes meaning.
    64	
    65	## Where things live
    66	
    67	- Canon (read-only for this edition): `universe/UNIVERSE_BIBLE.md`,
    68	  `universe/CANON_RULES.md`, `universe/STATE_LEDGER.md`, `craft/NAME_REGISTRY.md`,
    69	  `craft/VOICE_CHARTER.md`, `series/THE_FRACTURED_PATH_SERIES.md`.
    70	- Source edition (the story being retold): `books/<book>/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md` and
    71	  `books/<book>/chapters/chapter-NN.md`. Read them for events, not for sentences.
    72	- This edition: `editions/monroe-1.3/<book>/` — `BOOK_MAP.md`, `STATE_LEDGER.md`,
    73	  `packets/MOVEMENT-NNN.md`, `manuscript/chapter-NN.md`, `state/movement-NNN/`.
    74	- Series-level edition maps: `editions/monroe-1.3/SERIES_MAP.md`, `CHARACTERS.md`.
    75	- Metrics: `python3 editions/monroe-1.3/tools/formula_metrics.py <chapters...>`.
    76	
    77	## Rhythm calibration (measured, 2026-10-01)
    78	
    79	Book 1 Movement 1 — the first movement drafted from this brief — overshot the formula in
    80	the short direction: sentence mean 8.8 words (target 14.6), 41% of sentences at five
    81	words or fewer (target ~28%), 0.3% at forty-plus (target ~3.3%), Flesch-Kincaid grade 2.8
    82	(target 6.8), about 106 dialogue tags per 10k words (target ~41). Paragraph median (15)
    83	and scene spacing were close. So, when drafting:
    84	
    85	- Let thought, action and description run in full, well-built sentences. Compound and
    86	  complex sentences with a clear hierarchy are the house texture, not the exception.
    87	- Join a run of three or four clipped statements into one sentence when they are one thought.
    88	- Keep short sentences for beats that land — a recognition, a hit, a turn — not as default.
    89	- Give each chapter a few deliberately long, readable sentences (forty words or more)
    90	  where an action or a thought earns the length.
    91	- Drop "he said" / "she said" when the paragraph already makes the speaker clear.
    92	- Keep short paragraphs (median ~18 words) and scene breaks about every 950 words.
    93	
    94	## No scripted prose surgery (2026-10-01)
    95	
    96	Rhythm and length repairs are done by reading, sentence by sentence, never by a script that
    97	splits sentences or paragraphs at clause or sentence boundaries. Two repairs did that and
    98	each needed a recheck to find the splits that broke a thought. Where the formula and a
    99	character's speech disagree, keep the speech: one-to-three-word dialogue lines and the short
   100	landing beats of a fight are not drift to be repaired.
   101	
   102	## Working ranges and accepted drift (coordinator, 2026-10-01, after five repairs)
   103	
   104	Five movement repairs show three of the formula's numbers pulling against the brief's other
   105	asks (full sentences as house texture, speech kept as people speak, paragraphs broken where the
   106	thought turns). Repairs that chase one of them push another out. So, until the owner rules
   107	otherwise, these are the working ranges a movement is held to, and repairs aim at them — not at
   108	the decimals:
   109	
   110	| Measure | Formula target | Working range | Why |
   111	|---|---|---|---|
   112	| Sentence mean | 14.6 | 13–15.5 | primary target — keep |
   113	| ≥40-word share | 3.3% | 2.5–4.5% | primary target — keep |
   114	| Words per scene | ~950 | 850–1,050 | primary target — keep |
   115	| ≤5-word share | 27.7% | up to ~34% | dialogue-heavy movements; short speech is kept |
   116	| Paragraph median | ~18 | up to ~30 | the 18 is an ASR pause proxy, not print; long cutaways run longer |
   117	| Flesch-Kincaid grade | 6.8 | 3.5–6 | clear short words; the syllable estimator is approximate |
   118	
   119	Measured drift outside a working range is reported in the movement's review; a repair is for
   120	the primary three and for concrete defects.
   121	
   122	## Source-reuse gate tightened to 8 words (coordinator, 2026-10-02)
   123	
   124	The 10-word gate let distinctive source phrasing through in 6–9-word fragments (the first
   125	movement's opening line, for example, echoed the current edition's). Measured at 8 words, movements
   126	written after authors began self-checking run 1–6 shared runs per 10k; the worst ran ~30. From now
   127	on `ed.sh overlap` and every editorial review use runs of **8 or more** words. Every listed run that
   128	BOOK_MAP does not protect is rewritten — re-composed, not a synonym swapped. Names, Path names,
   129	ranks and plain facts of canon will sometimes share a short run; re-compose the sentence around them.
   130	Movements closed under the 10-word gate get one book-level 8-word sweep at book completion.
   131	
   132	## Draft from your own event list, not from the source page (coordinator, 2026-10-02)
   133	
   134	Book 3 Movement 6's review found the retold scenes in ch42–44 to be close paraphrase: 23–32% of
   135	sentences followed a source sentence word for word in order, with words varied enough to pass the
   136	8-word gate. Everything the author invented was clean. The cause is drafting with the source open.
   137	So, for every movement:
   138	
   139	1. Read the source chapters the packet names, once, for what happens and who these people are.
   140	2. Close them. Write a private event list in your own words — what happens, in what order, who
   141	   wants what, what changes. Keep it in your working notes, not in the manuscript.
   142	3. Draft from that list and the book map. Do not reopen the source chapters while drafting.
   143	   Choose your own entry point into each scene and your own order of beats; the source's scene
   144	   shape is not canon, only its events are.
   145	4. Only protected wording (BOOK_MAP) is copied, exactly, from the map — not from the source page.

