    82	
    83	---
    84	
    85	It was a ship's plank.
    86	
    87	Somebody had brought it up from some river long ago, twelve feet of oak as thick as his wrist, tarred black, and set it breast-high on two posts under the eave of the lower inn, where the weather could not get at it and the whole meadow could. Slips were tacked along it from one end to the other. They were on anything that would take a mark: bark, mostly, peeled thin and written on the pale side, and scraps of hide, and one cedar shingle, and a single piece of real paper that had been somebody's waybill and was written across the back. Each hung by one iron tack, and the oak round them was pitted with the holes of older tacks, hundreds of them, in drifts, so that he could see where the slips had clustered thickest in other years. It was a board that had been used hard for a long time by people who did not waste a nail.
