   148	
   149	He looked at Lira and then at Cael. Then he looked at each of them again, in the same order, just as carefully, as though the first look had been only a draft.
   150	
   151	"That's all a hirer wants to hear from me," he said. "Did they come back, and did his goods come with them. I've been asked it every day for thirty years and I've not yet been asked a second question."
   152	
   153	"Are you the keeper?" said Lira.
   154	
   155	"I keep the book."
   156	
   157	"What are you?"
   158	
   159	It was a western question, and she asked it in the western way, chin up, as one fighter asks another across a yard. Cael saw that he understood exactly what she meant by it. Something moved at the corner of his mouth.
   160	
   161	"Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads," he said. "Pike. Now you know less than you did."
   162	
   163	He set the book on the plank. There was a hole bored in the oak at that place, the size of a hen's egg, and a horn ink-pot sat down in it to its lip, where no elbow could tip it; beside the hole lay a flat grey river stone as big as a hand. He opened the book and put the stone on the corner of the leaf. Cael understood then what it was for, since a man with one arm cannot hold a page down and write on it as well.
   164	
