   209	It was a job that Cael had done before, at a higher price and for worse people.
   210	
   211	He thought about it through the first two crossings, with his hand on a rail that had been worn pale by other hands. For fourteen sittings he had climbed four steps in Ostrand to stand on a number carved in a wool exchange's floor, and he had stood there to be looked at. A seat had looked, under a hood, and a procurator, and two hundred people on benches, and every one of them had been deciding what he was while he stood. It had cost a house its year, and a registrar thirteen sleepless nights, and four people their enrollments.
   212	
   213	Here he was worth two marks among five, and what looked at him was a river. There were alders on the far bank, and a heron, and upstream, a long way off, something that might have been a boat drawn up under a bank. Whoever was in it would see five heads on a rail and count them, and that was all they would ever want from him; nobody on that water cared what the heads had in them.
   214	
   215	"You're smiling," said Karis. She was amidships, with her notebook, timing the crossing by Hesk's clock.
   216	
   217	"I've been stared at for a living before. I didn't know it could be this restful."
   218	
   219	"I shall enter that you said so. Nine minutes, and no stops." She looked across at the round shed, where a brown shape went steadily about a post. "He hasn't stopped once all morning. I've a column for it."
   220	
   221	Lira had been looking at the shed as well, at every crossing, and had not said one word about it.
   222	
   223	The log came down on the fifth.
   224	
   225	It was not a log. It was a whole tree, a pale one, with its root-plate still on it like a great clenched fist, and it came round the upstream bend riding low and slow, and turning as it came. The ferryman's boy saw it first and shouted, for on a river, it seemed, a person might shout as he pleased.
   226	
   227	"The cable," said Seln sharply. "If the roots take the cable it will have us round."
   228	
   229	Three things happened in Cael at once, and he had been waiting for them. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, as breath gathers before a shout, for a push at that range would have turned a tree. Storm leaned, and showed him the air between the scow and the trunk as a thing with a grain in it. And Wind remembered a painted floor in his feet, and offered to put him somewhere else.
   230	
   231	*Hold*, he told all three fragments. It took him no longer than it takes to lower a hand.
   232	
   233	Brom had the sweep, which was a pole of ash twenty feet long, shod with iron, and he had it out of its crutch and over the upstream rail before the boy had finished shouting. He set the iron against the trunk, just ahead of the roots, and the tree came on and leaned its whole wet weight into him.
   234	
   235	Cael saw his forearms.
   236	
   237	It was Iron Skin, which was the whole of Brom's Path, and he had seen it come a hundred times, in a harness room and in a river: the skin ceased to crease, and went the grey of a hearthstone, from the wrist upward. It began now, and it got as far as the cuff of his coat before Brom looked down at his own arm, as a man looks at a dog that has got up on a chair.
   238	
   239	The grey went out of it.
   240	
   241	He grunted, and his boots slid a hand's breadth on the planks, and he bent his knees and put his back into the pole until the cords stood out in his neck. He was a large young man pushing a tree with a stick, and he had never looked less like anything else. The root-plate swung, slowly, and went wide of the cable by the length of an arm, and the whole pale mass turned under the stern and went on down the river.
   242	
   243	"Like a large man," said Brom, breathing hard, and looking at his forearms, which were red. "It's heavier that way."
   244	
   245	"Is it worse?" said Cael.
   246	
   247	"I don't know yet. I've never weighed one before."
   248	
   249	---
   250	
   251	The ferryman made his mark on the slip with a thumb dipped in tar: a ring with a dot in it, which Cael supposed was the shed.
   252	
   253	Pike was at the plank when they brought it up. He took the slip in his one hand and read it, and then he read it again, with his lips moving a little, and after that he read it aloud. It was not for them. He turned his shoulder to the meadow and said it out over the nearest tents in a level carrying voice, and a woman frying dough looked up from her pan.
   254	
   255	"Ferry, the landing's goods. Six crossings stood. Nothing lost. Two marks."
   256	
   257	Then he opened the book at their leaf and put the stone on its corner and wrote, and when the ink was down he said the same words over again, exactly, to the same tents.
   258	
   259	"He says everything twice," said Lira in Cael's ear. "He looks at you twice and he reads it twice. Is it his age?"
   260	
   261	"I don't think so. I think it's for something."
   262	
   263	"For what?"
   264	
   265	"I don't know yet. He'd tell us if we asked, and I'd rather see it."
   266	
   267	There was an iron box bolted to the nearer post, under the plank, with a lock on it the size of a fist. Pike opened it with a key from round his neck and took out two coins, and laid them on the oak in front of Brom, who happened to be standing nearest.
   268	
   269	They were silver, small and thick, and a good deal worn, and Brom picked them up and held them in the middle of his palm, and went on looking at them after Pike had locked the box.
   270	
   271	"What's the matter with them?" said Pike.
   272	
   273	"Nothing." Brom closed his hand. "It's the first money I've ever had that nobody sent me."
   274	
   275	Pike looked at him, and looked again.
   276	
   277	"Well, it spends the same," he said. "That's the pity of it." But he went on watching Brom's back for a little while as they walked away down the meadow.
   278	
   279	The woman with the dough had gone back to her pan. She had heard it, though, and so had the man with the harness across his knees, and the three children; and by dark, Cael supposed, the whole meadow would know that five strangers had stood on a boat all day and lost nothing. It was not much to be known for; but it was the first thing that anybody east of a line knew about them at all, and it happened to be true.
   280	
   281	It bought flour, and salt, and a piece of bacon no bigger than a hand, and Brom told them that it would have kept the gelding for one day and the half of another. Karis wrote the line into the field archive as she had heard it said, and drew her second rule under two of its words.
   282	
   283	"*Nothing lost,*" she said. "And *stood*. He gives an outcome no subject. He doesn't say who stood. He says only that it was done, and what came of it." She considered the page. "I think that's his whole grammar. I shall want a good many more before I'm sure."
   284	
   285	Lira had gone up to the long eave again, and this time she was away rather longer, and she came back walking fast.
   286	
   287	"We're on it," she said. "The sheet. There's a new line at the bottom, and it's wet." She sat down. "Thirteenth. Of thirteen."
   288	
   289	"It's one line," said Brom. "We've been here five days."
   290	
   291	"I know what it is. I'm telling you where it is." She pulled her knees up. "Every other line's a name, or a trade. *The hide-woman's two.* That sort of thing. Ours is the only one that's a list. And the top one's a single word."
   292	
   293	"What word?"
   294	
   295	"*Teague.*"
   296	
   297	Nobody knew it, and Cael turned it over once, as he turned any new name, and found nothing under it.
   298	
   299	"Aren't you coming to look?" said Lira. "You read everything. You read the back of a waybill this morning."
   300	
   301	"Not yet."
   302	
   303	"Why not?"
   304	
   305	He thought about how to say it, for he had been on a great many boards. A crossroads in Ardenmere had carried him, in chalk, and a hall at Norhold, and four black ones in a sunk yard, and every one of them had told the people looking at it what to think of him before he opened his mouth.
   306	
   307	"I'll read it when there's something on it that I did," he said. "One line for standing on a boat isn't worth the walk. I'd like the first thing that sheet tells me to be true."
   308	
   309	He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
   310	
   311	"Two marks," said Lira, to the fire. "For standing still."
   312	
   313	"It's harder than it looks."
   314	
   315	"You've had practice."
   316	
   317	"Two years of it."
   318	
   319	"Then you had better show the rest of us how it's done," said Lira. "Because when that tree came round the bend I had one foot on the rail, and I don't remember putting it there."

