     1	# Chapter 5 — The Stretch
     2	
     3	By the forty-first day they had three lines in Pike's book and a place at the edge of the carters' fire.
     4	
     5	Nobody had offered it to them. A fire burned most nights in the middle of the meadow, a little way from the ash, and the people with carts sat round it on their own shafts and upturned buckets. For a month the five had kept to their own fire, forty yards off. Then, on the evening after the wash, the woman with the hides had moved her bucket a foot to the left without looking at anybody, and it had left a gap in the ring. Brom had looked at the gap for a whole day before he would let them sit in it.
     6	
     7	Nine marks earned, the book said. It did not say that Lira's palms had skinned over, or that Brom could now drop a tree at the first slip and catch it again at the second, or that Karis had held a point unlit to the count of three hundred and then put it out herself. Cael kept those in his own book, at the foot of each day, under a line that had not changed since a ring of churned turf.
     8	
     9	The carter came in at dusk with one cart and one mule.
    10	
    11	He was a short, thick man, burnt as brown as a nut, and his cart was loaded to the rails with sacks. He chocked his wheels, saw to the mule, and sat down in the ring with a yard of empty ground on either side of him. Cael had seen him once already that day, at noon, handing a slip across the plank; and he had watched the meadow read that slip all afternoon, crew after crew, and go back to its dinner.
    12	
    13	*Thornwater road, the stretch. A crew to walk ahead. Nine marks.*
    14	
    15	"Nine," Lira had said, at the plank. "For walking. That's as much as we've made since we got here."
    16	
    17	"And nobody's taken it," Brom had said. "So there's a thing we don't know."
    18	
    19	The carter ate what he had brought. He did it slowly, looking into the fire, and when he had done he wiped his knife on his knee and spoke to the flames.
    20	
    21	"Two carts went quiet on it last month," he said.
    22	
    23	Nobody answered him. On the far side of the ring the old man from under the ash shifted his feet.
    24	
    25	"It's a noon road. Always was. You take the stretch with the sun straight over, and you've no trouble, and I've done it so for twenty-two years." He turned the knife over. "I lost a day at the ferry to an axle. I'll not be at the mouth of it by noon now, whatever I do. I might be there by the middle of the afternoon, if nothing else goes."
    26	
    27	"That's a noon price," said the old man, to nobody in particular, "for a road that mightn't be walked at noon."
    28	
    29	"It's what I've got."
    30	
    31	"What's on it?" said Lira.
    32	
    33	She had asked it clearly, across the fire, as she would have asked a coach what a drill was for. Cael felt Karis go still beside him, with her pencil half out of its spine. Several of the people in the ring looked at the ground.
    34	
    35	The carter turned his head and considered Lira for some time. He did not seem offended. He seemed to be working out how much of an answer a person was owed who knew no better than to ask.
    36	
    37	"Walk quiet," he said, "and you'll not need telling."
    38	
    39	"That isn't—"
    40	
    41	"It's what there is." He looked past her, at the rest of them, and his eyes stopped longest on Brom. "You're the ones had her wagon out of the wash. Before the water. And never lit, she says, nor shouted, nor one of you did a thing but pull."
    42	
    43	"Yes," said Cael.
    44	
    45	"I'd as soon have that."
    46	
    47	He said no more, and the fire talked for a while by itself.
    48	
    49	Cael did what he always did, and began with the claim, which was a mile of road that carters crossed only at one hour of the day. Under it stood two carts that had not come home, and a price that the whole meadow thought too small, and a word, *quiet*, that everybody out here used for a thing done and also for a thing that happened to a cart. He had no notion what walked on that stretch; but a man who had kept the road for twenty-two years had just said what he wanted for it, and it was the one thing they had spent a month learning to do.
    50	
    51	"Hands," he said, low.
    52	
    53	Brom's went up first, and then Seln's. Lira's followed, with her jaw set, for she had not forgiven the answer. Karis kept hers down.
    54	
    55	"The boxes," she said. "I left them three days in that loft, for a fence, and I didn't sleep. This is two days out and I can't tell how many back."
    56	
    57	The carter had heard her. Out here, it seemed, everybody heard everything.
    58	
    59	"What boxes?"
    60	
    61	"Three. They're paper."
    62	
    63	He looked at his sacks, and at her, and shrugged.
    64	
    65	"They'll ride on the grain. I'll not charge a fare for paper."
    66	
    67	Karis put her hand up.
    68	
    69	---
    70	
    71	Pike marked the slip at first light, with the mist still on the river.
    72	
    73	He had made them a sign, since five names would not go on a scrap of bark: five short strokes side by side, like a tally with its bar left off. He drew it under the carter's own mark and read the whole aloud to an empty meadow, and then he handed it across the plank and looked at each of them twice.
    74	
    75	"It's a noon road," he said.
    76	
    77	"He's told us."
    78	
    79	"Then you know what I know."
    80	
    81	They went out by the east road, over the saddle. Beyond the top the country changed, as Seln had said it would: the road shrank to two ruts in a grey scrub that stood as high as a man's belt, and the mule cart, which had looked a toy in the meadow, fitted those ruts as a foot fits a boot. The three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas. Karis walked beside the off wheel, where she could have put her hand on them, and never did.
    82	
    83	They passed the wash in the middle of the morning. It was dry again. The bank on the western side was still raw where a wagon had been dragged up it, and on the eastern lip stood a thorn stump with the bark worn off it in three bright rings.
    84	
    85	Lira looked at the stump as she went by, and smiled with one side of her mouth.
    86	
    87	Nobody spoke for the whole of that day, and Cael found that it was no longer anything he did on purpose. Brom carried the bill across his shoulder with the blade out. Lira's knife rode on her hip. At noon they sat in the ruts and ate cold cake, and the carter, who had watched them out of the side of his eye since the saddle, stopped watching.
    88	
    89	The mule took to Brom at the first halt. It walked the length of its tether to reach him and laid the whole weight of its long head on his shoulder, and sighed.
    90	
    91	"He doesn't do that," said the carter. It was the first thing he had said since the plank.
    92	
    93	"They do, with me." Brom stood quite still under it. "I've never known why. I think it's that I'm the same height as a post."
    94	
    95	Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
    96	
    97	They camped cold, in a hollow, a good way back from a stream. Lira and Seln took the first watch and Brom and Karis the second. It was Cael's night to be the fifth, which he spent lying on his back, hearing every sound the scrub made and failing to sleep through any of them.
    98	
    99	The second day went well until two hours after noon.
   100	
   101	They had forded the valley's water twice by then, at shallow places with no name, in good light, and the carter had begun to walk with his shoulders down. Then the off wheel gave a crack like a stick breaking. It ran on three turns more, wobbling, and came off its axle altogether, and the cart sat down on that corner with a jolt that sent Karis's hand to the canvas at last.
   102	
   103	The pin had sheared. That was the least of it, for the wheel had dropped onto a stone as it went, and one of the curved timbers of its rim had split along the grain for half its length.
   104	
   105	Brom looked at it. Then he looked at the carter, who was looking at the sun.
   106	
   107	"How long?" said the carter.
   108	
   109	"I don't know yet. Have you wire?"
   110	
   111	He had wire. They got a pole under the axle, and Brom lifted, with the cords standing in his neck and no grey in him anywhere, while Seln and Cael built stones up under the bed. He whittled a new pin out of thorn with the hedging bill, and bound the split rim round and round, and twisted the wire tight with the back of the blade. Karis timed him, since nobody had told her not to.
   112	
   113	"An hour and four minutes," she said, when the wheel was on. "By the only clock there is."
   114	
   115	The carter said nothing at all. He took the mule's head, and they went on faster than before, and the wired wheel ticked at every turn.
   116	
   117	---
   118	
   119	They came to the mouth of it with the sun a hand's breadth above the ridge behind them.
   120	
   121	Cael knew the place before anybody named it. The scrub ended, as though it had been cut with a knife, and the valley drew in between two long shoulders of hill, and from one shoulder to the other the whole floor was grass. It was the pale grass of the lower river, chest high, and with the light coming level along it every blade stood up gold on one side and grey on the other. The ruts went into it and were lost within thirty yards.
   122	
   123	The carter had stopped the mule. He stood with his fist in its cheek-strap and looked up the valley, and he spoke for the second time that day.
   124	
   125	"That's it. A mile, near enough. The fence stands across the head of it, and you can't see that from here."
