     1	# Chapter 6 — Thornwater
     2	
     3	The woman who held the lantern kept it low, at her knee, where it would light the ground and not her own eyes.
     4	
     5	Cael noticed that before he noticed her. She was a broad grey-braided woman of fifty or sixty with her sleeves rolled to the elbow. She stood in the gap of her gate and counted them through it as a farmer counts sheep at a hurdle: the dark coat first, then the two of them abreast, and the mule, and the cart, and last a very large young man with a hedging bill. She moved her lips for each. When the tailboard was inside she put her shoulder to the gate and shut it, and dropped a bar as thick as his arm.
     6	
     7	Then she let out her breath, and so did everybody else.
     8	
     9	There was a platform beside the gate, on the inner face of the fence, six feet up, with a ladder to it. A man stood there. He had a bow in his left hand and an arrow on the string, and Cael had seen enough people threaten him to know that this one was not doing it. The bow hung at the length of his arm. The arrow pointed at the planks between his boots. He was looking out over the top of the posts, down the valley, at something a hundred and ten yards off beside a white stone, and he did not once look round at the people who had just come in under him.
    10	
    11	Inside the fence there was noise. Cael had forgotten, in a mile, that there could be. A dog was barking at the mule, and a door stood open on a lit room, and somewhere a child was being told for the last time to come in.
    12	
    13	The carter took his hand off the mule's nose. He wiped the palm down his breeches, slowly, and turned to the five of them, in the lantern's light, with an expression that Cael could not at first place. It turned out to be respect, on a face that was not much used to showing it.
    14	
    15	"First crew I've hired that knew to shut up," he said.
    16	
    17	He did not mean their mouths, or not only. Cael had seen him look at Brom's forearms as the tailboard came through the gate, and at Lira's feet.
    18	
    19	"What was that?" said Lira.
    20	
    21	She had her back against a post of the fence, and her hands had begun to shake now that they were allowed to. She had turned to the woman with the lantern as she would have turned to a referee.
    22	
    23	"Stillhound," said the woman.
    24	
    25	"A what?"
    26	
    27	It was the carter who answered her. He was unbuckling a trace, and he spoke over his shoulder, in the tone of a man who has been asked what rain is.
    28	
    29	"It's a stillhound. You shut up. It went home."
    30	
    31	He seemed to think that covered it, and he led the mule away toward the barn.
    32	
    33	Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
    34	
