     5	Cael had not expected that to be hard, and it was the worst mile of the four he had made on that ground. There was no cart this time, and no buckle; there were five people in two ruts, with Seln thirty paces out on the left verge and Brom at the tail, and the pale grass stood chest-high on either hand with the light coming through it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other. Lira went the whole way with her right hand half lifted and her head a little on one side, listening for a hole in that noise, and her hand never rose above her belt.
     6	
     7	He put nine things down at every stride, all the same, since they had no means of knowing that it was the wrong hour.
     8	
     9	It was strange to walk that mile for a third reason. He had gone up it once behind a cart, for nine marks, with a grey thing pacing him; and he had come down it at noon behind the same cart empty, when it was nothing but grass. Now he went up it in the afternoon with a forty-mark slip in his shirt, toward a gate whose keeper had sent for somebody else. The ground was the same each time. What changed was the thing he had come to do on it, and he was beginning to think that this was the only sort of change of which the country out here took any notice.
    10	
    11	They passed the split shaft, pointing at the sky, and the stony swell, and came to the gully's fan, where the pale stones lay across the valley under a skin of water. Karis stopped there. She stood in the middle of the wet with her boots in it and looked down, and then to the right, at the slope that a grey thing had climbed sooner than set one foot where she was standing; and she wrote a single word, walking, and shut the book.
    12	
    13	There was nothing beside the white stone, and the gate was open before they reached it.
    14	
    15	The woman with the grey braid stood in the gap, as she had stood there once with a lantern, and counted them through with her lips: the dark coat, the two abreast, the small pale one, the large one. She shut the gate behind Brom with her shoulder and dropped the bar. Up on the platform the bowman was looking down the valley over their heads, with his bow hanging at the length of his arm.
    16	
    17	"I sent for the one at the top of his sheet," she said.
    18	
    19	"He's a day and a half up the cold road," said Cael. "Pike struck his hold. In front of the meadow."
    20	
    21	"Struck it."
    22	
    23	"With one line. We took the slip."
    24	
    25	She went over them slowly, and Cael knew what she was reading, for he had read it himself in a jug's worth of lamplight three nights ago: four lines in a book, and nothing gone wrong in any of them.
    26	
