   197	The beach was a long way off. The boy was a small dark shape up at the head of the bar with a grey thing dancing round his knees. She had come twenty yards down the gravel, or more, a pace at a time, and all of them in one direction; and there was black water close on her left and close on her right.
   198	
   199	Eight paces behind her right shoulder the seventh was standing.
   200	
   201	It was a good deal bigger than the one in front of her, and one of its ears hung in two pieces. It was not looking at her, and that was the part she would remember afterward, when she remembered any of it. It looked past her shoulder, up the bar, at nothing, with its weight forward on its toes. It had been lying on that spot when she ran at it yesterday. It had stood there for the whole of this, and a mild man had told her in a loft, in so many words, exactly where it would be and what it was for.
   202	
   203	*He is never the one you are looking at.*
   204	
   205	She had looked at the wrong dog for twenty yards.
   206	
   207	She stood quite still. The feinter was in front of her and the other was behind, and there was a pool on either hand with no floor to it. Her feet wanted to go. Every rank that she had ever been given had been given her for going: there were twenty feet of air between her and the far beach, and she could have been standing on those stones before either animal had turned its head.
   208	
   209	She had run round by the bed of a wash for less, with the water coming.
   210	
   211	*No.*
   212	
   213	It cost more than it had at the wash. She felt it go through her from the heels up, like a rope taking a load.
   214	
   215	Up at the head of the bar, the big fool declared.
   216	
   217	She saw the grey of it at the edge of her eye, and she felt the discharge; it was as though the weight of him had doubled. He had done it exactly on the count. She had never known him early or late for anything.
   218	
   219	Both heads went round.
   220	
   221	The feinter's swung away from her like a needle to the north, and behind her shoulder she felt the big one's turn as well, though she kept her eyes to the front. For a breath neither of them had any more interest in her than in the gravel.
   222	
   223	Then the feinter ran.
   224	
   225	It went by her within reach of her hand, flat out, up the spine of the bar toward the grey man on the stone, and it did not so much as bend its line to clear her. She was a post. She was a thing that happened to be standing in its road.
   226	
   227	And she was behind it.
   228	
   229	---
   230	
   231	She had run that gravel three times yesterday, and the dog had never run it at all with anybody after it.
   232	
   233	That was the only edge she had. It had four legs and a start of three yards. She had the spine of the bar by heart: where it was firm, and where one flat stone rocked, and the place a third of the way up where the ridge bent to the left and a person who went straight put a foot in soft shingle.
   234	
   235	At the first stride the burst said *now*.
   236	
   237	She told it no. At the second it said so again, a good deal louder, since the thing in front of her was pulling away and every yard of her life had taught her what to do when something pulled away. She refused it at the third stride and at the fourth, and after that she did not count. There was no room in her for counting. There was a grey back, and the line of the stones, and her own two feet, which were doing a thing that she had not known they could do by themselves.
   238	
   239	She was fast.
   240	
   241	It came to her at about the tenth stride, with something very like outrage. She had always supposed that the speed was the burst. The running had been the walk up to it, as a singer clears her throat; no panel had ever given her a rank for the running. She had not known that there was this much of it. The dog took the bend of the ridge straight, and its hind feet went into the soft, and she went round by the firm stones and had a yard back.
   242	
   243	She was gaining on it. She was gaining on a four-legged animal, on gravel, in bad light, with nothing in her feet but feet.
   244	
   245	Nobody was scoring it.
   246	
   247	That came to her in the middle of the run as well, and she had no time to do anything about it. There was no chalk. No slate hung on any wall. A man stood on a bank with a bow who might or might not have been able to make her out at that distance. She was running the best twenty-five yards of her life for no one at all, and she noticed that she minded.
   248	
   249	The weight went off the river.
   250	
   251	It went all at once, like a cart lifted off a foot. The big fool had put his Path down. In front of her the dog checked. It had come to the edge of the gravel at the bar's head, where the channel began, and the declaration it was running to had gone out like a lamp; it stood there on three legs with one forefoot raised over the water, and its head began to swing.
   252	
   253	She took it behind the shoulder.
   254	
   255	She put the long knife in low, where she had seen the boy put his, and the whole of her run went down her arm behind it. It came round at her. Its jaws shut on the air a hand's breadth from her face, so near that she felt its breath, and then it folded.
   256	
   257	There was the stop still to make.
   258	
   259	She was two strides from the edge with all that speed in her, and she did what she had spent a month on a river bar failing to do, and one morning under a platform doing at last while a man lifted two fingers from a bow. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet.
   260	
   261	The edge was round stones lying on round stones, with the water through them.
   262	
   263	She had said so herself, yesterday, about the tail. She had not thought to say it of the sides.
   264	
   265	Her right foot rolled outward, and went on rolling. It went past the place where an ankle stops, and she heard it go. It made a small wet sound, like a green twig bent double, and she knew that sound. She had heard it once at Fenmark from a girl two places above her on the list, who had not been on the list the term after.
   266	
   267	Then she was sitting in the river to her waist, with a dead hound across her shin and the long knife still in her hand.
   268	
   269	There was nothing at the end of it.
   270	
   271	She had not burst. She had not used her Path at all. So the air had not cracked, and the gravel had not gone up in a fan, and not one head on that river had turned to see where she was. That was the point of it; she knew perfectly well that it was the point. She had done the fastest and the quietest thing of her whole life, and the proof that she had done it rightly was that nothing whatever had happened.
   272	
   273	The pain was coming. She could feel it on its way, like somebody climbing a stair.
   274	
   275	"Five," said a voice across the water, through its teeth.
   276	
   277	The boy had turned round. He was three yards off, and he was looking at her with the eyes that went over a thing a piece at a time and missed none of it, and in a moment they would get to her foot.
   278	
   279	"Lira."
   280	
   281	She had one thing to say, and she picked it with care. There were dogs still on that gravel, and she would not have him kneeling over an ankle.
   282	
   283	"I'm fine."
   284	
   285	He knew what that was worth, and she watched him know it. She held his eye until he gave way.
   286	
   287	"Four left," she said. "Mine makes it four. Somebody ought to keep the count." She pointed past him with the knife, since her hand was shaking too much to point with anything smaller. "Behind you."
   288	
   289	At the head of the bar, by a stone the size of a loaf, something grey had begun to walk.

