   270	
   271	*Entered: the not-asking.* She had written that thirty-six days ago at a fence, and had kept to it since, and it had not grown easier with practice.
   272	
   273	Lira had never written anything of the kind.
   274	
   275	She was standing on her stick by the nearest of the three carts, where a man was going over his traces. He was grey and spare, and his left eye was clouded right across, white as milk, so that he turned his whole head to look at whatever he was buckling.
   276	
   277	"Where's your road go?" said Lira. "Past here. East."
   278	
   279	The man turned his head and looked at her with the good eye. Then he looked across the yard, at a small pale person on a tail-board with a map spread out under four stones, and a boy beside her holding a pencil.
   280	
   281	"I don't talk roads to paper," he said.
   282	
   283	He was not angry, and he went back to his buckle.
   284	
   285	She could not even resent it. A road was the whole of what such a man owned. He knew which ford shifted and which hold cheated on a measure, and he was worth his hire exactly so long as other people did not know those things; one did not hand that to a stranger who would write it down and carry it away in a box.
   286	
   287	Lira came back across the yard with her chin up.
   288	
   289	"He won't talk roads to paper," she reported. "I'm not paper. I've never been paper in my life. I can barely spell."
   290	
   291	"You were asking on behalf of it," said Karis. "Sit down. You're in my light."
   292	
   293	She returned to the hide. She had been dictating its features to Cael since breakfast, one at a time, since she could not copy the thing herself and would not have a line of it lost.
   294	
   295	"East of the gate," she said, low. "The double line runs up the valley and over, and I count two days of ticks; then there is a place where it leaves its straight."
   296	
   297	"Where?" said Cael. He leaned to look.
   298	
   299	"There. Don't touch it. It goes out to the left in a curve, and comes back to its own line a little farther on, as a path goes round a pond; but he's drawn no pond. He draws water as three wavy strokes, and a hill with short hatching, and he's put neither inside the bend. There's nothing in it at all but bare skin."
   300	
   301	"He leaves the hide bare wherever a wheel has no business."
   302	
   303	"He does; and everywhere else on this map his roads go straight across bare skin to the next square. It's only here that a road walks round a patch of it." She studied the bend. "I haven't a word for it. Put that it's a quiet place. A quiet place on the hide, with a road bent about it."
   304	
   305	"You mean the quiet ground? Everybody knows the quiet ground. Nobody goes in."
   306	
   307	It was a woman's voice, and it came from her left.
   308	
   309	She was broad, in a wide straw hat, and she was walking past the tail-board with a sack of barley on her hip, and she did not stop. She had spoken as one puts a child right about the name of a bird.
   310	
   311	Karis heard three sentences and no invitation to a fourth.
   312	
   313	The woman went on to the cart with the clouded-eyed man. A small boy of four or five had been sitting on its foot-board, swinging his heels, and she heaved the sack up beside him and climbed after it.
   314	
   315	Karis's mouth had come open.
   316	
   317	Four questions were in it. They had arrived together, whole, each with its grammar. What was it? Where did it begin and stop? What became of a person who went in? And who, precisely, was everybody?
   318	
   319	She looked at the woman's back, settling on the board, and at the clouded eye, which had turned toward the tail-board at the sound of his wife's voice, and stayed there.
   320	
   321	He did not talk roads to paper. His wife had just given paper three sentences, free, across a yard, because paper had not asked. If Karis put a fourth to her now she would no longer be a woman who had dropped a word in passing; she would be a carter's wife under question by a girl with a boy to write it down, and she would shut, as an old man's gate had shut on the north water, and take her husband's road with her.
   322	
   323	And Karis had made the rule herself. *I should like to find out what one learns the other way.*
   324	
   325	She shut her mouth; her bound hands had risen an inch from her lap, both of them, and she put them down.
   326	
   327	The clouded-eyed man clicked his tongue. The cart turned in the yard, with the child waving over the tail at Brom, as children did, and went out by the east gate after the other two, and she heard the bar go across.
   328	
   329	She would not see them again, for they were going two days, or four, to a square with a dot in it, by a road that walked round something.
   330	
   331	It would have been easy to call it a triumph. One got exactly what one was given: she had three sentences, and no means in the world of cross-examining them, and their only witness was on her way east at a mule's pace.
   332	
   333	"Write," she said.
   334	
   335	Her voice shook, which annoyed her.
   336	
   337	"As she said it. All three, with the question mark after the first."
   338	
   339	Cael wrote. She had him read it back, and then a second time, since she could not see the leaf well enough from where she sat, and she corrected nothing.
   340	
   341	"Now underline two words. *Everybody knows.*"
   342	
   343	He ruled a line beneath them with the edge of the notebook's cover.
   344	
   345	"Again."
   346	
   347	"Twice? You told Lira that a heading hadn't earned two."
   348	
