editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:He had been awake a good while, as Cael knew by the gelding, who had been watered and picked out and now stood with a full net in front of him, and by the tailboard itself, which had been swept clean. On the clean oak Brom had set down four small heaps of grain in a row and a fifth by itself, and he waited until all of them were looking before he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:13:Nobody spoke, and the gelding pulled at his net, loudly, as though to make the point for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:21:"I walked a short way last evening, and again before it was light," he said. "I do not make a practice of saying where, but this bears upon the horse. The east road is a cart's width as far as the top of that rise; beyond it there are two ruts cut in scrub, and I could span the pair of them with my arms. The north road is the same once it has passed the last roof." He considered the cart with something like regret. "It was built for a metalled way with a ditch on either side, and it will go back along the road it came by, or it will stand in this meadow; I do not think there is a third thing it can do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:He was in the shed when they came, with the old mule's off hind foot between his knees, and he did not put it down to look at them. The shed was a ring of posts under a cone of thatch, and in the middle stood a drum of oak as thick as a barrel, with the wet rope coiled on it, and from the drum a beam ran out level, fifteen feet, with a padded collar at its end. Round the whole the earth was trodden into a perfect circle, as hard as a threshing floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:87:Lira drew breath, but Brom spoke first, as Cael had known he would, since it was not in him to let a man buy a load without being told what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:91:The ferryman turned his head and looked at his own floor, which lay as level as water all the way round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:95:They put him to the beam, and he stood there with the collar on and his ears back, plainly waiting for somebody to show him the slope. Then the ferryman clicked his tongue, and the gelding leaned, and the drum groaned and began to turn. He went a quarter of the way round and stopped, and looked at the ground in front of him, which was flat; then he went on again, and at the half he stopped once more, and found it flat there as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:105:"That's as near as he'll get. I'd not take it from him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:107:They settled the price on an upturned bucket, Brom and the ferryman, and it took a good while, because Brom had counted the old mule's stops. There had been eleven in a single crossing, the day before, and he knew how many carts had stood waiting on the far bank through each one. He set that sum against the gelding's teeth and went on setting it there, patiently, until the ferryman stopped talking about teeth. Cael did not hear the figure, though he heard what went with it, for that had been his own notion: dry room for three boxes in the loft above the drum, where the ferryman's boy slept, for as long as the horse walked underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:115:The ferryman looked at him as though the question had been in some other tongue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:121:Lira said her goodbye in her own way. She stood at the edge of the trodden ring with her arms folded and told the gelding, as he came round, that he was a great idle brute who had been bought for more than he was worth and sold for more than that, and had now fallen into the one employment in all the world without a hill in it, having done nothing whatever to deserve it. He did not turn his head, for he was looking at the ground in front of him, with his ears forward, and it was flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:129:The smith shrugged, and then sold Lira a long knife, which was the last thing the old purse ever bought. She chose it by its balance, across two fingers, as she would have chosen a lath; and when he told her that it was a skinning blade she said that it was whatever she said it was, and wore it home on her hip, outside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:137:"I shan't read it out," she said. "I've thought about it. If we all have it by heart we shall all be doing sums with it by tomorrow, and I should rather we didn't know how long we could go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:155:"I can give you one reason," said Seln, "and it is the least of them. A crew that spends silver in a place like this has told the place where it came from. We have just sold a horse so as not to say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:157:"The estate's paid me a son's allowance since I was fourteen," said Brom. "Every month, whether I answered the letter or I didn't. I never earned a copper of it, and I never went hungry, and I can't tell you what I'm worth to anybody, because nobody's ever had to pay it. The registry says Copper and a district panel says Iron, and neither of them has ever paid me either." He turned his big hands over and looked at the palms. "There's a man here with one arm who'll write down what I did and what it fetched. I'd like to read that line without knowing there's a bag behind it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:159:Karis nodded once, as she nodded at a citation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:167:So Karis sewed the bag. She had a sailmaker's needle in her kit, for the spines of notebooks, and she closed the canvas with a flat seam and went back over it, and Brom warmed his wax at the fire and laid it along the stitches in a strip as wide as a thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:199:It was the mark that was in the wax, and Cael looked at it with care, as he looked at anything that came twice: a hook, with a bar under it, which told him nothing about the man who used it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:205:"That is the whole of it. I should be obliged if you would look as though you were paying attention."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:207:So they stood, and Seln placed them without being asked: one at each forward corner, and one aft on either side, and the fifth amidships by the goods, where the casks were. He changed the fifth at every crossing, as he changed the sleeper on a watch, and Cael noticed that he never once put himself where the sun would be behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:221:Lira had been looking at the shed as well, at every crossing, and had not said one word about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:225:It was not a log. It was a whole tree, a pale one, with its root-plate still on it like a great clenched fist, and it came round the upstream bend riding low and slow, and turning as it came. The ferryman's boy saw it first and shouted, for on a river, it seemed, a person might shout as he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:229:Three things happened in Cael at once, and he had been waiting for them. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, as breath gathers before a shout, for a push at that range would have turned a tree. Storm leaned, and showed him the air between the scow and the trunk as a thing with a grain in it. And Wind remembered a painted floor in his feet, and offered to put him somewhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:237:It was Iron Skin, which was the whole of Brom's Path, and he had seen it come a hundred times, in a harness room and in a river: the skin ceased to crease, and went the grey of a hearthstone, from the wrist upward. It began now, and it got as far as the cuff of his coat before Brom looked down at his own arm, as a man looks at a dog that has got up on a chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:253:Pike was at the plank when they brought it up. He took the slip in his one hand and read it, and then he read it again, with his lips moving a little, and after that he read it aloud. It was not for them. He turned his shoulder to the meadow and said it out over the nearest tents in a level carrying voice, and a woman frying dough looked up from her pan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:277:"Well, it spends the same," he said. "That's the pity of it." But he went on watching Brom's back for a little while as they walked away down the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:281:It bought flour, and salt, and a piece of bacon no bigger than a hand, and Brom told them that it would have kept the gelding for one day and the half of another. Karis wrote the line into the field archive as she had heard it said, and drew her second rule under two of its words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:297:Nobody knew it, and Cael turned it over once, as he turned any new name, and found nothing under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:299:"Aren't you coming to look?" said Lira. "You read everything. You read the back of a waybill this morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:307:"I'll read it when there's something on it that I did," he said. "One line for standing on a boat isn't worth the walk. I'd like the first thing that sheet tells me to be true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:"I can hear you stop breathing at the end of each one." Brom spoke low, as he had been taught in an orchard. "That's three. It's zero. I've checked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:27:Cael let his breath go, and found that Brom had been right about that as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:Shadow, the quiet one, had lifted at dusk, as it lifted at every dusk, soft behind all the rest, and he had held it where it was while Seln laid a fire ten feet away. Blade was the newest of the nine and the quickest, and it had asked about the edge of the scree, and for once had been told that it was right. Storm did nothing whatever. It was a Gold's worth of weather, the heaviest fragment he owned, and it sat in him as a full pail sits on a step, so that he was aware of it all day in the way that one is aware of a pail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:37:He took Hesk's clock out of its stocking and tilted the face to the embers, and it said a quarter past the hour. It lost, as it always had, and a researcher with her pencil out had ruled two days ago that it was right by definition, being the only clock east of a line. He supposed the watch was over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:39:Lira woke as she did everything. He had put two fingers on her shoulder, and she was sitting up with her hand shut on his wrist before he had taken them away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:45:"You say it as if it were bad news."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:51:Karis woke more slowly. She put out one pale hand before her eyes were open, and it found the canvas and went along it to the knot over the middle box. Then she sat up and asked what the clock said, and wrote the figure down before she had so much as looked at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:69:There was as much of it under the shelf as Brom had said, and it was no kinder than what lay above. The boxes went down a leg at a time, and the cart followed on its locked wheels with a large man leaning back on a rope; and Cael went ahead with the wedge, his back to the slope, and said where the edge was. He said it forty times that morning, and Blade agreed with every one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:73:It had looked like a thread from the ridge. It was a river, forty feet of fast brown water over stones, the colour of weak tea, and so loud after the silence of the hill that Lira had to say everything twice. Trees stood along it, which he had seen from above as a darkness; and they were a darkness still, tall and close, with bark as red as a fox and a smell he could put no name to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:85:Karis looked at the twig. Then she wrote, and Cael, who was near enough, read it over her arm. *Conifer, red-barked, needles flat and paired; unidentified. L. says "red not-pine." Entered as L.'s.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:91:The track was there, on the near bank. It was only two ruts with a ridge of grass between them, as it had been on the upland. But it kept to the river and it ran downstream, and that was more than any of them had been promised. Lira opened her own book on her forearm and wrote as she walked, while Karis kept the printed sheet rolled under her arm and did not once unroll it, since there was nothing on that part of it but a list of what the symbols meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:95:He had done so all the way from Ostrand, and Cael had taken it for tiredness, and been wrong. Brom was reading, slowly, as he read anything, and a little after noon he gave them what he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:117:"It's rained on the dung as well, and that's still here." He stood up. "And there's no ash under the grass, for I've had my hand in. Nobody's lit a fire in that. Not once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:So they laid their own fire in it. Seln's flint was at the bottom of his coat. Karis put out one finger and lit the kindling with a point no bigger than a pin's head, as she had lit a page for Cael under a cart two nights before. The red wood caught fast and burned with a high clear flame; and its smoke stood up out of the valley, straight, in the still air, to twice the height of the trees, where anything with eyes on any of seven ridges might have looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:131:It was a wide place, a hundred feet across and more, where the two streams ran together over shingle, and from the bank it looked no deeper than a boot. A cairn stood on the near side, a neat one, waist high, a good forty yards upstream of where the ruts went in; and there was another on the far bank, as far downstream.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:143:The rider did not hail them. She put the mule's nose at the upstream cairn on their own bank and came across on the slant, a long quartering line, and the water was never over the animal's knee. Halfway she passed within ten yards of the place where Lira had meant to cross, and Cael watched the surface there as she went by: it was smooth, and darker than the rest, and it turned slowly in its own length, like something asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:151:The mule came up out of the shallows beside their cairn and stood dripping. The woman on it was fifty or so, brown and spare, with a lip that had been split a long time ago and had healed a little crooked. Her hat had the brim cut away on the right side. A hatchet hung in a loop at her saddle-bow, edge outward, where anybody could see it, and behind her lay two flat leather bags with long flaps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:She looked them over. She took in the horse's feet, and the iron on the wheels, and the shape of the canvas, and gave each about as long as a clerk gives a line he has seen before. Then she lifted her chin at the cairn beside her and at the one across the water, once each, and turned the mule, and rode it forty yards down the bank before she let it drink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:155:Cael understood the second thing a moment after the first. An animal drinking stirs up the bed, and a ford is a thing people read by its colour; so one watered below the line, and left the crossing clean for whoever came next. Nobody had told her that he needed it explained, and she had not explained it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:163:She put out her hand. He gave them to her, and she did not turn them over to read the outsides; she laid them on her palm, one and then the other, and weighed them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:187:The woman looked at her without unkindness, as one looks at a child who has asked a guest how much he earns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:197:*Fourth day. The letters to Hesk and to Vell went west by a courier on a mule, a copper each, as far as the turf fire. She showed us the ford by riding it. She did not ask what the letters were. She did not ask what we were either. Entered, because I can't remember the last person who didn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:207:Other ruts came into theirs out of side valleys, one after another, like streams joining; and by the fifth day's noon the track was a cart wide and beaten hard, with the grass gone from its middle. The valley had opened. The red trees stood back on the slopes, and there was pale grass in the bottom, shoulder high, that went over in the wind like a cat under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"So has a mule, and a mule goes unshod on this ground. I have been looking at the prints for two days, as the large one has, and I do not think that anybody on this road has ever wished to be heard from a ridge." He considered the gelding without ill will. "I do not say he is wrong. I say he is an announcement."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:235:They went on late, since the light held. Cael walked last, behind the tailboard, where the dust was; he had taken that place because his arm was sound again and he thought it his turn, and because from the back he could see all four of them and the road behind as well. He had not stopped looking at the road behind; he had only stopped expecting anything of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:It was Shadow, the one fragment of the nine that he kept sealed. He knew it, and he was ready, as he had been ready at every dusk since a door log in a wool exchange. It came up soft behind the other eight and offered him what it always offered, which was to make less of him. He told it no. He had told it no so many times that the word had worn smooth, and he was tired, with eleven miles in his legs, and he said it as a man says good night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:Then he shut it, as he would have shut a lid, with his whole hand. The stones spoke again under his heel, loudly and at once, and he had never been so glad of a small ugly sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:255:"You have it now. For four strides you had not, and by the second it was both feet." Seln walked on a little. When he spoke again it was no louder, and the others, at the horse's head, could not have heard. "I should say that I know it. I learned it on a stair, as a boy, by wanting very much not to be heard by the person at the top. It took me a year to do on purpose and three more to stop doing by accident. It always came at dusk, when the eye is worst and the ear must do the work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:259:"No. It is not a thing that waits to be asked, at that hour, by a tired man, and I was a tired man for some years." He turned his head a very little, not so far as Cael. "It was not spent. Nobody was on the road to see it, and I have the best reason of anyone living to know what it sounds like. I only remark that I heard a person stop being there, and I should prefer that you did not grow to like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:265:Seln went forward to the wheel and laid a hand on the tailboard, as though it had needed steadying, which it had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:269:*Fifth day. Deployment since the Line: none. At dusk, on an empty road, Shadow got as far as my boots, seal and all. Four strides, both feet, before I had it shut. I didn't ask for it and I didn't use it for anything, so it isn't a deployment. It's what the thing costs to carry. Call it rent. Entered as rent, and Seln heard it, which is right. He would.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:277:"Don't pass," said Lira. She had sat on a landing wall for a week learning carters, and she said it as a rule. "You don't go by a cart on a road. You fall in behind, and you wait until somebody speaks to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:299:The carters sat on the shafts of their own carts, each with a cloth open on the knee, eating something cold and flat, and none of them had so much as glanced at the wood lying under the trees. Lira came back down the bank with her hands empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:307:The boy came back along the carts while there was still some light. He had been looking at the gelding over his shoulder all day and wanted a nearer look. He grinned at Lira as boys had been grinning at her since Ardenmere, and stood there with his thumbs in his belt. Then he seemed to feel that five strangers and a horse deserved something of him. He took out his right hand and turned it palm up, with the fingers spread, as Karis spread hers before she set a point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:311:It was not a blow. The old man had come up behind him without a sound, and he put the boy's arm down as one puts down the lid of a box, and the boy's ears went red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:329:Cael sat down on the cart's shaft. He did what he had done on the east tier of a yard every week for two years, with a notebook on his knee, and it steadied him as it always had: he set out the claim, and what stood under it, and then he ruled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:343:"All of it. Whatever each of you has. Nobody declares anything on a road, for any reason, until we know what it brings." He kept his voice where the old man had kept his. "Not a Wind burst. Not Iron Skin. Not an Ember point to light a pipe by."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:15:The woman with the grey braid stood in the gap, as she had stood there once with a lantern, and counted them through with her lips: the dark coat, the two abreast, the small pale one, the large one. She shut the gate behind Brom with her shoulder and dropped the bar. Up on the platform the bowman was looking down the valley over their heads, with his bow hanging at the length of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:25:She went over them slowly, and Cael knew what she was reading, for he had read it himself in a jug's worth of lamplight three nights ago: four lines in a book, and nothing gone wrong in any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:67:"Thirty years I've had that walk. Four evenings in five there's one comes up the grass as far as the stone. I've never seen grey on that valley with the sun over it. Not once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:83:There had been three stones still to set, and the last of them had been as much as two men could carry in the water; they had got it to the lip and it had gone over, and it had taken his leg down with it and held him there, against the rock under the edge, with the river at his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:97:"Then he shouted. It wasn't words. He put out all he had, both arms, and he threw one of them clean over the water. And they went to him." The man's voice had gone quite level. "Every one of them. Mine opened its mouth and let me go, and it never so much as looked back. I'd no more Path out than that settle has. I was a log to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:111:She said it as she might have told a child why one did not lean over the well, and she went on ladling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:113:"The ones that find out don't walk up my valley to tell it. The ones that walk up are the ones who did as their fathers did, and never learned which part of it was the part that mattered. So we keep all of it." She set the pot down. "Ask me anything else you like about the ford. Don't ask him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:It took him perhaps a minute: two lines for the banks, and a bar across them for the rock, and on the bar a long shape like a shut eye, lying with the stream; and below the rock, on the near side, he set a row of dots and stopped it five dots short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:133:"They came one at a time; there were never two at the water together, and the next did not show until the one before had gone back into the grass. Each came to the last dry stone and stood on it with all four feet, and put its neck out over the river, and drank from there. One of them misjudged its stone, and a forefoot went in, no deeper than the first joint; it had that foot out again as you would have your hand out of a fire, and it stood for some time shaking it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:143:"It set it down on the dry stone, and it did not drink again. I have seen cats do as much, and one registrar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:155:"The seventh did not come down from any bank. It got up from here, where it had been lying on the gravel since before we arrived, among stones of its own colour, in the full light, with its chin on its feet; and it walked four yards to the far pool and drank, as the others had, dry-footed, and walked back and lay down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:165:"Because you were not what it was waiting for. None of us was, all morning, though we walked and talked and one of us tried forty stones for a doorstep." Seln sat back on his heel. "A thing that could have had any of us by the leg lay and watched us for three hours as it might have watched the weather. I think that is the largest matter I have to report."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:169:"The rest is what I read in the gravel, and I hold it less firmly." Seln drew three short lines coming in from each bank toward the head of the bar. "On the forty-ninth, six came to those two men from the beaches; the marks are deep, and they are all about one place, here, at the top. The seventh's feet are at the tail and nowhere else. It stood there the whole time with its weight forward, and it moved only at the end, a matter of five yards, to meet a man who was coming down the gravel toward it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:185:"I have a hypothesis," she said. "I've had the first half of it since the fan this afternoon, and the second since he shook his hand at me. I'm going to read it as it stands, and then I should like all four of you to try to break it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:203:She read the evidence, and Cael listened to it as he would have listened to his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:205:The country kept one rule for fire, and voices, and Paths, all three together, and kept it only on a road; and that was the rule of people who knew that one of the three would kill them and had never been able to afford to learn which. A grey thing had walked a mile beside six people and done nothing, having been given nothing. It had heard a buckle, and had only followed. It had gone round a hundred feet of wet stones sooner than set a foot on them, and seven of its kind had drunk from a river today as if the river would burn them. A man had lifted with his whole Path for a long while, to the shoulders in that water, and been let alone; he had stood up on dry gravel with it still in his arms, and had not been. And a hound with its teeth in a silent man's hand had opened its mouth and left him for one who shouted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:207:"There's the ferry as well," she said. "I entered it on the fourteenth day and didn't know what I had. A boy shouted on that river as loudly as he pleased, and nobody on the boat so much as turned round. A river isn't a road, and I think the people out here know it in their feet and have never said why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:213:"*Not accounted for: 110 yards. The hour.*" She read the last line more quietly. "*Evidence against: none, which I don't trust.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:225:"There's a part of it that I ought to say first. When one of mine answers, I feel something go out, the same as you do. It isn't a Path, and I can't tell you whether one of those animals would know the difference." He looked at the charcoal river. "I've held all nine fragments for fifty-one days on the chance that it wouldn't, and I'm going on holding them. If the page is right, then what I've got is nine different ways of shouting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:267:"I can do three breaths. What I can't promise is that the Skin'll go down while I'm being bitten. It never has." He said it heavily, as a fact about a load. "Everything I was ever taught says that when teeth come you answer with iron. I won a continental title doing that and nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:279:"A burst moves you. You'd land somewhere that none of us had picked, with every head on that river coming after you, and nobody within thirty feet." He held her eye. "You run. As fast as you've got, wherever you're needed, and you stop on your feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:283:"As you did at the wash."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:287:"Then it's me, and it's Wind." He had thought about that as well. "There'll be a man on that bank with a bow, and he's her witness; and Wind is the one thing I carry that he'd take for a Path, and think no more about. But I'd as soon it didn't come to a third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:317:He wrote the day while Brom trimmed the wick. *Fifty-second day. Thornwater. Seven at the ford, by Seln's count, and one of them lies on the bar. Karis has a hypothesis, in two clauses, and tomorrow at dusk we test both. If I spend anything it will be Wind, once, where it can be seen. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:The boy rode straight down through the tents. A person coming in from a road went round the carts, by the river side, at a walk; this one came between them, and a woman snatched a pan out of his way, and not one voice was raised at him, which Cael noticed. The mule was dark with sweat to the girth and carried her head low. He saw the rider's face as they went by the ash, and he knew it. It was a brown boy of twelve or so, who had been sent for a hammer and a billet of ash on the morning a rung broke, and had come back with both at a run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:Nobody was at it, and the book was not out. The slips hung in their row along the tarred oak, and the boy looked down the whole length of them without reading, as someone looks who has no letters and has been told what a thing is like. He found it by the right-hand post. Cael watched him put up his hand and lay one finger on a piece of real paper, low down, on a thorn twig with a wavy line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:27:There was one other man in the meadow who had not gone back to his fire. The Thornwater carter had loaded yesterday, forty sacks under a net, and he had been buckling a trace when the mule came by him. He stood now with the leather hanging from his fist, and he was looking at the boy's back as a man looks at a door behind which somebody is ill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:31:Cael had never seen him so. The coat with the pinned sleeve was a thing he put on to keep the book, as a smith puts on an apron, and without it the left sleeve of his shirt hung empty from the elbow and stirred a little as he walked. He had the book under the other arm. He did not hurry, and he did not call out to ask the boy's business; he came down to the plank and set the book on it, and bent his bald brown head, since the boy was small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:Pike nodded to him, as one tradesman nods to another. He put the flat stone on the slip's corner and dipped his pen, and held it over the paper while he did his sum aloud, so that the meadow should have it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:71:Pike wrote. He drew one line through the name, level and thin, so that the name could still be read beneath it; and he left the word in capitals standing as it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:75:He turned his shoulder to the grass and raised his voice, and it went as far as the ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:He set out the claim, since it steadied him. Forty marks were offered for a ford that the first name on the wall had asked to keep; and under that he had a road that he had walked four times, and five counts and a distance on a leaf in Karis's coat. And he had the hold, which was fifty days of declaring nothing on a road, and which a carter of twenty-two years had said he would as soon have as anything. It was not much to put under a claim of that size. He was still weighing it when a mild voice spoke at the far end of the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:93:Karis was looking at him as a hungry person looks at a loaf. She had spent a fortnight with one leaf, and a second witness to it had been sleeping six feet from her boxes the whole time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:99:"I'd have had it up before he'd the tack out," she said. "I thought I ought to look as if I'd considered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:117:It was only as the feather went back behind the man's ear that he saw whose hand was on that paper besides theirs. The whole meadow had watched Pike strike a hold and send the bottom of his sheet to do the work of its top. Whatever came up from the ford, or failed to, he would have to stand at this plank and say it, twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:133:The road was the one they knew. It went down into the grey scrub in two ruts, and the cart fitted them as before; the three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas, and Karis walked at the off wheel. They passed the wash in the middle of the morning, dry and red, with its thorn stump on the eastern lip. They kept the hold without thinking of it, and Lira's knife and Brom's bill rode where a stranger could see them. In nearly every particular it was the walk they had made ten days ago for nine marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He had gone to a good many fights. He had gone to them down a lane in Ardenmere, and up the steps of a hall at Norhold, and across sixty feet of sprung oak every week for two years; and in every case somebody else had drawn the card. A keeper had matched him, or a bracket had, or a house that needed its charter, and he had walked out onto whatever floor they pointed to and tried to lose as little as he could. Nobody had drawn this one. A slip had been laid on a plank for anyone at all, and he had put up his hand, and reached for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:141:On a floor he had only ever had his own hurts to price, and he had grown so used to pricing them that he did it now without thinking, as a carter greases an axle. He could not do it for Lira's feet, or for Brom's hands. He watched Karis's small straight back beside the wheel, and he had no figure for it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:171:Lira had come to sit by him, with her arms round her knees. She had read it over his arm, as she always did, and she said nothing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:193:The valley's water was fifty yards wide there, where everywhere else on that road it was twenty, and the reason lay under it. A single ledge of grey rock crossed the bed from bank to bank, ten feet broad and as level as a doorstep, and the river slid over it no deeper than a man's shin, fast and clear, with a skin on it like glass. Two grooves had been worn along the ledge by more wheels than he could guess at, and the rock in them and round them was green.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:195:Above the ledge the river was a long slow glide. Below it there was a row of white stones, each the size of a loaf, set along the rock's downstream edge at intervals of a yard; and beyond the white stones the water was a different colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:199:Cael saw. The white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a dozen of them, and stopped; and for the last five yards before the middle of the river there were none. One lay on the rock by itself, a little out of the line, where somebody had set it down meaning to come back to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:201:In the middle the river had built itself an island. It was a bar of gravel, a long narrow heap of stones that sat on the ledge like a boat run aground upon a weir, with its blunt bow upstream and its stern trailing away below, between the two dark places, to a point. The road came up out of the water onto the bar and crossed it and went down into the water again on the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:205:He took his cart over without another word, and it was the first time Cael had watched him do it as a thing that could go wrong. He led the mule a step at a time. Brom went at the tail-board with both hands on it, and halfway across the near channel the off wheel slid a hand's breadth toward the white stones, quite gently, as a plate slides on a wet table. Brom leaned, and the carter clicked his tongue, and it came back into its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:"I go at my hour," he said. "I've done that for twenty-two years and I'll not stop for forty marks of somebody else's. I'll have your boxes in her loft before the sun's over." He looked at the bar, and then at the five of them, and Cael thought that he had something more to say. But all he did was nod, once, at Brom, as a man nods who has lent a tool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:213:"I timed myself," she said. "From when the canvas went out of sight. I looked at the river again in less than a minute." She took a cord out of her coat, with knots in it. "I believe that I'm improving. Will somebody pace for me? I want it in yards, and mine aren't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:219:The rock was worse than the carter had said. The green on it was a short close weed, and the water had combed it all one way, and a boot set down on it had no more hold than on a fish. Brom went along the lip on the near side with the bill reversed in his hand, feeling ahead of him with the ash haft as a blind man feels with a stick. Where the white stones stopped he reached out past the edge and let the haft go down. It went in to his fist, and his arm after it to the shoulder, and he drew it up again with nothing on the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:231:Cael stood on the bar's head and made his own catalogue, as he would have made it of a floor before a bout. There was the near beach, and the cut bank behind it, as high as a man, with grass along its top. There was the far beach, wider, where the mule had stood. He counted the large stones along the far waterline, the ones too big to shift in a flood, since he wished to know that shore when he saw it again in a worse light: seventeen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:233:Nothing remained of the forty-ninth evening except an iron bar, lying on the gravel where it had been put down, and the one white stone out of its row. At the tail of the bar the stones had been raked up in long scars, as the sand of a ring is after a bout, and something had since gone over them and smoothed them partly down. It might have been the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:241:There were a good many. The rock had cracked in old floods, and slabs of it stood up here and there a hand's breadth clear of the water, some no bigger than a plate and some as broad as a table. He went from one to the next like a man buying a horse. He stood on each, and shifted his weight, and came down off it shaking his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:251:He found it in the near channel, five yards out from the gravel. It was a slab as long as his arm each way, and its top was dry; but the thing that stopped him was its downstream edge. The rock had split there and tilted, long ago, so that a rim stood up along that side to the height of his ankle, like the lip of a dish. He set his left heel against it and looked at them over his shoulder with an expression that Cael had not seen on him since a ferryman's price was agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:265:"It rolls," she said, walking back up. "The spine's firm. You can run on the spine as if it were a road. But the last five yards are round stones lying on round stones, with the water through them, and the edges are worse. If I had to stop there in a hurry I'd want to do it short."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:281:There was nothing to do it against. He lay on his back in scrub that smelled of dust, with the sun climbing to the top of the sky, and the fragments offered him things. Anchor had found the lip of the ledge from the first moment he saw it, and it asked about that edge now, patiently, though the edge was three hundred yards behind him. *There?* He told it that the stones were the hold's, and to leave them. Storm lay in him like a full pail and showed him the air above the river, cool and heavy and sliding downstream, as a lane half made already. Wind wanted the bar. It had watched Lira run that gravel three times and it had her stride by heart, and it leaned in his feet though they were pointing at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:5:It had all been arranged by noon. The steading's woman had stood in her yard with her sleeves rolled and told them what she would give and what she would keep, as she might have laid out the terms for a load of barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:23:Nothing changed that Cael could have pointed to. A man's head was there on the green water, and then it was a thing that the eye did not care to rest on, like one stone among a great many. Behind eight others the ninth of his own fragments turned over, as a dog lifts its head at a voice it knows. It was Shadow, which had come to him out of that same Path, and he put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:35:It took the colour first. The river had been green all afternoon, and brown in the shallows, and gold where the light struck along it; and when the western ridge took the sun, the whole valley went grey in one breath, as it always did. He looked at the water sliding over the rock at his feet, and he knew what the man on the settle had meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:37:And it was dusk. He had known that it would be, and had thought of little else for the whole mile down. Shadow lifted behind the rest, soft, as it had lifted at every dusk for fifty days, with the one offer that he had ever wanted to take; and tonight he must stand still for an hour with it at his shoulder, in a place where being less would have been worth more than forty marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:39:*No*, he told it. He ground his heel in the gravel, and the stones answered him, loudly. He did it again a minute later, to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:59:They did it all together and without any sound. They had been lying along the waterline with their bellies on the wet shingle and their chins on their paws, and they came up as mist comes off a pond. They were just as he had seen one on a rise of ground ten days ago: low, long in the back, with the head carried level.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:79:*Every head but one.* He marked it, and knew that a man with his chin in a pool had marked it as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:85:The two beside it went in after it. They did not go abreast; they fell in behind, one and then the other, in its wake, as ducklings follow. He looked back across his shoulder. On the near beach not one of the three had stirred a foot. Nothing on that side had gone first, and so nothing there had gone at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:89:It was twenty yards of fast water on green rock, and they went over it as cats go over ice, flat-footed, with each paw set down and tried. Their heads never wavered from the yellow seed. Karis was counting under her breath, as she had counted to ninety on a gravel bar with a stone in her lap. He did not hear the number at which she stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:91:The yellow went out. She had ended her declaration between two beats of her heart, as she had said that she could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:95:He understood it then, and it was the first part of her second clause that he had ever seen with his own eyes. They did not know. They had left dry stones with a declaration fixed in their heads, and the river had shut over their feet, and with it, if she was right, whatever sense they had for a Path; and they were walking to the place as a man crosses a dark room to where he last saw the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:99:The first hound came up between them. It set its forefeet on dry stones and shook one of them, twice, and raised its head, and its head began to swing, low, from side to side. It had lost its line. It was as tall at the shoulder as his knee and as long as he was, and it smelled of wet slate. It was a yard from him, and it had not so much as looked at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:103:It turned on him faster than a thing of that size ought to turn. Its teeth shut on his left sleeve and went through the cloth of his coat as if it had been paper, and he felt the wind of them on his wrist. Then its legs went from under it, and it was dead before it had finished coming round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:125:It had been easier on the stretch. Nothing had touched him there. Compression had waited all these weeks for a blow, and each time the teeth met in his coat it rose to take the weight and send it down through his heels. Wind begged for his feet. Pressure had begun to gather under his breastbone, a breath at a time, where anything might feel it gathering. He put them down, and they got up, and he put them down again, while a grey thing tore the hem of his coat to ribbons.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:135:Brom did not hurry. He stood on his stone with three grey shapes in front of him across the water, and he rolled his shoulders once, as he did before he lifted anything. Then he declared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:143:The hound at his knee swung round in the middle of a snap, as a weathercock swings. Twenty yards down the bar the other did the same. On the near beach the three that had stood like posts while their fellows crossed and one of them died all moved their heads by the same inch, together. At the tail, the torn ear came round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:147:His own was gone. It went from him across the head of the bar at a flat run, past Karis's knees as though she were a bush, straight for the grey man on the stone; and he went after it, and he had never felt so slow. Something low passed him on the right, and something else behind that, which was not grey. He had no eyes for either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:He let Iron Skin go as a man lets a coat fall from his shoulders in a doorway, all at once, and stood in what was underneath. The grey went out of his neck and his hands, and out of the arm with the jaws shut on it, between one beat of his heart and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:161:The current took the hound's hindquarters. It swept them round and out over the lip, into the falling water, and the river pulled one way and Brom's heel against the rim of his stone held the other, and the animal was drawn out straight between them like a flag in a wind. Its jaws dragged down his arm as the river hauled on it. He let them drag. He kept his shoulder where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:169:It took as long as it takes to count thirty slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:177:Lira had stopped thinking of it as a dog at about the time it took her sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:183:*No*, she told her feet, and cut at it, and it was a yard off with its head level, looking at her as a clerk looks over a counter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:185:Her whole body knew what to do with this animal. She was Wind. The burst sat in her calves like a held breath, and it had sat there since she was fourteen, and for fifty days she had been taking a stride at a time away from it, on a river bar, with a boy telling her how who had been her partner since a lane in Ardenmere. *You say it at the first stride. Then you say it again at the second.* She said it at the snap. She said it again as the thing sprang back, and again as it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:189:It gave ground. She took the ground. That was correct; she had been taught it before she was taught to read a card. It gave another yard, down the firm spine of the gravel where she had run in her bare feet yesterday, and she followed with the long knife out in front of her and her weight low, and it nipped at her wrist and gave again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:197:The beach was a long way off. The boy was a small dark shape up at the head of the bar with a grey thing dancing round his knees. She had come twenty yards down the gravel, or more, a pace at a time, and all of them in one direction; and there was black water close on her left and close on her right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:217:She saw the grey of it at the edge of her eye, and she felt the discharge; it was as though the weight of him had doubled. He had done it exactly on the count. She had never known him early or late for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:221:The feinter's swung away from her like a needle to the north, and behind her shoulder she felt the big one's turn as well, though she kept her eyes to the front. For a breath neither of them had any more interest in her than in the gravel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:225:It went by her within reach of her hand, flat out, up the spine of the bar toward the grey man on the stone, and it did not so much as bend its line to clear her. She was a post. She was a thing that happened to be standing in its road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:241:It came to her at about the tenth stride, with something very like outrage. She had always supposed that the speed was the burst. The running had been the walk up to it, as a singer clears her throat; no panel had ever given her a rank for the running. She had not known that there was this much of it. The dog took the bend of the ridge straight, and its hind feet went into the soft, and she went round by the firm stones and had a yard back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:247:That came to her in the middle of the run as well, and she had no time to do anything about it. There was no chalk. No slate hung on any wall. A man stood on a bank with a bow who might or might not have been able to make her out at that distance. She was running the best twenty-five yards of her life for no one at all, and she noticed that she minded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:259:She was two strides from the edge with all that speed in her, and she did what she had spent a month on a river bar failing to do, and one morning under a platform doing at last while a man lifted two fingers from a bow. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:It was not unfriendly, for Cael had been ignored by experts, in a house on a bluff, and he knew the feel of it; this was something else, and he came to think of it as being let alone. On the eighth evening the woman with the hides walked back along the road at dusk and laid a flat cake on the shaft of their cart, still warm from being carried inside her coat, and walked away again before anybody could thank her. Lira broke it in five, and Brom said it was barley, and nobody found anything to add.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The country came down as they went. The red trees thinned and gave over to a broad pale tree with leaves that showed silver when the wind turned them, which Karis also declined to name, and the river they were following grew slow and wide and took in others. It was warm in the bottom. The dust stood behind the carts until long after they had passed, and there were flies about the gelding's eyes all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:13:It lay on the far side of a river a hundred yards across, green-brown and unhurried, and he read it from the water upward, as he had been taught to read a floor. There was a landing, with a flat boat at it. Above the landing stood a long low building with a deep eave, and on the rise behind that a taller one with two chimneys; between them a smithy was sending up the first smoke he had seen in three days. Along the river below the buildings ran a meadow, half a mile of it, with a single great ash standing in the middle and carts and tents scattered round the tree like washing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:29:It took them the better part of an hour to work out how. The boat was a scow, square at both ends, with a rail, and it hung on the cable by two running blocks; a second rope went from its bow to a round shed on the far bank, open at the sides, and inside that shed something walked in a circle. When it walked, a drum turned and the rope came in. When it stopped, which it did every few turns, a man's voice came across the water, patient and tired, and after a while it went on again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:37:The carts went first, as was plainly their right, two to a crossing. A boy took a copper for each wheel and a copper for each beast, and he looked at the gelding as the courier had looked at it, and charged for it as a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:47:The old man had gone straight to the ash. He drew his two mules into its shade as a man walks into his own kitchen, and nobody else stood within twenty feet of him, though the shade was the best in the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:It smelled of mules, and tar, and of something frying. A woman went between the tents with a board on her head and flat loaves stacked on the board; a man sat cross-legged in the grass with a harness across his knees, driving an awl through it with the heel of his hand. Three children and a yellow dog were trying to get a goat to go somewhere it had decided against. After nine days of ridges and a river, it seemed to Cael as loud as a city.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:57:There were fires here, and he felt Karis see it beside him, for her pencil came out of the spine of her notebook. There were cook-fires at half the tents, and a woman frying something over one, and smoke lying along the river in a low blue shelf. People were talking, too, in ordinary voices, and somebody laughed aloud. Down by the smithy a man was holding the end of an iron bar across the anvil with his bare forearm, and the arm was grey to the elbow, and nobody round him so much as turned to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:"No," said Cael. "It tells us where the ruling runs. We hold on a road. I'd hold here as well, until somebody's seen what we're like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:65:A boy came for the standing-money. He was the same sort of boy as the one at the ferry, brown and quick, with a tally stick in his belt, and he walked round the cart and counted its wheels aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:71:The boy told him, and told him as well how much a horse of that size would want of them in a day, which he seemed to know from sad experience of other people's horses. Brom's face did not move at all, but he stood on with his hand on the gelding's neck when there was nothing further to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:87:Somebody had brought it up from some river long ago, twelve feet of oak as thick as his wrist, tarred black, and set it breast-high on two posts under the eave of the lower inn, where the weather could not get at it and the whole meadow could. Slips were tacked along it from one end to the other. They were on anything that would take a mark: bark, mostly, peeled thin and written on the pale side, and scraps of hide, and one cedar shingle, and a single piece of real paper that had been somebody's waybill and was written across the back. Each hung by one iron tack, and the oak round them was pitted with the holes of older tacks, hundreds of them, in drifts, so that he could see where the slips had clustered thickest in other years. It was a board that had been used hard for a long time by people who did not waste a nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:89:Lira went along it fast, with her finger out, and her face fell as she went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:125:He read all fourteen from the left post to the right, and then he went back and read them the other way, because he had found what was wrong with them and wished to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:Every name on the board in Ardenmere had carried letters after it, and a number: *Cu 3*, and the like. They had been ratings, borrowed from the registry's tiers and kept in somebody's private ledger, and a stranger could not have stood in that yard for a minute without being told where he stood among them. He had learned to read a card by them before he learned anything else; and here there was only a job, and whoever wanted it done, and what they would pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:129:Not one of the fourteen said who might take it: none of them asked for a tier, or a rank, or a Path of any kind, and none said *Iron or above*, or *a Bronze to lead*. The slip with the seal on it wanted a crew to stand on a boat, and it was all one to that slip whether the crew were five Golds or five carters' boys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:131:"It doesn't price us," he said. "The board, I mean. It prices the work, and it doesn't say one word about who does it." He put his finger under the shingle, without touching. "At the Cinder House you'd have read this in a breath. You'd have known who was above you and who you could reach. There's nothing here to read that way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:149:He looked at Lira and then at Cael. Then he looked at each of them again, in the same order, just as carefully, as though the first look had been only a draft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:It was a western question, and she asked it in the western way, chin up, as one fighter asks another across a yard. Cael saw that he understood exactly what she meant by it. Something moved at the corner of his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:163:He set the book on the plank. There was a hole bored in the oak at that place, the size of a hen's egg, and a horn ink-pot sat down in it to its lip, where no elbow could tip it; beside the hole lay a flat grey river stone as big as a hand. He opened the book and put the stone on the corner of the leaf. Cael understood then what it was for, since a man with one arm cannot hold a page down and write on it as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:165:The others had come up while he spoke, Brom large and quiet, and Karis with her notebook shut on her finger. At the plank's far end, reading the last slip as if he had been there for some time, stood a mild man in a dark coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:183:Nobody answered him, for Lira looked at Cael, and Cael looked at Brom, and Brom looked at the ground, and Karis opened her notebook as though it might be written there. It had never once come up. They had been four enrollees and a clerk; before that they had been a provision, and a ladder fighter, and a docket, and a research budget, and a man who sent in returns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:195:They said them, Lira as she would have given hers to a registrar, and Brom as he would have told the hour, and Karis spelled hers out letter by letter. Then there was a gap, and Cael knew whose it was without turning his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:211:He ruled the leaf with the edge of the stone, drawing the pen down beside it four times, and when he had five columns he wrote a word at the head of each. Cael read them upside down, across the plank, as he had read a deposit book and an enrollment book and most of what he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:219:"You read upside down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:237:She had been turning it over since the plank. She sat with her arms round her knees and her chin on them, and she was frowning at the fire as she frowned at a floor that had been chalked wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:239:"I had it ready. I've had it ready for a year. *Wind. Iron, Rank One.* I've a certificate for it in this coat, with a seal, and I thought he'd want to see that before he'd so much as write my name." She shook her head. "He wrote my name and went to his dinner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:241:"I'd mine ready as well," said Brom. "Copper. For good, and I can say why. And then the man with the book's a Copper himself, and he says it the way I'd say I was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:247:They looked at Cael then, as he had known they would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:251:Nobody laughed, and Brom nodded slowly, as a man nods at a weight he has also lifted, and Karis wrote something short and did not underline it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:263:She made a sound in her throat, and got up, and walked off up the meadow toward the long eave, between the fires, with her hands in her coat. She was gone as long as it takes to walk to a door and stand in it, and when she came back she sat down in the same place and put her feet to the heat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:265:"There's a sheet on the wall by the hearth. I didn't go in. Twelve lines on it, one under another." She put her chin back on her knees. "I couldn't read them from the door, and there was a room full of people who'd have watched me try. I'll read it when I'm on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:Karis had the field archive open, by the light. She had written five words across the head of a clean leaf, in her small exact hand, with a ruled line under them. Now she went back along the row and drew a second line beneath one of the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:5:There was the corner of the trough, and the well's coping, and a post by the long house door where a hired man might reasonably stand to look at the weather. Each lay ten strides from the last, or a little under. The leg gave him eleven, as it had on a gravel bar in the dark; at the twelfth the calf shut like a fist and held him wherever he happened to be, which was generally among geese. So he had learned to arrive somewhere on the tenth, of his own accord, and to make it look like a choice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:He did with it what he did with everything, which was to measure the thing and then live inside the measure. He drew water in elevens and carried in elevens, and he had begun to reckon the whole hold as so many of them from the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:15:They had gone back up into the loft that morning. Karis would not sleep a second night with a floor between herself and three boxes, and nobody had cared to argue with a person who could not so much as shut her hands on the argument. So there was the ladder to be dealt with: twelve rungs, one of them whiter than the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:21:Karis could not close her fingers on a rung. She hooked her forearms behind each one and came down by her elbows, with her two white fists stuck out before her, in a silence so complete that she was plainly composing a letter of complaint and had not yet settled to whom it should be sent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:25:He went down mostly by his arms; and Seln went down as he went everywhere, and nobody saw him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:*Fifty-fourth day. The ladder. Brom, one hand. Karis, elbows. Lira, seated, counting aloud. Mine, by the arms; the calf gives eleven strides on the flat and no rungs at all. Seln, as usual. The nine: idle, and asking. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:49:"I tried. I got up to fetch something." He looked at his right hand as if it had spoken out of turn. "I didn't even know what. And the Skin's not stirred once since the river. There's nothing in this yard for it to answer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:61:He had been hurt often enough. He had broken a forearm against a Gold on a stone floor, and carried a ten-count bill home from a wool exchange, and stood up from the oak with a hand that would not close; and every one of those hurts had gone into a file. On the hill a hurt was a line of writing before it was anything else. Somebody at a table would ask what it showed, and whether he might have avoided it, and what it did to the rate they were keeping for him, and a mild clerk would copy the answer out fair. He had lain in an infirmary bed once and listened to two people in the corridor deciding what his ribs meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:69:The hold had changed toward them, though no one made a speech of it, Thornwater making none about anything; he read it instead in small things. There were five bowls on the long board at supper, set down among the others, where on their first stay the crew had eaten at the end. A child of four came across the yard on the second morning, with great care, carrying an egg in both hands, and gave it to Lira, and ran. And the old man in the chimney corner, who had not woken for anything in Cael's hearing, opened one eye when Brom came in under the lintel and moved his feet to make room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:81:She had been set on an upturned trough by the barn wall, with her foot up on a sack. The woman had put her there on the first morning as she might have put down a bucket, and Lira had endured it for an hour. Then she had asked for a whetstone, and after the whetstone, for every blade in the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael had not known that he ever did. He walked across the yard in his slow dry way with a length of ash in his hand, peeled, as tall as his shoulder, with a fork left at the top of it for a palm to sit in, and he stood it against the trough where Lira's hand would find it. He kept his eyes off her foot; he lifted two fingers from his side, an inch, and let them fall, and went back up his ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:95:He stopped in the barn door, as the bowman had, with his right arm in its thick linen held across his body and his bad leg dragging a little. He had not spoken to any of them since the settle, and he stood looking for some time at Brom, who was sitting on a keg with a sewn arm in a sling of the woman's making.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:111:She had lain awake over it, as he knew because she told him so, at the ladder's head, with the grey light coming in under the thatch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:113:"I can't write, and I can't turn a leaf. I've three boxes over my head that I can't open, and a finding in a book I've never read. I shall go out of my mind by noon." She held up her two fists. "I want a table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:117:It made three shelves in a row, as tall as her shoulders when she sat, with no wall behind them and a barn all round; and a hen walked along the top of the middle one and was removed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:They divided her between them. Cael turned leaves and fetched what she named, for he could stand eleven strides at a time and the boxes were within three. Brom, who was allowed to hold nothing, read out the tags on the bundles in his slow voice, and was corrected. Lira was given the pencil, which was a mistake, and everybody knew it by the second line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:153:In the middle of the morning Cael read her the finding. He stood at the end of the trestle with Hesk's book open at the one page, and read it from *Finding* to *five dead animals*, slowly enough for a pencil to follow; and Lira took it down into the field archive in her large angry hand. It was the first time a leaf of that book had been read aloud for copying. He turned no other page, and Karis sat with her face toward the barn door until he had shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:165:"No. I'll put it properly. That's everything I know about the wrong country." Her chin went along the shelves, left to right. "Every leaf I've copied since I could hold a pen, and every one of them is about the place we walked out of: tiers, and ranks, and what an Arbiter station does with a person. I've read forty accounts of how a Path is assessed, and there isn't a page in three boxes that has the word *stillhound* on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:191:The carter was back on the fifty-seventh day, an hour after noon, as he had been before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:197:"From the one-armed man, same as the last. You're to read it, and if it's wrong you say so when you're down." He held it out, and then looked at the hands in front of him, and gave it to Cael. "He's said it once already, before I'd my hat on, and he'd the whole meadow standing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:199:Cael was at the well, on the last stride of an eleven, and he read it there, aloud, since one of them could not have held it and one had only a single hand to spare.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:213:"I know we did." She shifted her grip on the ash. "I've had a rank read out before, and a result. I never had one that gave my hurts and left out the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:215:Karis had come up at Cael's shoulder to see the bark; she could not take it, and so she read it where it lay in his hand, with her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:217:"*Cleared*, and a colon after it," she said. "He's never used one before, and everything after the colon is what *cleared* cost. And *five confirmed*: he won't write seven, and he won't write that two went down the river, because nobody counted those with a bow; he writes what somebody stood over." She looked up. "The last noun has changed again. *Nothing lost* was casks, and *no losses* was a cart and a mule. *None lost* is us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:221:It was the question the carter had been told to bring an answer to. Cael went through the line again, a word at a time, as he had gone through the other one in this same yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:237:He had had a great many written about him. A panel had given him one for a bout, and it had been arranged beforehand; a house had kept a rate for him that was scattered on purpose, so that it should never settle into a line that anybody could read. The registry had never managed a tier or a rank for him, and had settled on a word. There had been a question mark on a board in Ardenmere, and a level on an advisory that he had never been shown. Some of those figures had flattered him and some had been meant to sink him, and he could not think of one that had simply been counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:261:The woman had unwound them yesterday and wound them again more thinly. There were two lines across each palm, the upper where she had first taken hold and the lower where she had taken hold again; they would be pink in a month, the woman said, if she left them shut. Karis had inquired how one left a hand shut, and had not been given an answer. She had not set an Ember point since the ford, and she doubted that with these hands she could have said where one would go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:263:So on the fifty-eighth morning she sat on the tail-board of a cart in Thornwater's yard with the calfskin unrolled beside her. She could not hold its corners down, and so Brom had brought four stones from the well's foot, and set one on each, and stepped back with his arm in its sling, looking as pleased as if he had been allowed to carry something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:275:She was standing on her stick by the nearest of the three carts, where a man was going over his traces. He was grey and spare, and his left eye was clouded right across, white as milk, so that he turned his whole head to look at whatever he was buckling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:285:She could not even resent it. A road was the whole of what such a man owned. He knew which ford shifted and which hold cheated on a measure, and he was worth his hire exactly so long as other people did not know those things; one did not hand that to a stranger who would write it down and carry it away in a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:299:"There. Don't touch it. It goes out to the left in a curve, and comes back to its own line a little farther on, as a path goes round a pond; but he's drawn no pond. He draws water as three wavy strokes, and a hill with short hatching, and he's put neither inside the bend. There's nothing in it at all but bare skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:309:She was broad, in a wide straw hat, and she was walking past the tail-board with a sack of barley on her hip, and she did not stop. She had spoken as one puts a child right about the name of a bird.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:321:He did not talk roads to paper. His wife had just given paper three sentences, free, across a yard, because paper had not asked. If Karis put a fourth to her now she would no longer be a woman who had dropped a word in passing; she would be a carter's wife under question by a girl with a boy to write it down, and she would shut, as an old man's gate had shut on the north water, and take her husband's road with her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:327:The clouded-eyed man clicked his tongue. The cart turned in the yard, with the child waving over the tail at Brom, as children did, and went out by the east gate after the other two, and she heard the bar go across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:337:"As she said it. All three, with the question mark after the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:339:Cael wrote. She had him read it back, and then a second time, since she could not see the leaf well enough from where she sat, and she corrected nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:The first was the pair from Brom's stone. They had left a large silent man kneeling in a river and come up out of the channel onto the gravel, and they passed within ten feet of Cael, and of Lira sitting in the water with a knife; and neither of them turned its head. They went by as carters go by a milestone, and trotted away down the spine of the bar, shoulder to shoulder, toward the tail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:13:The second thing was his own hound, the one that had torn his coat. It had lost Brom at the water's edge when his declaration stopped, and he had forgotten it while he watched a man hold a river. It had come to a conclusion as well, and a worse one, for it was going back to the first declaration it had felt on that gravel, and to the person who had made it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:17:She stood beside her boulder, at the upstream nose of the bar, with the little knife in front of her. The hound that had torn his coat had turned its back on the channel and was crossing the head of the gravel toward her. Her Ember point had been out for a long time, and she was as dark as any of them; that made no difference to the animal, which remembered. It did not rush her. It went as a man goes up to a door at which he has knocked before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:23:The hound struck at her right wrist. The knife turned in her fingers and went spinning onto the stones, and she went over backward after it with her head toward the water, and caught it up as she fell. He saw her hand shut on it, and saw that she had it by the wrong end, with the steel in her fist and the horn handle sticking out below; and the animal was already coming again, and she had no time to turn it round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:41:It was over before he reached her. The hound had done the work itself; it had driven forward, as they drove at everything they could feel or remember, and the knife was only the length of a hand, and a hand had been enough. It lay across her with its head on her shoulder, and she lay under it with her elbows locked and her eyes wide open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:57:He knelt. Her hands were shut round the blade of a pencil knife as a child's shut round a rail, and there was blood between the fingers, running down both wrists into her sleeves; and he had to open them for her, one finger at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:73:The pair from the near beach were halfway to the tail, side by side, turned to face the head, and the torn ear was where it had been all evening. They did not come on, or cast about. They stood with their heads level, as a grey thing had once stood beside a white stone while a gate was barred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:They could go. The near channel was open behind them, twenty yards of green rock under fast water, with a hole beside it. One of them could not stand, and must be carried over that; the man to carry her had one arm; and a third could not close her hands on a sleeve. They would do it in the dark, with their backs to three animals that had just shown how well they could learn, and somewhere on that rock somebody would slip. He knew what Iron Skin did when a load shifted and there was no time to choose, and he had watched a Wind burst go off by itself on a river bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:87:Four hurt people could not keep the hold through a night as five whole ones had kept it for a mile. And when the first of them broke, three would be standing ready for it, as seven had stood ready for a man shaking a lift out of his arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:97:It had been a different animal each time: the middle one, and then the left. He had been looking for a chief among them all evening, as he would have looked for the captain of a crew, and there was none. There was only whichever went first. The rest followed that, and when it was gone they had nothing to follow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:117:"We said in the loft that it would be. Wind, once."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:137:"Go past it," said Karis. She was sitting where he had left her, with her hands turned up in her lap. "Downstream, as far from here as the gravel goes. If you land short of it, it will come up the bar to you and bring the other two back with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:139:"I'd got as far as that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:151:The two in the middle let him come. He had known that they would, and it was a different matter to do it. He passed between them close enough to have touched either, and their heads did not turn; one of them let out a breath as he went by, and he felt it warm on the back of his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:157:It was older than the rest, as he could see now. The muzzle was nearly white, and the torn ear had healed long ago into two stiff flaps, and there were pale seams in the hide of its shoulder. It looked past him, up the bar, as it had looked past Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:159:He had not let Wind have a stride since a ring of churned turf beside a split thorn. He set his feet as he had watched Lira set hers ten thousand times; and for the first time in fifty-three days he did not tell it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:163:He heard the gravel go up behind him in a fan, and he had left it. He was past the white muzzle, an arm's length from its flank, going by faster than it could turn its head; there was black water under him, and then there was the drowned tail of the bar, where the gravel ran on a hand's depth below the surface, and his boots came down on it left foot first, with the left side toward the animal, as she had told him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:197:The other followed, as he had known that it must. They were poor swimmers, and the current off the lip had them at once and swung them out into the middle of the stream; and for a little he could see two long grey heads, low on the black water, going away down the river faster than a man could have run on the bank, until the bend took them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:221:They took stock at the head of the bar, in what light there was, and Cael did it as he would have catalogued a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:223:Brom held his left arm out from his body and looked at it as he might have looked at a split shaft on another man's cart. It was laid open from the wrist to the middle of the forearm, in two long furrows where the teeth had dragged, and at the bottom of the deeper one something showed white.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:241:Karis sat with her two hands in her lap, turned up. There were two cuts across each palm, one below the other, as straight as ruled lines, and she could neither lay the hands open nor shut them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:255:He came slowly, flat-footed on the green rock, with a bow held up out of the wet. He had been told not to come down, and had told them so himself. He stopped at Brom's stone and stood looking into the deep water under the lip. Then he came up onto the gravel and went about it with the tip of his bow, touching each one as a woman touches loaves on a board: the first, at the far landing; Lira's, half in the river; the one that lay beside the boulder. He walked the thirty yards down to the tail by himself, and walked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:265:"I've stood over that valley thirty years," he said, in his dry unused voice. "I know what it sounds like when they take a cart. You can hear it from the fence, with the wind right." He turned his eyes on Lira. "I stood on that bank through the whole of this, and none of you shouted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:277:He put no question to them. He let down the tail-board, and then he walked out along the rock by himself, in the last of the light, as far as a white stone that lay a little out of its row; and he picked it up in both arms and carried it to the lip, and set it down at the end of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:287:She had water hot and linen torn before the gate was barred behind them, and she did not waste a word on what she saw come in under the lintel. She took the arm first. She washed it with salt water and looked into it with the lantern held close, and then she threaded a needle. Brom sat on an upturned bucket and watched every stitch go through his own skin, with his lips moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:315:"A week before you hold a spoon. Longer for that." She nodded at the notebook's corner, which showed at the breast of Karis's coat, and Karis looked as though she had been sentenced by a court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:321:She came to Seln last. He sat on a sack in Brom's coat, with his wet hair on end, and she looked him over as one looks at a ewe that ought to be lame.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:331:Then she stood back, with the basin on her hip, and counted them. She did it as she had counted them through her gate, with her lips, a head at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:341:"Five dead," said the bowman, from the door. "I counted them with the bow. Two took to the water and went down with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:345:Cael had it inside his shirt, where it had kept nearly dry. She spread it on the barrel-head and read the square black hand, and the name with a line through it, and the five strokes beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:349:"He can read why. It's all there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:363:Brom was asleep; he had lain down with his sewn arm across his chest and gone out like a candle, as he did everywhere. Seln slept sitting up, inside the coat, and Lira lay on her back with her right foot outside the blanket on its piece of stave. Cael had got as far as one line about what the animals hunted by, and there he stopped with the pencil lifted, since the sentence was not his to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:371:She had never read a leaf of that book; whatever he showed her of the fragments she saw, and the book she left alone. She turned her face a little toward the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:373:"Read me what you have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:385:*One: the stillhound hunts Path discharge. Seven turned to one Ember point the size of a seed; six turned to Iron Skin held for three breaths; three ran at a single Wind burst. Between those, they went past silent people as they would go past posts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:399:"There is still none. There are five dead animals, and I trust it rather more than I did." Her voice was going. "Put that. Then read me the whole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:401:He read it to her from the heading, and she was asleep before he reached the fourth sentence. He finished it anyway, quietly, since she had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:409:*I spent one. Wind, a single burst, down the bar and past the last of them, where the man on the bank could see it. I priced the landing before I went, and the price was right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:417:He read it over, and wrote the last line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:11:He was a short, thick man, burnt as brown as a nut, and his cart was loaded to the rails with sacks. He chocked his wheels, saw to the mule, and sat down in the ring with a yard of empty ground on either side of him. Cael had seen him once already that day, at noon, handing a slip across the plank; and he had watched the meadow read that slip all afternoon, crew after crew, and go back to its dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:15:"Nine," Lira had said, at the plank. "For walking. That's as much as we've made since we got here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:33:She had asked it clearly, across the fire, as she would have asked a coach what a drill was for. Cael felt Karis go still beside him, with her pencil half out of its spine. Several of the people in the ring looked at the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:45:"I'd as soon have that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:73:He had made them a sign, since five names would not go on a scrap of bark: five short strokes side by side, like a tally with its bar left off. He drew it under the carter's own mark and read the whole aloud to an empty meadow, and then he handed it across the plank and looked at each of them twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:81:They went out by the east road, over the saddle. Beyond the top the country changed, as Seln had said it would: the road shrank to two ruts in a grey scrub that stood as high as a man's belt, and the mule cart, which had looked a toy in the meadow, fitted those ruts as a foot fits a boot. The three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas. Karis walked beside the off wheel, where she could have put her hand on them, and never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:85:Lira looked at the stump as she went by, and smiled with one side of her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:93:"They do, with me." Brom stood quite still under it. "I've never known why. I think it's that I'm the same height as a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:103:The pin had sheared. That was the least of it, for the wheel had dropped onto a stone as it went, and one of the curved timbers of its rim had split along the grain for half its length.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:121:Cael knew the place before anybody named it. The scrub ended, as though it had been cut with a knife, and the valley drew in between two long shoulders of hill, and from one shoulder to the other the whole floor was grass. It was the pale grass of the lower river, chest high, and with the light coming level along it every blade stood up gold on one side and grey on the other. The ruts went into it and were lost within thirty yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:147:"What he has paid for. We walk." Seln looked at the carter's fist on the strap. "Everything you carry that has an edge, carry it where it shows. Nothing else. I mean that as exactly as I have ever meant anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:151:For the first quarter of a mile there was nothing but the noise, and Cael had not known until then how much noise a quiet valley makes. There were frogs. The bottom was wet, somewhere under the grass, and they were in it by the hundred, a creaking that rose and fell like something breathing. There were crickets, nearer, in a dry steady saw, and some small bird that ticked. Over all of it the wind went through a mile of standing grass with a sound like a hand drawn slowly down a coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:He walked in the left rut with his knife on his belt and looked outward, as he had been told. The sun touched the ridge behind him. His shadow went out in front, forty feet long, and Lira's beside it, and then quite suddenly there were no shadows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:A little way past that, on the left, something stood up out of the grass that was not grass. It was a cart-shaft. There was only the one, grey and split, pointing at the sky at the angle of a man's raised arm, and whatever it had once been fastened to lay down in the stems where nobody could see it. The carter did not turn his head as they went by. Karis did, and then looked very steadily at the mule's ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:165:It took him a dozen strides to hear it, because it was a thing that was not there. Out on the right, forty or fifty yards into the grass, the frogs had stopped. They had not stopped everywhere. They had stopped in one place, a patch no wider than a room, and all round the edge of that place they creaked as before. It was a hole in the noise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:He had not turned his head. He was thirty paces in front, a dark coat walking the verge, and his voice came back along the ruts as small and plain as a pebble skimmed on water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:He meant a Path. He meant Lira's burst, and Brom's Iron Skin, and any point that Karis might set; and Cael knew, with a coldness in his stomach, that he meant nine fragments as well, which were nobody's Path at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:183:It was grey. That was the first thing, and for some strides it was the only one, for the grey was the grey of the grass in shadow and the eye kept sliding off. Then it moved against the sky, and he had the shape of it. It was low, and long in the back, and about as high at the shoulder as a calf. It carried its head level and stretched out, like a dog on a scent. It went at a walk, and not one stem of grass moved round its legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:189:She barely breathed it. She was in the right-hand rut, a yard from him, looking straight in front of her, and every line of her was strung as it had been on the gravel three strides short of a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:197:Ten strides later a stone turned under her heel. It was nothing, a loose cobble in the rut, such as any of them had trodden on forty times that day; but her whole weight went sideways, and in the instant before she had her balance he felt the air beside him tighten, as it tightens in a yard before somebody goes. Her hand shot out and shut on his sleeve. She did not go. She hung there for one stride with her fingers twisted in his coat and her teeth showing, and then she had her foot under her, and let go of him, and smoothed the cloth down where she had gripped it as though that had been all she meant to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might, for all she knew, be a declaration too. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:207:Cael had seen him fill a doorway and had seen him fill a district's frame. He had seen him make a harness room feel small by standing up in it. He had never seen this. Brom was walking with the bill held low across his thighs, and his arms were brown to the wrist, and somehow he had drawn in everything about himself that was more than bone and muscle and a coat. He was exactly as large as his body and no larger. It was still a good deal of body, and it was all there was, and it walked at the pace of a mule and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:Wind had his feet first. They had leaned for a month, a dozen times a day, and they leaned now toward the rise as if the ground sloped that way. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, slowly, a breath at a time. The Iron read wanted to open and tell him the mass of everything within a pace and a half, which was nothing but a mule; it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. Ember offered a point in the dry grass at the foot of the swell, and behind it another and another, so that he could see how the whole slope would take fire along a line. Anchor had found the wired rim and asked about it. Blade wanted Brom's bill. Storm turned over, once, like a great fish under a boat, and showed him the air between himself and the grey thing as a lane already laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:215:*No*, he told it, and Wind, and Pressure. *No. Rim; his; leave it. No.* He said it to each of them at one stride and to each of them again at the next. They were small refusals, one after another, quicker than he could have put names to them, and none of them was any harder than lowering a hand; it was only that there were nine hands, and that they rose again as often as he put them down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:The grey head swung. It went slowly to one side and then to the other, low, casting, as a dog casts that has lost a line. Brom let go. The buckle clicked, and the head came round and settled on it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:239:A gully came down out of the right-hand hill, and at its foot the floods of other years had spread a fan of pale stones clean across the valley, a hundred feet wide, with a skin of water shining on it. The ruts ran straight over. The mule's feet rattled in the wet, and the cart came after with a noise like a sack of nails. Out on the right the grey shape came to the edge of the fan and stopped as if it had met a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:251:The fence stood up out of the grass all at once, as the carter had said it would: split posts, as high as a man's head, from one shoulder of the valley to the other, with a gate in the middle and a lantern coming toward the gate from inside. He heard a bar drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:255:It had not sat, or turned. It stood in the thin grass with its head level, beside a white stone, and it came no farther. Cael fixed the stone. Then he lengthened his stride to the yard, as he had learned to do on sixty feet of oak, and counted from abreast of it to the gate, as Karis would have counted, with his lips shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:259:Nobody had struck a blow, or so much as drawn a knife. Behind him he heard Brom let out a breath that he seemed to have been keeping since the mouth of the valley. The gate swung inward on a leather hinge, and the lantern came up, and behind it he saw a woman's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:5:Cael noticed that before he noticed her. She was a broad grey-braided woman of fifty or sixty with her sleeves rolled to the elbow. She stood in the gap of her gate and counted them through it as a farmer counts sheep at a hurdle: the dark coat first, then the two of them abreast, and the mule, and the cart, and last a very large young man with a hedging bill. She moved her lips for each. When the tailboard was inside she put her shoulder to the gate and shut it, and dropped a bar as thick as his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:There was a platform beside the gate, on the inner face of the fence, six feet up, with a ladder to it. A man stood there. He had a bow in his left hand and an arrow on the string, and Cael had seen enough people threaten him to know that this one was not doing it. The bow hung at the length of his arm. The arrow pointed at the planks between his boots. He was looking out over the top of the posts, down the valley, at something a hundred and ten yards off beside a white stone, and he did not once look round at the people who had just come in under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:17:He did not mean their mouths, or not only. Cael had seen him look at Brom's forearms as the tailboard came through the gate, and at Lira's feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:21:She had her back against a post of the fence, and her hands had begun to shake now that they were allowed to. She had turned to the woman with the lantern as she would have turned to a referee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:33:Cael looked at Karis. She had the notebook out at last; it had come out of her coat the moment the bar dropped, as though it had been on a spring. She wrote one word at the head of a clean leaf, and looked at it, and drew a single line beneath it. Then she looked up at the woman with the lantern, and he saw the second question arrive in her face, whole, with its grammar. It was the question of what a thing like that could be waiting for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:35:She shut her mouth on it. He was close enough to see the effort.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:It was the third time he had been asked, in three different ways, and the first time the answer seemed to be put away somewhere for later. She took the slip from the carter's hat, where he had left it on the shaft. She read Pike's hand, slowly, and the five strokes under it, and then she fished a stub of charred stick out of her apron and made her mark on the bark, pressing hard. It was a thorn twig, three spines to a side, with a wavy line under it for water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:The bowman had not moved. But as Cael watched he eased the string, and took the arrow off it, and slid it back into a case at his hip. Whatever he had been looking at, by a white stone, was not there any longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:69:She ran it as Bracken had run a counter, which Cael saw within an hour, and it warmed him to her more than any welcome could have done. She had a tally of the sacks on a board by the barn door, in strokes, and when Brom took the first one up the ladder she drew her charcoal through a stroke without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:71:There were forty-one sacks, and each weighed about as much as Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:Brom's left arm took it. The knuckles went white and the shoulder stood out through his shirt, and the ladder bowed like a drawn bow and came back; and when he had found the rung with his foot he went on up, more slowly, and set the sack down in the loft before he looked at his own hands. They were brown, both of them. Iron Skin had not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:The bowman had been watching her come. He was a lean, weathered man of no particular age, who seemed to live on that platform, and Cael had not yet heard him use his voice. He looked down at Lira standing under him with her hands on her knees. Then he lifted two fingers from the stave of his bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:109:She had made a table of it in her exact hand. At the head stood the word, underlined once, and under the word his five counts in the order he had given them: *keeps station at forty-five yards; makes no sound; follows the buckle with its head; will not cross the gravel fan; answers nothing, having been given nothing.* Beneath those stood the figure, *110 yards*, and after it, in smaller writing, *gate to stone; C.'s count, paced at a yard to the stride; not taped.* She had gone out in the heat of noon and walked the same hundred and ten yards along the inside of the fence, in her own shorter steps, so as to know in her legs how far it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:111:"It stops short," she said. She was sitting on the top box with the leaf on her knee. "Short of what? I can think of four answers, and I've no way of choosing among them. It may stop short of the fence, or of the people behind the fence. It may be that something lies under the ground at that distance and nowhere else. Or a hundred and ten is simply as near as it ever comes to anything, and the stone is an accident."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:127:There were four prints, set square, where a thing had stood. Each was as broad as his palm and deeper at the toe than at the heel, and that depth told him how long. A creature that stands a moment leaves a mark like a seal in wax, and one that stands until its weight has settled forward leaves a mark like these; he judged that it had been there from the time a gate opened until the time an arrow went back into its case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:129:It had not circled or lain down, and he could find no place where it had shifted its feet. When it went, it had turned in its own length, and gone back down the valley on the line it had come by, at a walk; he followed that line for two hundred yards with the grass over his head, and the prints lay as evenly as stitches. He cut across to the ruts at three places and paced the gap. It was forty-five yards at each, as nearly as his legs could tell him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:133:He knew what he was looking at, and it was not anything that he knew about animals, of which he knew very little. It was his own trade. A man set to follow another is given his distance. He is told to keep it, and neither close nor fall back; and it is chosen so that the ordinary noise of the person in front will cover him, and so that one particular thing, if it should happen, will reach him across the gap. He does not act until it does, and he may walk behind a subject for a year and never be given leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:139:The form had made itself without his leave, as it did. He read the last sentence over. He had written *nothing further to report* at the foot of seven hundred returns, and he had never before written that he did not know a thing. It had always been possible to leave such matters out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:He considered, walking back up the ruts to the gate, whether any part of it bore upon the day in hand. That was the rule he had made for himself, and he applied it with care, since he distrusted rules that he found convenient. They were inside a fence. They would go down this valley again behind a cart, at noon, with the sun over them. On that day there was nothing in his eight roads that five people needed in order to put one foot before the other, and so he would keep it, as he had kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:161:He had gone out at noon on the first full day of their stay, with the witnessed slip in his hat and nothing in the cart, sitting on the tailboard as he had not once sat while the grain was on it. An empty cart behind a rested mule, it seemed, could do in one day what a loaded one did in two. He had brought salt up with him, and a keg of nails, and a bolt of blue cloth that three women came out of the house to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:163:He had also brought a scrap of bark. He took it out of his hat and held it out to Cael between two fingers, as though he were not sure that he ought to be touching it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:165:"From the one-armed man. He's wrote it in the book, and that's it copied. He says you're to read it, and if it's wrong you can tell him so when you're down, and he'll say it over for the meadow after." The carter rubbed his neck. "He never sends silver up a road. It's in his box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:167:It was in the square black hand. Cael read it standing in the yard, with the other four at his shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:177:Lira read it with her lips moving, and then looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:181:Cael went through it once more, a word at a time, as he would have gone through an instrument. There had been one cart. They had walked ahead of it. Everything that had gone in at the mouth of that valley had come to a fence. He could not find a word in it that he would have changed, and what struck him harder was that he could not find one that said anything about him at all, beyond what he had done on a particular evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:187:The carter had been looking at his off wheel while they read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:189:It had held, all the way down the valley and all the way back, and it was an ugly piece of work: a thorn peg for a pin, and a rim wound round with wire like a mended pot. Brom had been looking at it as well. He had looked at it every time he crossed the yard for three days, with the expression of a man who has left a knot tied wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:203:It had been drawn in oak-gall ink and gone over, here and there, with a burnt stick. There were roads on it, each a double line, as a wheel sees a road. Three wavy strokes stood for a ford and a square with a dot in it for a hold. A ring with a bar through it lay on a river in the west, and Cael knew the ferry. Between one square and the next ran rows of small ticks, and he had counted the ticks from the ring to a square at the head of a valley before he understood that they were days. Roads went off the hide's edge to the north, and the south, and the east. Wherever a wheel had no business, he had put down nothing at all, and the skin was bare.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:227:The carter had said it as he might have said that the hay was in. *That's the season open.* He had meant the roads, and the weather on them, and a board in a meadow with slips enough on it for every crew that could walk; and he could not have known what the word had been to the person he said it to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:*This one belongs to a one-armed man's book, and the weather. It has four lines in it. I can read all four.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:239:He read the last line over, and shut the pencil in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:Down in the yard Brom was spinning a wheel again, for the pleasure of hearing nothing, with a row of geese drawn up behind him like a jury. Lira sat on the well's coping, binding the hilt of her knife. On the platform by the gate the bowman stood as he always stood, with his bow at the length of his arm, looking down the valley at a white stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:9:There were twice the tents, for the dry roads had done it, as the carter had said they would: crews had come in from every quarter while the five were away, and the grass along the river was trodden brown, and three fires burned where there had been one. A knot of people stood at the plank's near end, under the long eave, as people stand at a counter when a clerk is giving out results.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:11:Pike had the book open and the stone on it. He looked up as they came along the eave, and looked again, and put his finger on a leaf without having to turn to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:13:"You've read it," he said. "He gave it you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:21:Pike nodded, and turned his shoulder to the meadow, and raised his level, carrying voice over the heads of the people at the plank, so that it went as far as the ash. The Thornwater line went out exactly as it had come to them on bark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:23:It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Pike did not wait for her to finish. Another slip lay ready under the edge of his stone, where somebody had left it for him earlier in the day. He drew it out with two fingers, and read it to himself, and read it again, and he was giving it to the meadow before the last of their silver was off the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:59:"Half this meadow can't read. The book's for me. The saying's for them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:63:Cael had been written in a good many books. There was one in a district office with a single word against his name, and one that a mild man had filled for two years, and one on a shelf somewhere with slips in its pocket that no officer would own; and the thing they had all had in common was that they were shut. People had read them about him in rooms he was not let into, and he had stood on a carved number for fourteen sittings and never once been shown the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:71:He had brought fleece down from the high fold. Cael remembered the slip, for it had hung on the plank the day they came: eleven bales, at a mark the bale, delivered dry. The big man laid it down with a slap. Somebody at the landing had put a row of strokes on it for a witness, eleven of them, and through the last a short bar had been drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:Pike read it twice, and then he gave it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:81:"It rained on us the last mile. You can't call that the carrying." He had a good loud comfortable voice, and he looked round at the people by the plank as he used it, inviting them in. "Eleven bales went up on my mules and eleven came down to the landing. Eleven bales, eleven marks. That's the slip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:Cael did not understand it, and then he did, and the hair stood on his arms. It was no threat, for Pike would write it, exactly, as he wrote everything. He would put down that a captain said eleven dry, and beside it that a witness at the landing had marked ten; and then he would stand up and say both to two hundred people, and say them again when the ink was on. It would be in the book for as long as there was a book, and every hirer who ever asked about that crew would be told that its captain had once stood at this plank and argued with a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:91:The big man had got there as well. His eyes went over the faces, and the fires, and came back to the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:105:The plank was nearly full. The dry weather had brought slips in from everywhere, and there was hardly a hand's breadth of bare oak between the posts; he read along it by habit, the bark and the hide and the pictures drawn for people who had no letters. At the right-hand post his eye stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:"He's not gone yet. He goes out at first light, up the cold road, and that's a week's work or near it. Then the ford's his." Pike considered the slip, and something crossed his face that Cael could not read. "It'll keep a week. Carters know the hour for that road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:149:"I know what his paper said the year he walked in. Copper, the same as mine." Pike dipped. "That's a good while back. There's no station nearer than nine days west, and he's never gone to one to be looked at again. By what I've seen him do I'd call him Bronze, if I'd to call him anything. He's not had it written. I've not asked him to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:155:"Copper," said Brom. He had not been asked, and he said it as a man lays down a thing he has been carrying since a gate. "For good. There's a finding that says otherwise, on a table, and I'll never sign it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:159:"Same as me, then," he said. He set the stone a little higher on the page. "I'll tell you what I tell every one that comes over that hill with a number in his coat. A Bronze who lost my carter is worth less than a Copper who brought him home. Out here that's arithmetic. In there it was heresy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:181:It was a low room, longer than it was wide, with beams gone black and a hearth at the far end that a man could have stood in. Benches ran down both sides, with thirty or forty people on them, by crews, each crew at its own boards, and the noise dropped a little as the five came through the door and then rose again. He was looked at once, and not twice, by people who had heard a line said about him two hours ago and wanted nothing more just yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:185:It was one sheet, as Lira had told them: a piece of sized sailcloth as tall as a child, pinned to the plaster at its four corners, and written from top to bottom in the square black hand. He had said that he would read it when there was something on it that he had done. He went and stood in front of it now, with his thumbs in his belt, and began at the top, as he began everything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:189:It was the one word, and it had a line to itself. Below it came the rest, twelve of them, one under another, each a name with a number of people after it, or a trade where a name would not serve. He read them all, and they meant nothing to him yet, though one crew, about the middle, seemed to be entered under the name of its mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:209:Cael opened his mouth to say, and found that he had no way of telling it from here. It was west, beyond a line and a hill and a river and the whole of a country, at the far end of everything he knew; and the best keeper of a straight book that he had met out here had never so much as heard the town's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:215:Lira had come up on his other side. She had been standing in front of the sheet, as he had, a good deal longer and a good deal less quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:221:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:243:Seln had been sitting a little back from the lamp, as he sat a little back from anything. He leaned forward now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:255:She said it in her small dry voice, as she would have corrected a citation. Lira took her finger off the map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:285:Nobody in the room was watching him. He checked it, as he checked it everywhere, and the count came out at nothing, and for the first time it did not go round again by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:291:He read it over, and his hand had been steady.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:293:There was a thing in the grass on the Thornwater road that had gone beside them for half a mile, waiting to be told something. A whole country kept its Paths in its pockets whenever it set foot on a road, and would not say why. He had those two facts side by side in his head, as Karis had her noun and her six numbers, and he had the strong feeling that they were the two ends of one sentence and that nobody had yet given him the middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:295:He did not put that under the entry. It was true as well. It could go on its own leaf, when he knew what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:303:He did it without turning his head. He had done it so for longer than the boy had been able to lift a pack, at every door and gate and ford, and he no longer knew that he was doing it until it was done. The spear was on his left, half a pace back, grounded. The bow was on his right and a full pace back, standing on the good leg, as the bow always stood when there had been a day's walking. The boy was between and behind, where he had been told to be, and by the sound of him he was already looking for the table with the food on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:319:He set it down, as he set down a pack at a door, and looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:321:His eye went to the sheet from habit. He did not read the top of it, since he knew what was at the top; he read the bottom, where the ink was new, and it was as he had been told at the ferry: Pike had written a list. There were five names on one line, and in all the time he had been looking at that sheet there had never been a line on it with more than one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:323:He had heard their four lines said at the plank, or heard of them: a boat, a fence, the hide-woman's wagon. And today the stretch, walked at dusk, by the carter's own telling, with a hound pacing them for half the mile; and not one of the five had so much as lifted a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:327:They were at the boards nearest the door, on his right, with a lamp and one jug. He took them as he took ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:329:The girl was standing at the sheet, and he read her from the feet. She had her weight on the balls of them, even standing still, even with her arms folded, and her heels hardly marked the rushes. Somebody had spent years teaching those feet to leave a floor in a hurry. They had been taught on a chalked floor, too, with someone watching to give a mark, for she stood as if she were being scored. She was fast, and proud of it, and she had not learned yet that out here nobody was going to tell her so; he thought that it would cost her something before she did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:331:The big one he did not read at all. He was a fact, like a ford or a gradient, and one did not read a fact so much as allow for it. His hands were the hands of somebody who had lately been on a rope. He was pouring out a jug into five cups as though it were a thing that could be done wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:333:The small pale one had a notebook open on the table, in an inn, among cups, and a skin of some sort pinned under her fingers. As he watched she copied something from the one to the other, and looked at what she had written, and ruled a line beneath it with real satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:343:There was a man at the end of the bench. He sat a little back from the lamp, in a dark coat, with a strap across his chest and a cup in both hands, and his eyes were shut. Teague's look had gone across him the first time as it would have gone across a patch of plaster. He was the same height sitting as everybody else, and the same colour as the wall. He had chosen the one place at those boards that the light from the door did not quite reach, and Teague did not believe for a moment that he had chosen it by chance, or that he was asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:345:*That one first*, he thought. He ought always to have been first. The one a man finds on his second pass is the one he should have looked for on the first, and Teague marked it against himself, as a thing to be remembered on a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:349:He was the youngest of them. He had a book in front of him, shut on a pencil, and he sat quite still in the long light from the door with his hands on the cover; and he was not staring, as boys stared who wanted a place in a crew. He was reading. Teague could see the order of it. The boots. The wrist, and the leather on the wrist. The three in the doorway behind, one at a time, left to right. He was going over a stranger as Teague himself went over ground, and he was not troubling to hide it, any more than Teague was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:353:That was the part worth a price. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and anybody could tell a Skin by how he came through a door. This one sat like none of them. There was nothing in the set of him to say what he had in his hands, and nothing to say that they were empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:357:He stayed on the sill a breath longer than he needed, all the same, and let himself be read to the end. It seemed only fair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:361:Cael read him back, a thing at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:363:He was a man of forty or so, of the middle height, heavy in the forearm, with hair cropped close and gone grey over the ears. He had stopped on the sill before he came in. That was the first item, and Cael put the most weight on it. Three people had stopped behind him in the same instant, at the same distances, without a word; and for the length of a breath the man's eyes had gone nowhere at all, while he listened to them be there. He had counted his own before he counted the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:369:He had read Lira, and it had taken him three breaths. Brom had hardly taken him one. He had looked at Karis ruling her line, and something had happened at the corner of his mouth and been put down again, quickly, like a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:3:The paper was on the oak before the mist had left the river, and Lira read it with one boot on and the other in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:She had gone up to the eave on purpose to be first. Cael came after her at his own pace, which on level grass was anybody's pace now, and he took his inventory on the way, as he took it at the start of anything. The calf was a leg again on the flat; he had asked it the evening before, on the saddle road, and it had told him nine strides uphill and then shut. Brom's forearm was closed along the whole of its seam and wrapped by Brom's own choice, and it was to pull nothing. Lira had wound a strip of linen round her right ankle, over the stocking, and she walked on the whole sole of that foot. Karis had two pink lines across each palm and held a pencil for a quarter of an hour at a time. The nine fragments lay in him as they had lain since the ford, idle, each with its rent paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:15:"I told you to read it first," said Pike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:17:He had come out with the book under his arm and his coat on, and he set the flat stone on the page before he so much as glanced at the new slip. He had read it by lamplight when the carter brought it, and had no need to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:23:Pike read it out for the meadow, and then he read it again from the first word, with his shoulder turned to the tents. There were not many to hear. The dry roads had emptied the grass to seven fires, and at most of those people were still asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:25:"Seventy," said Brom. He had come up behind them without a sound, as a large man can who has spent sixteen days with nothing in his hands. "That's more than the six lines we've got. All of them put together."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:35:"Oxhollow's a ford with a fence round it. The keeper takes a copper a wheel, the same as my ferryman, and it's the keeper's paper. He'd sooner his wheels came back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:47:"A strip, then, with the shelf drawn down the middle of it, and a table beside. A line to every stretch." She was writing as she spoke. "I can rule that tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:49:Cael read the slip through once more, for the thing that was not in it. There was no name on it but the hold's. Nobody had asked to have it kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read. There was a fence to be set for a steading on the north water, at five marks, and a wheel to be fetched for a carter on the south road, and hay to be got in at the upper inn for a mark and the loft to sleep in. A string of mules wanted walking westward to the turf fire, and that slip he passed over without stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:57:When he had done, the paper with the horned head was at the top of the heap in his mind, and it had got there so quickly that he distrusted it. He made himself say why, in items, as he would have made a stranger say it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:The second was that nobody's finger was on it. The last large slip they took had carried a captain's name in capitals. A one-armed man had struck that name with his own pen before a meadow, and they had gone down to a river under the weight of it. This one had been posted for anyone at all, with no hold on it. If they took it they would take it off nobody, and the captain at the top of the sheet could read it when he came down and find nothing in it that had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:65:It was *ground*. Nothing in those three lines of careful writing had teeth; he looked for the opponent in it, from a habit as old as his reading, and found only a shelf, and a gorge, and a question about a wheel. Somebody wanted a mountain looked at closely and written down, with numbers, by people who would say what they had counted and no more. The paper named no tier and no rank. It asked for a cart and a count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:67:He had done that for nothing since before he could remember. He had catalogued a harness room and a painted floor and sixty feet of sprung oak, and two years of other people's fights from the east tier of a yard; and all of it had been held against him at one time or another, as the habit of a person who watched because he could do nothing else. Now a stranger with a ford was offering seventy marks for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:69:It was work, besides, that four half-mended people could do. A man with a bitten calf could pace a level shelf and write. A girl with a stiff ankle could tell him whether a hundred yards of it would bear her, and a researcher who could hold a pencil for a quarter of an hour had a cord, and a clock, and a head that kept numbers as a jar keeps water. Nobody would have to be fast, or hard, or to set anything alight; the job asked for no Path from any of them, and for no fragment of his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:93:"I said it as I say everything." But he had not, quite, and Cael saw Seln's mild eyes rest on the bald brown head for a breath longer than the answer needed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:103:Cael looked back from the ash. The eave was sixty yards behind him, and under it two men stood at the far end of the plank, where the oak ran out past the right-hand post. One of them had a book under his arm. The other stood with his head bent, as a clerk stands who has come to ask for the loan of a file. He was talking, low, and Pike was listening with his chin down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:107:He knew what it would be. A word had been dropped at that plank a quarter of an hour ago, in a voice that was not quite Pike's ordinary one, and Seln collected such things as other men collected debts. He would be asking what a shale-back was, and who had been under one, and what had become of them, and he would get it in fewer sentences than anybody else could have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:111:He had told that man at that same plank, ten days ago, that nobody was coming to take his report off him. It had been easy to say, and he had learned since that the saying was the smallest part of it. The promise had to be kept at exactly such moments as this one, when the report was sixty yards off and plainly worth having, and when nobody but himself would ever know that he had turned his back on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:125:Brom went all round it slowly, as round a horse he had sold and now met on a road. He set his right hand on the off wheel and rocked it, and stooped to look along the new axle-tree, and lifted the tail-board on its two chains and let it down again without a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:153:So three numbered boxes went onto the cart they had come east on, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward where Karis could see it; and at the bottom of the third, under the masonry chips, the reserve lay in its canvas with five thumbs in the wax. Seln came down the meadow as the canvas was being roped. He looked at the cart and seemed to recognise it, and put his hand for an instant on the place under the seat where a locked case had ridden from a bluff to a river. The case was on its strap across his chest. He said nothing whatever about a plank, and Cael kept his eyes off his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:161:The cart went up to the saddle between two people. It had no beast, for there was not a mule in Lowmarch to be hired for a mountain by a crew that might not bring it back. Seln took the near shaft and Cael the off, and they leaned, and the narrow wheels went round. On the flat of the meadow it was nothing. On the hill Cael counted nine strides and felt the calf begin to close, and at the tenth Lira's shoulder came in beside his without a word, and took the shaft, and he fell back to the tail and walked with his hand on a box until the leg forgave him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:167:He bore it as far as the dead tree. Then he stopped the cart with a word, and took the long rope from under the canvas, the ferryman's, which had drawn a wagon out of a wash and a boy's worth of bad air out of a well. He cut nothing. He doubled a bight of it, and knotted it, and passed the loop over his head and one shoulder so that it lay across his chest like a bandolier, and he made the two ends fast to the cart's frame on the near side, low, behind the wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:205:"You've left out the strap," said Brom, who had read none of it and knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:217:A second water came into the river at that place from the north. It was no wider than a cart, and brown, and it ran out of a fold in the hills between banks of alder, and beside it there went a track. Wheels had made it, a long time ago. The ruts had grown over with a short wiry turf, and here and there a sapling as thick as a wrist stood up in the middle of the way. Seln, who was thirty paces ahead, bent each one down with his boot as he passed, and the axle went over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:223:The hills closed in as they climbed. The alders gave way to the red-barked trees with the paired needles, and then those thinned as well, and there was grey rock on either hand with heather growing in its cracks. The stream had gone down out of sight on their right. He could hear it, a steady rushing from somewhere below the level of the track; and then, after they had gone another half mile, he began to hear something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:225:It was the cart. The off wheel had a dry place in its hub that ticked once at every turn, and he had been hearing it since the saddle without attending. Now he heard it twice. It ticked beside him, and then, about as long after as it took to draw a breath, the same small sound came again from across the valley and a little above, thin and exact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:241:The valley ended. It did not close gently, as valleys did in the country below; the two hillsides simply drew together until they were walls, and between the walls there was a slot full of blue shadow, with the stream coming out of the bottom of it white. The far wall went up sheer and dark for as high as he could tip back his head. The near one, on their own side of the water, was broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:243:It was broken in one place only. For two hundred yards along its foot the mountain had been shedding its skin for longer than there had been carters. A slope of loose stone came down there out of the sky, grey and flat and steep as the roof of a barn, spreading as it came, like corn poured out of a sack; and it ended at their feet in a litter of thin slabs, none thicker than a hand, that rang under the cart's wheel like broken crockery.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:245:He followed it up with his eyes. It narrowed as it rose, and at the top, a very long way above the red trees, it stopped against a level line. There was a shelf up there. He could see the edge of it against the light, running away westward along the gorge's lip as straight as a ruled margin, until the rock took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:5:There was a bar of gravel below the meadow, sixty yards long, where the river had laid down its stones on the inside of a bend, and at first light nobody was on it but a heron. The mist lay on the water to the height of a man's knee. Cael scratched a line across the gravel with his heel at the bar's lower end and walked back up to where she stood, with her boots laced tight and her long knife left on the bank, shaking out her wrists as she had shaken them out before every bout of her life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:7:"Run it," he said. "As fast as you've got. Stop on the line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:The second time she pulled up on the line itself, stumbling, with her weight all wrong and her face dark with temper, and said that it was like running hard at a door and then leaving it shut. The third time she ran as she must have run on a painted floor with a coach watching. She came down the bar like something thrown; and three strides short of the line the air cracked, and the gravel went up in a fan, and she was standing twelve feet beyond his mark with her hands out in front of her and the heron lifting off the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:29:"I mean that I didn't decide it. I was running, and then I'd gone." She looked at her own feet as if they belonged to somebody she had trusted. "It went off by itself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:35:"You've been saying no at the line. That's why it doesn't work." It was strange to be the one explaining a thing to her, on gravel, with her breath still coming fast, and he went carefully. "By the line you've been saying yes for sixty yards, with every stride, and then you want one no to stop all of it. You say it at the first stride. Then you say it again at the second. It isn't one refusal. It's as many as there are steps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:39:"Every time you do anything at all, until it's worn in. I had a book for it, on the hill. What to take late, and what to give. I refused something or other every minute of every Fifth-day for two years, and I thought it was the price of being let stay. I didn't know I was learning a trade."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:45:Brom had found his own lesson without help. A pale trunk lay stranded at the water's edge, as thick as a man's body, and he got it up on his shoulder and began to carry it along the wet stones where the footing was worst. The rule was a simple one: when the load shifted, he was to let it fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:63:Karis sat on the bank above them with a river stone in her lap and one finger resting on it. She had explained it to him in three sentences, as she explained anything. To set a point was not to light it; she chose the place and held it ready, like a thumb on a latch; and the task was to hold it so. She counted under her breath. At ninety a flame no bigger than a seed stood up on the wet stone, and she took her finger away and looked at it with dislike.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:83:*Eighteenth day. The nine, as things held. Not what each can do: what it charges me to carry it and leave it alone, which I'm calling rent.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:85:*Wind, from Lira. It wants the first stride of anything, and my feet lean a dozen times a day before I've decided. If I ever spend a burst, the landing locks for one count, and that count has never got shorter.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:89:*Iron, from Brom: the read. A pace and a half round me, the mass of a thing and which way it is going, never what it means to do. The cheapest and quietest thing I own, and the hardest to leave shut, because it feels like looking. I don't know what looking sounds like out here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:95:*Shadow. Source protected, and sealed against anyone who might carry the seeing west. It costs from the moment I wake and it drifts at dusk; on the fifth day it got as far as my boots. Two reasons to keep it shut now, where there was one: the seal, and whatever it is a road hears.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:97:*Storm, from Daeva. Gold weight, the heaviest thing I carry, and idle it only sits, so that I tire sooner than the miles account for. If I open it, the read is the air or my own body and the ground, never both, and a lane takes the air. Three lanes so far: after each, everything I have answered a beat late, for ten counts, and ten, and twelve, and came clean at eleven, eleven and thirteen. That is a bill, not a flag. I have not tried a fourth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:107:He read it over, and he had not expected it to come to so much.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:111:She had come up the bank with her hair stuck to her forehead, and she was reading over his arm as she had read over it since Ardenmere. Brom had the same leave. Karis, who sat ten feet away with her stone, saw what he showed her and never once looked at the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:137:They walked without talking, and by the middle of the morning Cael found that he had stopped missing it. Lira's long knife and Brom's bill rode where a stranger would see them, and at noon they sat on a bank and ate cold cake, and not one of the five so much as glanced at the dead wood lying under the trees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:139:The steading stood in a loop of the river, behind a fence of split posts as high as a man's chest, and the old man who held it was at his gate before they had come within a bowshot. He had a white dog beside him, and a face like the bark of the red trees. He read Pike's mark on the slip, and then he looked at their hands, each pair in turn, and it was the only question he put to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:141:"Forty," he said, and walked them along the fence to where a stretch of it had gone at the butts and leaned outward like a row of drunkards. "Cut them in the coppice. A stride apart, and deep. There's an axe." He turned back toward the house, and the dog stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:145:Cael watched him for a part of the first hour, while he and Lira were getting the measure of a spade and an iron bar. The axe was an old one, and its edge had been ground a little out of true, so that every tenth stroke or so it glanced in the grain and kicked back up the helve into the hands that held it. That was a load shifting, with no warning at all, and each time it happened Cael looked at Brom's wrists, and each time they were as brown as the rest of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:159:It was the new butts that Karis was looking at. She had read about fences, as she had read about most things, and she told Cael the principle while she chose a post from the stack. The end of a post that goes into the ground is charred first, to the depth of a finger, because rot will not take hold in charcoal; a fencer does it in a fire, and turns the post as he would turn a spit. She laid her post across two stones with its butt clear of the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:173:He went on down the fence, pushing at each new post with the flat of his palm as he passed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:175:Karis had not moved. Her hand was still out, over the wood, and her mouth had come a little open; and Cael, who had sat beside her through fifty-one minutes in front of a hooded seat, watched her want something more than she had wanted to win that day. He saw her mouth close, and her hand come back into her lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:177:She took out her notebook, and he read it over her arm, since she tilted the page for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:He came back with four coins in a sock and a boy behind him carrying a sack on his shoulder. The coins he counted into Brom's hand. The sack, which was flour, and a good twenty pounds of it, the boy let down at Brom's feet as though that were the natural place for anything heavy. The old man scratched his mark on the slip with a nail, three upright strokes and a bar across them, and gave it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:219:Cael felt it coming this time. The sun went behind the western ridge, all at once, as it did; the river turned from brass to lead; and he had dug holes for two days and his shoulders ached to the bone. It lifted behind the rest of what he carried, soft, and offered him the old thing. He had the refusal ready before it finished asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:223:It was one stride only, for he had it shut before the right foot landed, and the gravel spoke up again as rudely as ever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:229:"I report everything. You know that. You read it for two years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:231:"I did. It was a great deal of true paper, and I burned my copy of every sheet." He seemed to turn something over. "One stride is better than four. I should expect none at all by the time the weather turns, if you are as obstinate as I was. It never quite stops asking."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:249:He had walked the east road on the ninth evening, within an hour of stepping off a ferry, as far as a saddle and a little beyond it, and the north before light on the tenth. The south road had cost him two mornings, since it forked. The fourth was the road they had all come in by, and he had gone back along that one too, three miles, to see it as a person sees it who is arriving. This was the fifth. It left the north road at a dead tree and climbed, and the meadow called it cold; he learned why at the first turn, where a wind came down off bare ground that had no business in such mild weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:259:He did not use paper, for he had not put pencil to it for that purpose since a crossroads, where he had lit a fire with the last one. But the old form ran in him like a tune, and as he climbed it set itself out in the voice he had kept for the audit. *Cold road: leaves the north road at the dead tree, eleven hundred paces above the last roof. A mule's width from the outset. Water at the second turn, forty paces off the track, to the left. Three places of concealment below the crest, none showing use.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:263:It was long, and it stood straight, and he had found the same at every one of them, on five roads, for twenty days. A man who stands in a place, morning after morning, to watch a road leaves the mark of his feet there, and of his waiting, and Seln knew that mark as a baker knows his own thumb in a loaf. He had left it himself in every town he was ever posted to. There was none of it here. The grass had never been stood in, and he did not think that there was, in all this country, one person whose work it was to watch another and write down what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:273:He did not offer it. That was not sullenness; he had examined himself for it, with some care. He told them whatever bore on the day in hand: he had given them the gauge of the east road when a horse depended on it, and he had put five people where they ought to stand on a boat. The rest he kept, as he had always kept the rest, and he went about with it and waited for the level voice. *What did you see?* It would come at a fire, he supposed. The boy would say it, since the boy wrote everything down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:277:The case knocked against his hip. He turned at the crest, where the wind was, and went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:279:The sun was on the river when he came out above the meadow. Four people were on the gravel bar. One of them ran, and stopped dead on a line, on her two feet, and flung up both arms; and a large one carrying a tree let it roll from his shoulder and stepped out from under it as neatly as a dancer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:287:Pike read it twice, and did not tack it up; he passed it to them over the oak. *Wagon in the wash, east road. Out before the water. Three marks.* There was a mark under the words, a square with a peg at each corner, like a hide stretched to dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:303:They made a chain of it and had the bales out in half an hour, and while the last of them went up, Brom walked round the wagon as he walked round any load. When he came back he had the whole of it, and he gave it to them with his hands as much as his voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:319:The wagon lurched over toward its broken corner, and for the space of a breath it hung there, deciding. The check line came up out of the dust as taut as a bowstring. Lira went forward two strides on her heels, toward the edge, and Cael saw her knees bend in the way he had seen on the gravel bar, three strides short of a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:339:The check line had fouled. Cael saw it as the mule found its feet again: a loop of it had dropped off the lip and caught behind a rock in the bed, twenty feet out from the eastern bank, and while it lay there Lira could neither pay out nor take in. She had seen it too, from above. It was twenty feet of open air to that rock, and she could have been standing on it in the time it takes to blink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:345:The wagon went up the western ramp on three wheels and a pole, a yard and a wedge at a time. Its tailboard had cleared the lip by perhaps the length of the wagon when the wash filled, and the water did not come as a wave. It came as a brown tongue a foot high, quite quiet, with sticks in it, and behind the tongue the bed simply rose. Karis came up the ramp at a run with her skirts in her fists, and when Cael looked back from the top her notched stick was gone, and the place where a wagon had lain was hip deep and moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:363:"That wasn't no. That was a rope." She turned her hands over and looked at them. "A turn of rope round a stump does what a burst can't, as it turns out. Somebody might have told me that at Fenmark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:43:The woman said it as she might have said that barley stopped at a certain height on a hill. It was a thing about the ground, and nobody had decided it. One healer walked a route, and was not due; the rest of that trade kept to the country where somebody ranked it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:51:Up on the platform the bowman stood with his bow at the length of his arm. He lifted two fingers from the stave as the cart went under him, and Lira raised the ash stick an inch from her knees, and set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:He saw before the mule was in the water that the hold's men had been down. There was nothing at all on the gravel; and along the lip of the rock the white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a yard apart, and did not stop until they reached the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:61:Each of the five had a place on that river to look at. Brom looked at a slab in the near channel with a rim along one side, and as the cart drew level he put out two fingers and touched the tail-board, like a man greeting somebody across a street. Karis looked at a boulder the size of a loaf. Seln, walking behind, turned his head toward the deep water under the bar's flank and went on turning it until the place was behind him. Lira looked at the edge of the gravel, near the head, where round stones lay on round stones, and then she looked at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:93:Nobody moved. They had not had a fire on a road since the sixth day, when an old man asked Cael what he was; and he looked now at the dead wood lying under the scrub, twenty feet off, which not one of them had so much as glanced at for fifty days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:105:It was a small fire. Seln fed it as he did everything, with economy, and it gave off about as much light as a lantern. They sat round it with their hands out, five people who had not been warm after dark on a road since a valley with no name, and for some time none of them could think of a thing to say that was equal to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:109:"Lit with a flint," he said. He said it as a man reads the mark on a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:123:Then he came in, and sat down on his heels a little apart from them. He held his hands out toward the flames slowly, with the fingers spread, as a man puts a foot on pond ice to learn whether it will bear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:Seln unwound it for him, and looked, and wound it again more tightly, and kept what he had seen to himself. Brom sat through it with the patient face of a mule being shod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:She sat with her back against a wheel and the bound foot stretched toward the fire, and she had the stick across her knees. Every little while she turned it over, as he had seen her turn a lath on the morning of a bout that she did not expect to win.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:"*Four injured.* I've had it going round since the well. I'm on a wall in a meadow as one of four people who got bitten. That's the whole of what it says I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"I ran thirty yards of gravel at that ford with nothing in my feet," said Lira. "No Wind. No burst. I caught a dog. I've never run like it. I didn't know I could, and I'm telling you it was the best thing I've ever done on my two legs, and it cost—" She stopped. Her hand had gone toward the stave, and she took it back. "It cost what it cost. And there isn't a person alive who can say whether it was good. There's a man with a bow who lifted two fingers. There's a book that says nobody died."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:195:He had the sentence ready; it had come to him at once, whole, and it was the worst thing he could have said to her. *Isn't it better?* He had sat in a bar of red light ten days ago and written that no instrument out here could read him, and that he was free; and he had meant every word, and he meant it still. Not one book in this country had a column for a tier or a rank, or for what he was, and it was the best thing that had happened to him since a brass plate went dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:203:He took out Hesk's book instead, since it was the one honest thing he had by him. He wrote the day, and the place, and under them her question, word for word as she had put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:215:"As you said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:237:Cael had never seen him do it. On their first day at Lowmarch he had simply been discovered at the plank's far end, looking at the last slip as though he had been there for some time. Now he started at the left-hand post, in the open, with his hands behind him, and went along the oak a slip at a time, as Cael himself went along it, giving each of the fourteen the same attention; and at the tenth he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:243:"That's been up since the day we got here," said Lira. She had come along on her stick to see. "I read it on the ninth. It's been there fifty days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:269:"Not here. If a thing bears, you'll say so, as you did at this plank, and in the loft; and the rest is yours." He chose the next words with some care. "I'm not being polite. I mean that nobody is coming to take it off you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:271:"I had begun to suspect as much." Seln looked up from the bark at last, and something had eased about his eyes. "I find the suspicion a good deal harder to carry than the report; eight roads and a ford keep very well, and one simply walks about with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:277:He went and stood at the end of the plank, to wait for a man with a book. Cael watched him go, a mild man in a dark coat with eight roads in his head and a well's slip over his heart; and he thought that he had perhaps never been of so much use to anybody as by declining to ask a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:301:"Same as anyone," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:317:"Then that's a fifth of forty," he said. "Eight marks. I'd pay eight for that to a Gold or to a goat, if the goat came back." He dipped his pen. "There's no column for what it was done with. I thought you'd read that off the page the first day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:319:"I read it," said Brom. "I wanted it said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:339:Brom nodded to him, as one tradesman to another, and walked off down the meadow toward the ash, like a man who has set down something heavy and has not yet decided whether to miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:341:Pike watched his back, as he had watched it once before, on the day he paid out two worn coins.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:345:He said it level, in his carrying voice, as he gave out any line, though there was nobody near enough to hear but four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:3:Pike gave out the mail as he gave out everything, twice, with his shoulder turned to the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:13:She came and took it herself, and Cael knew her broad dusty back, and the long knife on her thigh. As she turned away she saw four people in linen under the eave, and she stopped, and looked at Cael with her head a little on one side. Then she tucked her letter into her bodice and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:15:There was one for the old man under the ash, who walked up for it slowly and held it as a thing that might be hot, for he had no letters. Pike told him, so that nobody else could hear, to come back at dusk and it would be read to him, and the old man nodded, and carried it away unopened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:17:The sack was nearly empty when Pike brought out a folded sheet, sealed with plain wax that had no mark in it; and he read the outside to himself and frowned, and read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:33:He read it three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:35:At the first reading he saw only that the same three words stood inside and out. Hesk had put them at the head of the sheet as another man would have put the name of a street; it was where Cael lived now, and the old man had written it down without a word of comment and gone on to the next thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:61:She read it, and she was a long time over five lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:85:He read it twice to himself, and then he turned his shoulder and gave it to the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:It went out exactly as it had come to them on bark, beside a well, and not a word of it had been changed. Cael listened to *four injured* go down the meadow in that level voice, past the woman with her dough, and a man mending a trace, and a big fellow in a new coat, who looked at Brom's sling and then at his own boots. Under the ash an old carter with an unopened letter in his belt had lifted his chin at the first word, and he kept it lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:91:He put it to all five of them, as he put it to anybody; and Lira drew a breath, and Cael heard her do it, and heard her let it go again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:111:It took him as long as a supper takes. He could not hold the cloth taut and write on it as well, so the boy stood with both palms spread on the sailcloth while the one hand moved, and the pen went to a horn pot on the bench and came back. *Teague.* It was the one word, on a line of its own; and then came the names and trades and numbers, one under another, each as square and black as the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:113:Lira counted them aloud as they went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:123:There was one line more, which read *the shingle pair*; and at the next boards two men in tarred aprons watched it go on at the foot of the sheet. One of them looked across at Brom, and lifted his cup an inch, without rancour, as a carter does who has been passed on a hill by a lighter load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:133:"He came down the cold road on the fifty-seventh, with his three and his mules, and he walked to the plank before he'd had so much as a wash, and stood in front of the place where that slip had been." He turned the cup. "Then he asked me for it. I'd kept it by, struck, as I'd struck it, and I gave it him, and he read it twice; I counted, since it's what I'd have done. Then he had me open the book at your leaf, and he read your line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:147:Cael had watched that man stand on a sill and listen to three people behind him before he looked at a room; and he thought that such a man would read *four injured, none lost* more slowly than anybody in the meadow, and would know to the ounce what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:"That's been on my oak since the thaw," he said. "It's the carters' well, at the fork, where the south road splits. It went sour. A lad was sent down after a bucket, and they wound him up asleep; he woke, and he's not been right about a ladder since." He drew five strokes on the bark. "Nobody's been down it; they water at a stream a mile on, and curse me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:169:It was a halting-ground like the others, a level of beaten earth with a ring of chock-stones, and in the middle of it stood a curb of dry stone as high as his waist. Two posts carried a windlass over it, with an iron crank at one end and no rope on the drum, and the trough beside it had gone green and then dried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:181:"It's in the middle box, over a ferryman's horse, and I couldn't hold it if it were here; but I know it by heart. It was a lecture on the works of towns, on wells, and drains, and cellars, and I copied it because nobody else in the room was troubling to." She put her bound hands on the curb. "It's the one leaf in three boxes that is as true on this side as on the other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:185:"That foul air is heavier than good, and lies at the bottom of a shaft as water lies in a cup, and has a surface. A flame will show you where the surface is, for it goes out there; and a man goes out a little lower down, and more quietly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:189:"As you'd get water out of a cup. You bail it, or you stir it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:195:Brom had been forbidden to pull anything, and nobody had said that he might not let a thing down. He took the candle on its string, and Seln lit it with his flint, at a halting-ground, on a road. Brom lowered it over the curb with his right hand, and called the knots as they went through his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:211:Brom showed them what to cut, standing over Seln with the bill and pointing with his chin at this branch of thorn and that. They bound up a faggot as wide as the shaft, and made it fast to the ferryman's rope and the rope fast to the drum. Lira sat on an upturned bucket with both hands on the crank, and wound the great prickly bundle down into the dark and up again, and down, like somebody churning butter in a giant's kitchen, while Cael stood on one leg and took the other side of the handle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:229:"You're the only one of us who can stand at that crank and brace. Lira can wind, sitting. If I come up limp, somebody with two legs will have to get me over the curb." Cael was tying a loop to sit in. "And a leg's no use to anybody on a rope."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:235:"I shall count," she said. "Aloud, by tens, and you'll answer every ten. If you miss one, they wind, and I shan't call it a second time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:It was cold in the shaft, and it grew colder with every turn of the drum. The stones went by an arm's length from his nose, course under course, each with its edge; and Anchor asked about every one of them. *There? There?* A well was nothing but rims. Blade wanted the arris of the curb far over his head; and the Iron read stirred, and offered him the weight of thirty feet of stonework round him, and he kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:247:That struck him oddly, hanging in the dark with a flame between his feet. He had spent a long while learning what each of the nine cost, and where each one stopped, and he had entered a rent against each on a river bank; and it had not once crossed his mind that he might meet a danger to which all nine had simply nothing to say. The bad air was not hunting him and could not be priced, and it cared nothing for a Path or the want of one. It was merely there, at the bottom of a hole, as it was for a carter's boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:271:He looked at the candle, which burned in its chink as it had burned all morning. The flame was perhaps a little longer than it had been, and a little blue about the root; or perhaps it was not, and it told him nothing that he could have sworn to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:297:"You called before the candle did," said Karis. She was kneeling by him, and her voice had the brightness it took on over a clean result. "By a minute, I should say. What told you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:331:A carter came up the south fork with two mules while the last bucket stood on the curb. He stopped them at the edge of the halting-ground and stood watching Brom, plainly waiting to learn whether a large young man would drop where he stood. When a full minute had gone by he came and dipped his own cup, and then he scratched a wheel on the slip with the point of his knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:341:"*Air cleared,*" said Karis. "He asked me what we'd done, and I told him, and he's written it down as though a person cleared air every day of the week." She had counted the four in. "Fifty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:345:It was the bowman's ash, and the fork at its top had gone pale and smooth where her palm sat. She turned it over once when Pike had done, and looked at it, and then she walked to the inn wall, beside the left-hand post, and stood the stick against the plaster; she set it upright, with some care, as one leaves a borrowed tool where the next person will want it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:359:She had been trying the foot without it since the well, a few steps at a time, behind a cart or at the river's edge, wherever she judged that nobody could see. Nothing had mended. The hold's woman had told her how the ankle would be, and it was being so; and to run on it was a risk, which she had looked over as she looked over a draw, and taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:369:He counted. He was doing it before he knew, as he counted everything, the strides and the breaths between them; and by the end of the first sixty yards he had the whole of it. Her left leg did what it had always done. Her right came down a little short, and the push from it arrived a shade behind where it ought to be, so that every second stride began a little after she had meant it to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:375:She was not speaking to him so much as putting the thing on record.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:405:Seln had drawn the stitches two days before, with Karis's small scissors, and Brom had counted them out as he had counted them in. The seam ran from the middle of the forearm to the wrist, pink and dry; and at the wrist end, where it had opened above a wash, it had closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:409:He looked at it for a long time, with the look that he gave a lock that had opened at the first touch, slow and without pleasure, as if he were waiting to learn what was wrong with it. Then he took up the cloth and wound it on again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:419:"On a road that's a declaration. In a meadow it's a lie." He pulled his sleeve down. "It mends as an arm, or it doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:435:He wrote the long entry on the sixty-ninth evening, at the boards by the inn door, where the light was. It was the hour at which he had once written that he was free, and he had not gone back to read that page, since there was no need.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:437:He had walked up the saddle road before supper, as he did now every evening, to learn what the leg would say; and he had stood on the slope with the meadow below him until it had finished saying it, and come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:447:*The nine: held. Wind leans, Anchor asks at every door of the inn, and Shadow lifts at dusk and is told no. I pay the rent on all of it and spend none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:471:"It's on the oak in the morning," he said. "Read it before you say so."

