/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n '\\b(Bracken|Current|Denvash|Ephram|Force|Glass|Kindling|Norhold|Ostrand|Reydan|Stone|Storm|Vell|Ash|Shield)\\b' editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-{01..15}.md" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:Shadow, the quiet one, had lifted at dusk, as it lifted at every dusk, soft behind all the rest, and he had held it where it was while Seln laid a fire ten feet away. Blade was the newest of the nine and the quickest, and it had asked about the edge of the scree, and for once had been told that it was right. Storm did nothing whatever. It was a Gold's worth of weather, the heaviest fragment he owned, and it sat in him as a full pail sits on a step, so that he was aware of it all day in the way that one is aware of a pail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:95:He had done so all the way from Ostrand, and Cael had taken it for tiredness, and been wrong. Brom was reading, slowly, as he read anything, and a little after noon he gave them what he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:167:"A long way. One's for Denvash, and the other's Ardenmere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:197:*Fourth day. The letters to Hesk and to Vell went west by a courier on a mule, a copper each, as far as the turf fire. She showed us the ford by riding it. She did not ask what the letters were. She did not ask what we were either. Entered, because I can't remember the last person who didn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:45:"You have talked to him every day since Ostrand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:135:Karis counted it. She had the purse from Ostrand in her lap, and the ferryman's price and the smith's on a cloth beside her, and she counted the whole twice over in her small dry voice, under her breath, and wrote one figure at the foot of a leaf. Then she did a thing Cael had never seen her do with a number, which was to turn the notebook over on her knee, face down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:211:He thought about it through the first two crossings, with his hand on a rail that had been worn pale by other hands. For fourteen sittings he had climbed four steps in Ostrand to stand on a number carved in a wool exchange's floor, and he had stood there to be looked at. A seat had looked, under a hood, and a procurator, and two hundred people on benches, and every one of them had been deciding what he was while he stood. It had cost a house its year, and a registrar thirteen sleepless nights, and four people their enrollments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:229:Three things happened in Cael at once, and he had been waiting for them. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, as breath gathers before a shout, for a push at that range would have turned a tree. Storm leaned, and showed him the air between the scow and the trunk as a thing with a grain in it. And Wind remembered a painted floor in his feet, and offered to put him somewhere else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:305:He thought about how to say it, for he had been on a great many boards. A crossroads in Ardenmere had carried him, in chalk, and a hall at Norhold, and four black ones in a sunk yard, and every one of them had told the people looking at it what to think of him before he opened his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:17:He looked for the wall first, since every town he had ever entered had kept one, even Denvash, which was nothing; and a wall meant a gate, and a gate meant a man in it with a book. He looked for the station next, the low stone house with the windowless building behind, and for a board of hours, and for any roof with a seal burned into its sign. His eye went over the place three times in search of them, like a tongue going back to where a tooth has been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:99:He had done this once before. He had been new to a district, and not long past his Kindling, and he had stood in front of a board at a crossroads in Ardenmere for the better part of a morning before he let himself go near the yard it spoke of. A girl had told him afterward that almost nobody did that. Most people either never looked, or walked straight in and were carried out the same day, and he had done a third thing, which was to stand in the road and try to understand the board first. It had served him then, and he did it again now, slowly, a slip at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:161:"Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads," he said. "Pike. Now you know less than you did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:249:"I had the word ready," he said. "The one Bracken wrote. I've had it ready since I was fourteen, for every door and every desk, and I'd got so I could say it before they asked." He picked up a stick and put it down. "And there wasn't anywhere for it to go. He ruled five columns and I went looking for the sixth. I think I'd have minded less if he'd had one and left it empty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:Wind had his feet first. They had leaned for a month, a dozen times a day, and they leaned now toward the rise as if the ground sloped that way. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, slowly, a breath at a time. The Iron read wanted to open and tell him the mass of everything within a pace and a half, which was nothing but a mule; it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. Ember offered a point in the dry grass at the foot of the swell, and behind it another and another, so that he could see how the whole slope would take fire along a line. Anchor had found the wired rim and asked about it. Blade wanted Brom's bill. Storm turned over, once, like a great fish under a boat, and showed him the air between himself and the grey thing as a lane already laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:199:"Did you ever hear of a ledger-keeper called Vell?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:277:*Asked Pike whether he had heard of Vell. No. Seln put a finger on the west edge of the map and said what's there. Once. The map is turned east now.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:289:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. Since my Kindling I've been the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:353:That was the part worth a price. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and anybody could tell a Skin by how he came through a door. This one sat like none of them. There was nothing in the set of him to say what he had in his hands, and nothing to say that they were empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:69:She ran it as Bracken had run a counter, which Cael saw within an hour, and it warmed him to her more than any welcome could have done. She had a tally of the sacks on a board by the barn door, in strokes, and when Brom took the first one up the ladder she drew her charcoal through a stroke without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:*The carter says the roads are dry and the season's open: work on every line of his map from now until the snow. I have been in somebody's season since my Kindling. The registry had one, and the circuit, and the house on the bluff, and the seat at Ostrand. I never chose the day any of them began, and each one ended with a ruling about me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:85:"He got in beside me. He put his arms down under it, right under, to the shoulder, and he lifted. You know how a Force lifts. He was at it a long while. I could feel it going out of him through the water like heat off a bake-stone." The man wet his lips. "Nothing came. The sun was on the ridge. I said to him, it's getting late, and he laughed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He had gone to a good many fights. He had gone to them down a lane in Ardenmere, and up the steps of a hall at Norhold, and across sixty feet of sprung oak every week for two years; and in every case somebody else had drawn the card. A keeper had matched him, or a bracket had, or a house that needed its charter, and he had walked out onto whatever floor they pointed to and tried to lose as little as he could. Nobody had drawn this one. A slip had been laid on a plank for anyone at all, and he had put up his hand, and reached for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:"He was Force," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:263:Cael watched her come down it and thought of a painted floor at Norhold. She had been quicker there, with the burst, and she had never looked like this, for there she had been running at a mark. Here she ran thirty yards of loose stones for no one, and stopped because she had decided to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:281:There was nothing to do it against. He lay on his back in scrub that smelled of dust, with the sun climbing to the top of the sky, and the fragments offered him things. Anchor had found the lip of the ledge from the first moment he saw it, and it asked about that edge now, patiently, though the edge was three hundred yards behind him. *There?* He told it that the stones were the hold's, and to leave them. Storm lay in him like a full pail and showed him the air above the river, cool and heavy and sliding downstream, as a lane half made already. Wind wanted the bar. It had watched Lira run that gravel three times and it had her stride by heart, and it leaned in his feet though they were pointing at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:179:"At Norhold there was a sheet. I lost to a girl that a whole program had built, with all of you at the rail, and when it was over the sheet told me to a tenth of a point how far I was from Silver. I hated it, and I kept it; I've still got it somewhere, in with the certificate." She looked into the flames. "And on the hill there was the ladder. Nobody had ever made me anything, and they made that to my height, with my name on the bottom rung. I knew where I was, every morning. Iron, Rank One, and I could have told you to a rung how far above it I meant to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:197:It was the same fact, and that was what he saw, sitting across a fire from her, and it went through him coldly. Nothing out here measured anybody; and to him that was a door standing open, and to her it was a floor taken away. He had been measured wrongly ever since his Kindling and had wanted only to be let alone; she had been measured rightly, a rung at a time, and it had been the nearest thing to love that any institution ever gave her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:91:*Compression, from Reydan. Only at contact; the load goes through me into the ground and the bill comes later. It charges nothing until something hits me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:97:*Storm, from Daeva. Gold weight, the heaviest thing I carry, and idle it only sits, so that I tire sooner than the miles account for. If I open it, the read is the air or my own body and the ground, never both, and a lane takes the air. Three lanes so far: after each, everything I have answered a beat late, for ten counts, and ten, and twelve, and came clean at eleven, eleven and thirteen. That is a bill, not a flag. I have not tried a fourth.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:99:*Anchor. Unnamed; the hand with no face. Fixed points, and lines between them, and a cage; and one unbinding, done once, on purpose. It sees what is fixed: sure at ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen. I don't know its reach, or its rate, or its price. Ever since the Ostrand road it has asked about every sill and gate and doorway I've gone through, and a town with no wall has a great many doors.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:101:*Blade, from Ephram. Partial still. It asks "Edge?" of anything with a corner on it, takes a name, and settles.*

