Down the gravel Lira had turned and set her feet.

---

He had told three people what it cost them, and he thought he owed the book the same account of himself.

He wrote it on the eighteenth day, at noon, on the bank above the bar, while Brom carried his tree and Lira ran, and it took him the better part of an hour. On the hill he had only ever listed what each of the nine could do.

*Eighteenth day. The nine, as things held. Not what each can do: what it charges me to carry it and leave it alone, which I'm calling rent.*

*Wind, from Lira. It wants the first stride of anything, and my feet lean a dozen times a day before I've decided. If I ever spend a burst, the landing locks for one count, and that count has never got shorter.*

*Pressure, from the Bronze from Ardenmere. It gathers over most of a breath, where anybody can see it gathering, and so it is the easiest to refuse.*

*Iron, from Brom: the read. A pace and a half round me, the mass of a thing and which way it is going, never what it means to do. The cheapest and quietest thing I own, and the hardest to leave shut, because it feels like looking. I don't know what looking sounds like out here.*

*Compression, from Reydan. Only at contact; the load goes through me into the ground and the bill comes later. It charges nothing until something hits me.*

*Ember, from Karis. One point, one channel, and it would set that point on anything I look at for long.*

*Shadow. Source protected, and sealed against anyone who might carry the seeing west. It costs from the moment I wake and it drifts at dusk; on the fifth day it got as far as my boots. Two reasons to keep it shut now, where there was one: the seal, and whatever it is a road hears.*

*Storm, from Daeva. Gold weight, the heaviest thing I carry, and idle it only sits, so that I tire sooner than the miles account for. If I open it, the read is the air or my own body and the ground, never both, and a lane takes the air. Three lanes so far: after each, everything I have answered a beat late, for ten counts, and ten, and twelve, and came clean at eleven, eleven and thirteen. That is a bill, not a flag. I have not tried a fourth.*

*Anchor. Unnamed; the hand with no face. Fixed points, and lines between them, and a cage; and one unbinding, done once, on purpose. It sees what is fixed: sure at ten feet, faint at twelve, nothing at fourteen. I don't know its reach, or its rate, or its price. Ever since the Ostrand road it has asked about every sill and gate and doorway I've gone through, and a town with no wall has a great many doors.*

*Blade, from Ephram. Partial still. It asks "Edge?" of anything with a corner on it, takes a name, and settles.*

*Nine. And one anomaly, not counted, that came from nobody. Still open. Still real. Patience.*

*Deployment since the Line: none.*

He read it over, and he had not expected it to come to so much.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:5:There was the corner of the trough, and the well's coping, and a post by the long house door where a hired man might reasonably stand to look at the weather. Each lay ten strides from the last, or a little under. The leg gave him eleven, as it had on a gravel bar in the dark; at the twelfth the calf shut like a fist and held him wherever he happened to be, which was generally among geese. So he had learned to arrive somewhere on the tenth, of his own accord, and to make it look like a choice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:He did with it what he did with everything, which was to measure the thing and then live inside the measure. He drew water in elevens and carried in elevens, and he had begun to reckon the whole hold as so many of them from the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:17:He had always taken an inventory at the start of anything. For two years it had been the same one, a count of the fragments he carried and how each of them lay idle; and on the fifty-fourth morning he caught himself at the head of a ladder, taking another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:*Fifty-fourth day. The ladder. Brom, one hand. Karis, elbows. Lira, seated, counting aloud. Mine, by the arms; the calf gives eleven strides on the flat and no rungs at all. Seln, as usual. The nine: idle, and asking. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:39:So he stood. There was work for a large man in that yard at every hour of the day: sacks to shift and water to carry, and a gatepost that wanted lifting back into its socket. He was forbidden all of it. He stood by the barn door with his hands hanging and watched two of the hold's men take a post out of the ground between them, with a lever, in the time it would have taken him to spit on his palms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:51:The hold's mule had come the length of her tether. She laid the whole of her long head on his good shoulder, and leaned, and sighed; and Brom stood under the weight of it with something like gratitude.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:69:The hold had changed toward them, though no one made a speech of it, Thornwater making none about anything; he read it instead in small things. There were five bowls on the long board at supper, set down among the others, where on their first stay the crew had eaten at the end. A child of four came across the yard on the second morning, with great care, carrying an egg in both hands, and gave it to Lira, and ran. And the old man in the chimney corner, who had not woken for anything in Cael's hearing, opened one eye when Brom came in under the lintel and moved his feet to make room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:73:He had been told that the hold did not feed people for sitting, and he was the only one of the five who could stand on both feet and use both hands. So he carried for all of them, though he was not built for it. He went up the ladder with a sack of barley across his narrow shoulders and the expression of a man doing a long sum in his head, forty times in a morning; and at the end of each day he reported to nobody at all how many he had taken up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:81:She had been set on an upturned trough by the barn wall, with her foot up on a sack. The woman had put her there on the first morning as she might have put down a bucket, and Lira had endured it for an hour. Then she had asked for a whetstone, and after the whetstone, for every blade in the hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:115:There was a trestle in the barn, twelve feet of scrubbed board where the hold mended its sacks. Seln and one of the men brought the boxes down the ladder between them, and she had them set along the board on their sides, with the lids off, in their order: left, and middle, with its one corner worn white, and right. Their open mouths faced her, and she sat down on a keg before them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:They divided her between them. Cael turned leaves and fetched what she named, for he could stand eleven strides at a time and the boxes were within three. Brom, who was allowed to hold nothing, read out the tags on the bundles in his slow voice, and was corrected. Lira was given the pencil, which was a mistake, and everybody knew it by the second line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:131:"I don't hold with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:133:"It isn't for you to hold with them. It's for you to put one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:151:Lira wrote it out, pressing hard enough to mark the leaf beneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:165:"No. I'll put it properly. That's everything I know about the wrong country." Her chin went along the shelves, left to right. "Every leaf I've copied since I could hold a pen, and every one of them is about the place we walked out of: tiers, and ranks, and what an Arbiter station does with a person. I've read forty accounts of how a Path is assessed, and there isn't a page in three boxes that has the word *stillhound* on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:179:Brom had picked a tag out of the right-hand box and was holding it to the light, at arm's length, with his good hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:213:"I know we did." She shifted her grip on the ash. "I've had a rank read out before, and a result. I never had one that gave my hurts and left out the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:227:"He's forty marks in his box against your coming," said the carter. "He told me to say that, and to say that he'd not paid a forty since the thaw." He scratched his neck. "I'm to tell you nothing about the top of his sheet, for there's nothing. The man wasn't down when I left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:233:*Fifty-seventh day. Thornwater. Fifth line, in Pike's hand, by the carter. Forty marks, in his box.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:237:He had had a great many written about him. A panel had given him one for a bout, and it had been arranged beforehand; a house had kept a rate for him that was scattered on purpose, so that it should never settle into a line that anybody could read. The registry had never managed a tier or a rank for him, and had settled on a word. There had been a question mark on a board in Ardenmere, and a level on an advisory that he had never been shown. Some of those figures had flattered him and some had been meant to sink him, and he could not think of one that had simply been counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:239:This one had been counted: a man who was not there had taken a bowman's word and a hold's mark, and written down how many dogs and how many hurts, and he had not made either figure a hair kinder than it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:261:The woman had unwound them yesterday and wound them again more thinly. There were two lines across each palm, the upper where she had first taken hold and the lower where she had taken hold again; they would be pink in a month, the woman said, if she left them shut. Karis had inquired how one left a hand shut, and had not been given an answer. She had not set an Ember point since the ford, and she doubted that with these hands she could have said where one would go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:263:So on the fifty-eighth morning she sat on the tail-board of a cart in Thornwater's yard with the calfskin unrolled beside her. She could not hold its corners down, and so Brom had brought four stones from the well's foot, and set one on each, and stepped back with his arm in its sling, looking as pleased as if he had been allowed to carry something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:279:The man turned his head and looked at her with the good eye. Then he looked across the yard, at a small pale person on a tail-board with a map spread out under four stones, and a boy beside her holding a pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:285:She could not even resent it. A road was the whole of what such a man owned. He knew which ford shifted and which hold cheated on a measure, and he was worth his hire exactly so long as other people did not know those things; one did not hand that to a stranger who would write it down and carry it away in a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:305:"You mean the quiet ground? Everybody knows the quiet ground. Nobody goes in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:337:"As she said it. All three, with the question mark after the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:349:"This has. Mind where you put them: they don't go under *quiet ground*, which is her noun, and I can't source it; I don't know what it is. But she said that everybody knows it, and that's a claim about a whole country, and I have just watched it be true of the first person I didn't ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:11:"There's a slip on that plank with a seal on it. Two marks, for a crew to stand on a boat all day." He put his palm flat over the four heaps and the fifth together. "He eats that in two days. So does the cart, standing. Every second morning one of those fourteen slips goes down a horse, and we've done the work for it, and we're no further on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:25:"And there is the other matter." Seln turned his cup in his hands. "The courier looked at his feet before she looked at us, and so did the woman with the hides, and the boy at the ferry, and the boy with the tally. A shod horse on these roads is a notice, in large type, and what it says is the word the old man used. I should prefer not to walk into every hold east of here behind it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:75:The ferryman came out from behind the mule, wiping his hands on his thighs, and walked round the gelding once. He ran a hand down each leg in turn and lifted the feet, and then he took hold of the upper lip, not roughly, and looked in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:"We've more silver in that bag than this whole meadow has seen," said Lira, "and you want us living on two marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:189:"Six days," said Lira. "To be worth two marks to somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:197:He was a brisk, pleasant, well-kept man with ink on his cuffs, and he had a clerk at his elbow who carried a ledger and a stick of dark wax. He seemed to Cael to be doing several things at once and to have his mind on a different one. His goods stood on the western landing in a long stack: bales, and tarred casks, and a number of crates nailed shut, and on the end of every one a mark had been burned with an iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:199:It was the mark that was in the wax, and Cael looked at it with care, as he looked at anything that came twice: a hook, with a bar under it, which told him nothing about the man who used it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:213:Here he was worth two marks among five, and what looked at him was a river. There were alders on the far bank, and a heron, and upstream, a long way off, something that might have been a boat drawn up under a bank. Whoever was in it would see five heads on a rail and count them, and that was all they would ever want from him; nobody on that water cared what the heads had in them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:231:*Hold*, he told all three fragments. It took him no longer than it takes to lower a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:241:He grunted, and his boots slid a hand's breadth on the planks, and he bent his knees and put his back into the pole until the cords stood out in his neck. He was a large young man pushing a tree with a stick, and he had never looked less like anything else. The root-plate swung, slowly, and went wide of the cable by the length of an arm, and the whole pale mass turned under the stern and went on down the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:251:The ferryman made his mark on the slip with a thumb dipped in tar: a ring with a dot in it, which Cael supposed was the shed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:255:"Ferry, the landing's goods. Six crossings stood. Nothing lost. Two marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:305:He thought about how to say it, for he had been on a great many boards. A crossroads in Ardenmere had carried him, in chalk, and a hall at Norhold, and four black ones in a sunk yard, and every one of them had told the people looking at it what to think of him before he opened his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:311:"Two marks," said Lira, to the fire. "For standing still."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:13:The second thing was his own hound, the one that had torn his coat. It had lost Brom at the water's edge when his declaration stopped, and he had forgotten it while he watched a man hold a river. It had come to a conclusion as well, and a worse one, for it was going back to the first declaration it had felt on that gravel, and to the person who had made it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:29:It was the size of a calf, and she was the smallest of the five of them. Its weight came down the steel into her two hands, and her hands slid, a finger's length, and she let go with the left and took a fresh hold higher up; and then the right slid, and she did the same again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:65:He had left the stone. The river had what he had been holding, and had taken it over the lip into the deep water, and he waded the five yards to the gravel flat-footed, with the bill tucked under his right arm. He looked at Lira, and at Karis's hands, and last at the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:87:Four hurt people could not keep the hold through a night as five whole ones had kept it for a mile. And when the first of them broke, three would be standing ready for it, as seven had stood ready for a man shaking a lift out of his arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:91:So he did what he had done from the east tier of a yard every week for two years, which was to set out what he had counted and look for the seam in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:153:All nine fragments were up. They had risen at the first stride and they rose again at each one after, and he had not known that there could be so many ways of wanting to do a thing. It was full dusk, and Shadow, the sealed one, was so near the surface that he felt the gravel begin to hush under his left boot; he stamped that foot down, hard, on purpose, and made the stones speak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:167:He had written it in Hesk's book a dozen times, and had never before stood in it with something coming. *One*, and every fragment he owned went dumb. There were no hands to put down. For that one count he was a boy standing in a river with a knife, who could not have shifted his feet for the forty marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:239:"It's holding the foot the right way round," she said. "It comes off when there's a roof and a lamp, and not before. I've said I'm fine. I'd be obliged if everybody would go on believing it till then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:315:"A week before you hold a spoon. Longer for that." She nodded at the notebook's corner, which showed at the breast of Karis's coat, and Karis looked as though she had been sentenced by a court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:351:She took the charred stick from her apron and made her mark, pressing hard: a thorn twig, three spines to a side, and under it the wavy line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:353:The bowman had not gone. He stood in the barn door with the dark behind him and looked at the five of them on the straw, like a man who has said a thing once already that night and wishes to hear whether it still holds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:371:She had never read a leaf of that book; whatever he showed her of the fragments she saw, and the book she left alone. She turned her face a little toward the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:405:*Fifty-third day. Thornwater. The ford, at dusk: five dead, counted by the hold's bowman. Two went down the river and are not counted. They're open.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:415:*Seln did the shadow work tonight. I didn't. Out here there's a ledger and a ferry west once a month, and a defector's tradecraft in my hands would tell anyone who reads carefully where I learned it. The seal holds. Different country, same arithmetic.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:He had written that under the inventory an hour ago. The last line of it was his own count and nobody else's: it was for the nine things he carried, and whether he had let one of them out. Since then he had done nothing at all but keep it true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:33:Shadow, the quiet one, had lifted at dusk, as it lifted at every dusk, soft behind all the rest, and he had held it where it was while Seln laid a fire ten feet away. Blade was the newest of the nine and the quickest, and it had asked about the edge of the scree, and for once had been told that it was right. Storm did nothing whatever. It was a Gold's worth of weather, the heaviest fragment he owned, and it sat in him as a full pail sits on a step, so that he was aware of it all day in the way that one is aware of a pail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:195:Cael sat on the shingle and wrote while the horse drank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:241:It was Shadow, the one fragment of the nine that he kept sealed. He knew it, and he was ready, as he had been ready at every dusk since a door log in a wool exchange. It came up soft behind the other eight and offered him what it always offered, which was to make less of him. He told it no. He had told it no so many times that the word had worn smooth, and he was tired, with eleven miles in his legs, and he said it as a man says good night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:259:"No. It is not a thing that waits to be asked, at that hour, by a tired man, and I was a tired man for some years." He turned his head a very little, not so far as Cael. "It was not spent. Nobody was on the road to see it, and I have the best reason of anyone living to know what it sounds like. I only remark that I heard a person stop being there, and I should prefer that you did not grow to like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:329:Cael sat down on the cart's shaft. He did what he had done on the east tier of a yard every week for two years, with a notebook on his knee, and it steadied him as it always had: he set out the claim, and what stood under it, and then he ruled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:331:The claim was that out here nobody on a road declared a Path, at any tier, for any purpose; or lit a fire he could do without, or spoke where a hand would serve; and that everybody showed steel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:337:"Hold," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:341:"Hold what?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:361:"And you?" said Lira, looking at him. "You've nine fragments, and not one of them's a Path. Does it count?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:363:"I don't know that either. So I hold all nine." It sounded like more, said aloud. "It's the one thing I've had real practice at."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:365:He wrote the day's lines in what light there was. *Sixth day. Deployment since the Line: none. Ruling: hold.* Then he put the book away and ate cold flour-cake with the rest of them, in the dark, with the river going by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:A cable ran over the river, bank to bank, sagging to the water in the middle. The old man had gone down to a post at the road's foot and was pulling on a cord, and the flat boat on the far side began to move, though nobody was rowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:57:There were fires here, and he felt Karis see it beside him, for her pencil came out of the spine of her notebook. There were cook-fires at half the tents, and a woman frying something over one, and smoke lying along the river in a low blue shelf. People were talking, too, in ordinary voices, and somebody laughed aloud. Down by the smithy a man was holding the end of an iron bar across the anvil with his bare forearm, and the arm was grey to the elbow, and nobody round him so much as turned to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:63:"No," said Cael. "It tells us where the ruling runs. We hold on a road. I'd hold here as well, until somebody's seen what we're like."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:65:A boy came for the standing-money. He was the same sort of boy as the one at the ferry, brown and quick, with a tally stick in his belt, and he walked round the cart and counted its wheels aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:87:Somebody had brought it up from some river long ago, twelve feet of oak as thick as his wrist, tarred black, and set it breast-high on two posts under the eave of the lower inn, where the weather could not get at it and the whole meadow could. Slips were tacked along it from one end to the other. They were on anything that would take a mark: bark, mostly, peeled thin and written on the pale side, and scraps of hide, and one cedar shingle, and a single piece of real paper that had been somebody's waybill and was written across the back. Each hung by one iron tack, and the oak round them was pitted with the holes of older tacks, hundreds of them, in drifts, so that he could see where the slips had clustered thickest in other years. It was a board that had been used hard for a long time by people who did not waste a nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:101:*Six crossings, the landing's goods; a crew to stand; two marks.* There was a seal on that one, pressed in dark wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:103:*Fence, the steading on the north water; posts cut and set; four marks and flour.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:107:*Shingles, the upper inn's lee side; one mark and board while it lasts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:109:*Fleece down from the high fold, eleven bales; a mark the bale, delivered dry.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:There were eight more. One wanted a night-watch kept on the landing, and one wanted stones picked out of a field, and one was a message to be walked to a hold four days off, for a mark and feed. Most were in the same square black hand, as if one person had written them out for others; but beside the words on each somebody had drawn a little picture, a wheel or a fence or a boat with sticks standing in it, for the use of people who could make nothing of the words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:Every name on the board in Ardenmere had carried letters after it, and a number: *Cu 3*, and the like. They had been ratings, borrowed from the registry's tiers and kept in somebody's private ledger, and a stranger could not have stood in that yard for a minute without being told where he stood among them. He had learned to read a card by them before he learned anything else; and here there was only a job, and whoever wanted it done, and what they would pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:129:Not one of the fourteen said who might take it: none of them asked for a tier, or a rank, or a Path of any kind, and none said *Iron or above*, or *a Bronze to lead*. The slip with the seal on it wanted a crew to stand on a boat, and it was all one to that slip whether the crew were five Golds or five carters' boys.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:133:Lira looked along the plank again, more slowly. He watched her understand it, and it was not a happy understanding. She had gone up a ladder a rung at a time since she was a girl, at Fenmark and in a yard and on a hill, and had paid for every rung with her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:163:He set the book on the plank. There was a hole bored in the oak at that place, the size of a hen's egg, and a horn ink-pot sat down in it to its lip, where no elbow could tip it; beside the hole lay a flat grey river stone as big as a hand. He opened the book and put the stone on the corner of the leaf. Cael understood then what it was for, since a man with one arm cannot hold a page down and write on it as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:239:"I had it ready. I've had it ready for a year. *Wind. Iron, Rank One.* I've a certificate for it in this coat, with a seal, and I thought he'd want to see that before he'd so much as write my name." She shook her head. "He wrote my name and went to his dinner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:243:"Iron, Rank Three," said Karis. "Certified, and retained. I've not had it counted since a counter, and I find that I expected him to count it." She considered. "I think I was looking forward to his declining."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:255:"I don't see how it works," said Lira. "That's what I can't get past. If there's no rank, then how does anybody out here know I'm better than I was last month?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:259:"That isn't a rank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:23:Nothing changed that Cael could have pointed to. A man's head was there on the green water, and then it was a thing that the eye did not care to rest on, like one stone among a great many. Behind eight others the ninth of his own fragments turned over, as a dog lifts its head at a voice it knows. It was Shadow, which had come to him out of that same Path, and he put it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:37:And it was dusk. He had known that it would be, and had thought of little else for the whole mile down. Shadow lifted behind the rest, soft, as it had lifted at every dusk for fifty days, with the one offer that he had ever wanted to take; and tonight he must stand still for an hour with it at his shoulder, in a place where being less would have been worth more than forty marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:79:*Every head but one.* He marked it, and knew that a man with his chin in a pool had marked it as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:123:Nine fragments came up in him at every snap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:133:Both of the two were up on the gravel now, and both were looking the wrong way. That made six, and he placed them once more: the two testers on the bar, the three on the near beach, which had not yet stirred a foot, and the torn ear at the tail. It was Brom's mark. Cael raised his left fist to the height of his shoulder and shut it twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:137:Iron Skin came up from both wrists together. It was the grey of a hearthstone, and it went over the backs of his hands and along his forearms and in under the rolled sleeves; it came out again at his collar and climbed the cords of his neck to the hinge of his jaw. His skin stopped creasing. In that light he looked like something a mason had left half finished on a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:167:The two that had followed came up to the stone through the water, and found no declaration there to meet them, and stood. One of them set its teeth in the tail of Brom's coat and tugged at it, twice. It was asking. He gave it no answer whatever, having a river to hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:179:It was a feinter. She had met a dozen of them, at Fenmark, on a chalked floor, with a coach walking the edge who gave a mark for every feint that was answered in time; and she had been the best in her year at it, since her answer was always the same one. When something came at your knee you were simply somewhere else. You were there before the eye of the girl opposite had finished telling her that you had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:207:She stood quite still. The feinter was in front of her and the other was behind, and there was a pool on either hand with no floor to it. Her feet wanted to go. Every rank that she had ever been given had been given her for going: there were twenty feet of air between her and the far beach, and she could have been standing on those stones before either animal had turned its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:241:It came to her at about the tenth stride, with something very like outrage. She had always supposed that the speed was the burst. The running had been the walk up to it, as a singer clears her throat; no panel had ever given her a rank for the running. She had not known that there was this much of it. The dog took the bend of the ridge straight, and its hind feet went into the soft, and she went round by the firm stones and had a yard back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:265:Her right foot rolled outward, and went on rolling. It went past the place where an ankle stops, and she heard it go. It made a small wet sound, like a green twig bent double, and she knew that sound. She had heard it once at Fenmark from a girl two places above her on the list, who had not been on the list the term after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:9:It was strange to walk that mile for a third reason. He had gone up it once behind a cart, for nine marks, with a grey thing pacing him; and he had come down it at noon behind the same cart empty, when it was nothing but grass. Now he went up it in the afternoon with a forty-mark slip in his shirt, toward a gate whose keeper had sent for somebody else. The ground was the same each time. What changed was the thing he had come to do on it, and he was beginning to think that this was the only sort of change of which the country out here took any notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:19:"He's a day and a half up the cold road," said Cael. "Pike struck his hold. In front of the meadow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:45:It was a long low room with a hearth across one end, and everybody in the hold was in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:131:He made a mark on the far bank, and a second, and a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:133:"They came one at a time; there were never two at the water together, and the next did not show until the one before had gone back into the grass. Each came to the last dry stone and stood on it with all four feet, and put its neck out over the river, and drank from there. One of them misjudged its stone, and a forefoot went in, no deeper than the first joint; it had that foot out again as you would have your hand out of a fire, and it stood for some time shaking it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:139:"Show me how it shook it. Was it the cold? Did it hold the foot up afterward, or set it down?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:147:"Six went back by three paths into the grass on the far side. I can give you the paths." He marked them, three short strokes. "They lie up within sixty yards of that beach, in the heat, and I did not go to look. So. Six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:155:"The seventh did not come down from any bank. It got up from here, where it had been lying on the gravel since before we arrived, among stones of its own colour, in the full light, with its chin on its feet; and it walked four yards to the far pool and drank, as the others had, dry-footed, and walked back and lay down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:169:"The rest is what I read in the gravel, and I hold it less firmly." Seln drew three short lines coming in from each bank toward the head of the bar. "On the forty-ninth, six came to those two men from the beaches; the marks are deep, and they are all about one place, here, at the top. The seventh's feet are at the tail and nowhere else. It stood there the whole time with its weight forward, and it moved only at the end, a matter of five yards, to meet a man who was coming down the gravel toward it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:197:"It's a lecturer's word: when you bring up Iron Skin, something leaves you besides the grey. An assessor feels it from across a floor and calls it presence, and gives you a mark for it; a station reads it off a brass plate. You felt your own on the gravel bar, the morning it came without asking." She tapped the page. "Nobody west of the Line has ever cared how far it carries, or what it might be like to something with a nose for it; there was never a reason to wish it small, and every school we went to taught us to make it larger."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:199:"So the hold isn't being quiet," said Lira slowly. "It's being dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:225:"There's a part of it that I ought to say first. When one of mine answers, I feel something go out, the same as you do. It isn't a Path, and I can't tell you whether one of those animals would know the difference." He looked at the charcoal river. "I've held all nine fragments for fifty-one days on the chance that it wouldn't, and I'm going on holding them. If the page is right, then what I've got is nine different ways of shouting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:289:"It will have been remarked," said Seln, "that there is a quieter way to put a knife behind the seventh dog than by sending a person down thirty yards of gravel to meet it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:7:"That's the hold's," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:33:It was too far to hear. The boy said what he had been sent to say, and it took him three breaths, and he said it to the mark on the paper. Then he said it over again at the same length. Somebody at a gate had made him learn it by heart, and had made him promise to give it twice, to a man who took everything twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:39:Then he reached up, and took hold of the tack in the slip that said *HOLD*, and worked it out of the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:49:"The third she's lost on that road since it dried. Two carts last month, and the people with them. Now a man of her own." Pike touched the thorn mark. "She sends two down every year when the water drops, to set the white stones along the lip of her ford, so that a wheel can see where the rock stops. They went at noon on the forty-ninth, and they were late coming away. One of them walked in at her gate after dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:77:"Thornwater ford. Clearance. Forty marks. The hold on it's struck, and it's me that's struck it. It's on the plank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:81:He set out the claim, since it steadied him. Forty marks were offered for a ford that the first name on the wall had asked to keep; and under that he had a road that he had walked four times, and five counts and a distance on a leaf in Karis's coat. And he had the hold, which was fifty days of declaring nothing on a road, and which a carter of twenty-two years had said he would as soon have as anything. It was not much to put under a claim of that size. He was still weighing it when a mild voice spoke at the far end of the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:105:"That's an Iron, Rank One," said Lira, "and an Iron, Rank Three, and a Copper. And two that haven't said. In case you wondered what you'd sent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:117:It was only as the feather went back behind the man's ear that he saw whose hand was on that paper besides theirs. The whole meadow had watched Pike strike a hold and send the bottom of his sheet to do the work of its top. Whatever came up from the ford, or failed to, he would have to stand at this plank and say it, twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:131:They were over the saddle before the mist had lifted, with a mark's worth of flour and bacon in the bag and nine marks left in Karis's purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:133:The road was the one they knew. It went down into the grey scrub in two ruts, and the cart fitted them as before; the three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas, and Karis walked at the off wheel. They passed the wash in the middle of the morning, dry and red, with its thorn stump on the eastern lip. They kept the hold without thinking of it, and Lira's knife and Brom's bill rode where a stranger could see them. In nearly every particular it was the walk they had made ten days ago for nine marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He had gone to a good many fights. He had gone to them down a lane in Ardenmere, and up the steps of a hall at Norhold, and across sixty feet of sprung oak every week for two years; and in every case somebody else had drawn the card. A keeper had matched him, or a bracket had, or a house that needed its charter, and he had walked out onto whatever floor they pointed to and tried to lose as little as he could. Nobody had drawn this one. A slip had been laid on a plank for anyone at all, and he had put up his hand, and reached for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:"A strong one. I never heard what tier, for he never said, and nobody asked him. I've had a wheel go off the groove on that rock four times in twenty-two years, and three of them he was there with his stones. He'd get down in the river to his chest and put his two arms under my tail-board, and it'd come up like a lid." The carter looked at his knife. "In the water. Laughing, the most of the time. He thought it was a great thing to do for a man."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:165:*Fifty-first day. The cold camp below the wash. A boy came down from Thornwater at first light: the ford has taken a third, a man of the hold's who set the stones on its lip. Pike struck Teague's HOLD with one line, in front of the meadow, and we put up five hands. Forty marks. I asked for this one. Nobody matched me to it. Entered, because I don't think I have ever written that before.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:"I go at my hour," he said. "I've done that for twenty-two years and I'll not stop for forty marks of somebody else's. I'll have your boxes in her loft before the sun's over." He looked at the bar, and then at the five of them, and Cael thought that he had something more to say. But all he did was nod, once, at Brom, as a man nods who has lent a tool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:213:"I timed myself," she said. "From when the canvas went out of sight. I looked at the river again in less than a minute." She took a cord out of her coat, with knots in it. "I believe that I'm improving. Will somebody pace for me? I want it in yards, and mine aren't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:219:The rock was worse than the carter had said. The green on it was a short close weed, and the water had combed it all one way, and a boot set down on it had no more hold than on a fish. Brom went along the lip on the near side with the bill reversed in his hand, feeling ahead of him with the ash haft as a blind man feels with a stick. Where the white stones stopped he reached out past the edge and let the haft go down. It went in to his fist, and his arm after it to the shoulder, and he drew it up again with nothing on the end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:263:Cael watched her come down it and thought of a painted floor at Norhold. She had been quicker there, with the burst, and she had never looked like this, for there she had been running at a mark. Here she ran thirty yards of loose stones for no one, and stopped because she had decided to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:279:So they went, and it was the hardest hold that Cael had kept since the stretch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:281:There was nothing to do it against. He lay on his back in scrub that smelled of dust, with the sun climbing to the top of the sky, and the fragments offered him things. Anchor had found the lip of the ledge from the first moment he saw it, and it asked about that edge now, patiently, though the edge was three hundred yards behind him. *There?* He told it that the stones were the hold's, and to leave them. Storm lay in him like a full pail and showed him the air above the river, cool and heavy and sliding downstream, as a lane half made already. Wind wanted the bar. It had watched Lira run that gravel three times and it had her stride by heart, and it leaned in his feet though they were pointing at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:287:He was dry. There was grass seed on his shoulders and down one sleeve, and he sat among them and held out his hand for the water-skin. He drank a good deal of it before he spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:23:It was strange to hear. Cael had read those words in a hold's yard, and thought them his; and now they belonged to a woman with a board of loaves on her head, and a man mending a trace, and an old carter under a tree, who had lifted his chin at the word *stretch* and held it there to the end. Several of the people at the plank turned and looked at the five of them. They looked once, at their boots and their steel, and then went back to their own affairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:29:"North road. Four carts of salt and iron walked to the upper holds. Delivered at the last gate. One mule lamed. Fourteen marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:71:He had brought fleece down from the high fold. Cael remembered the slip, for it had hung on the plank the day they came: eleven bales, at a mark the bale, delivered dry. The big man laid it down with a slap. Somebody at the landing had put a row of strokes on it for a witness, eleven of them, and through the last a short bar had been drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:75:"Fleece, down from the high fold. Eleven bales carried. Ten delivered dry, and one wet. Ten marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:81:"It rained on us the last mile. You can't call that the carrying." He had a good loud comfortable voice, and he looked round at the people by the plank as he used it, inviting them in. "Eleven bales went up on my mules and eleven came down to the landing. Eleven bales, eleven marks. That's the slip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:Cael did not understand it, and then he did, and the hair stood on his arms. It was no threat, for Pike would write it, exactly, as he wrote everything. He would put down that a captain said eleven dry, and beside it that a witness at the landing had marked ten; and then he would stand up and say both to two hundred people, and say them again when the ink was on. It would be in the book for as long as there was a book, and every hirer who ever asked about that crew would be told that its captain had once stood at this plank and argued with a mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:95:"Ten delivered dry, and one wet. Ten marks." Pike wrote, and said it over, and paid, and the man in the new coat went off down the meadow with his neck red.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:107:It was a piece of real paper, the first he had seen there since the waybill. There was a mark at its foot that he had watched a woman make with a charred stick, at a gate, by lantern light: a thorn twig, three spines to a side, and a wavy line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:109:*Thornwater ford. Clearance. Forty marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:113:*HOLD. Teague.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:"Forty," said Lira, beside him. Her voice cracked on it. "Forty *marks*. That's more than twice everything we've got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:123:"What's HOLD?" said Lira, across the plank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:151:"But why would anybody not *want* it written?" Lira's hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where a certificate was. "I'm Iron, Rank One. I bled for that. I'd have it on my forehead."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:175:He had turned it over all the way down from the saddle, Cael thought. The rule was that they ate what the board paid, and the board had that day paid nine marks into Karis's hand, and there was flour in the bag and bacon. He stood in the trodden grass outside the long eave and worked it through with his lips, and then he held up one finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:193:He stood there for some time, since a board in Ardenmere had carried him, once, with a question mark where the letters and the number should have been, and he had felt that question mark between his shoulders in every street of the district. This said only that there were five of them, and where they came in a list of thirteen. He looked for what it thought of him and could not find that it thought anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:197:He had a place, on the hearth bench, under the sheet, and nobody was sitting in it. Somebody put a cup in his hand before he was fairly down. Cael watched him drink off half of it, and then, because the thought had been with him since a scrap of bark in a hold's yard, he took his own cup and went over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:213:"Then I'd not have." Pike drank. "If she keeps it straight, it doesn't want me to have heard of her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:217:"We've four lines," she said. "Not a mark against any of them. That man today lost a bale and argued about it, and he's sixth. I counted. Why are we at the bottom?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:221:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:237:"There was a slip," said Lira. "On the plank, by the left post, on a shingle. Did you see it? A chest to be carried to the turf fire. *Eleven marks.*" She put her finger on the ring with the bar through it and ran it off the edge of the hide, toward the door. "That's a road we know. We know both fords on it. We know where the bad water is, and which keeper counts hay twice. It's nine days and nobody on it but deer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:239:"It's eleven marks," said Brom slowly. "That's more than the stretch paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:245:"The turf fire is a chartered house," he said. "Its keeper holds a station's brass, and he marks whatever is put in front of him. A chest has a carrier. A carrier has a name, which goes on a sheet, and the sheet goes down a hill in a bag."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:275:*Thirteenth of thirteen, and Pike gave the reason: it's a good book and a short one. Eleven lines between ours and the top one. The top one is a name, Teague, and it is also on a slip for Thornwater's ford, forty marks, with HOLD beside it. He goes out on the cold road at first light for a week.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:313:Forty marks, and Thornwater's mark at the foot. He had stood in front of that slip yesterday for longer than he stood in front of most, and then he had asked Pike to hold it. Pike had looked at him in that way of his, twice, and written the word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:329:The girl was standing at the sheet, and he read her from the feet. She had her weight on the balls of them, even standing still, even with her arms folded, and her heels hardly marked the rushes. Somebody had spent years teaching those feet to leave a floor in a hurry. They had been taught on a chalked floor, too, with someone watching to give a mark, for she stood as if she were being scored. She was fast, and proud of it, and she had not learned yet that out here nobody was going to tell her so; he thought that it would cost her something before she did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:345:*That one first*, he thought. He ought always to have been first. The one a man finds on his second pass is the one he should have looked for on the first, and Teague marked it against himself, as a thing to be remembered on a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:It was the third time he had been asked, in three different ways, and the first time the answer seemed to be put away somewhere for later. She took the slip from the carter's hat, where he had left it on the shaft. She read Pike's hand, slowly, and the five strokes under it, and then she fished a stub of charred stick out of her apron and made her mark on the bark, pressing hard. It was a thorn twig, three spines to a side, with a wavy line under it for water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:61:"Tell me in order," said Karis. "I'll write them in the morning. I think that if I tried to hold a pencil now I should break it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:65:By daylight it was a hold like any other, he supposed, though it was the first he had been inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:67:The fence closed off the whole head of the valley and ran on round, up one shoulder and down the other, a quarter of a mile about. Within it stood a long house, and the barn with the loft over it, and a yard for carts, and a well with a sweep; there were a dozen people or so, of every age, and a line of geese that hissed at all of them alike. The grain was the point of the place. It came up from the river country in sacks, on one man's cart, and it sat under that roof until other carts came down from holds farther east to fetch it, and the woman with the grey braid stood between the two and saw that nobody was cheated in either direction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:103:"No. They teach the other. Every school west of the Line gives you a rank for declaring quicker than the next person, and none of them has a word for this." He watched Lira go round. "We call it the hold. We're having to find it out for ourselves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:127:There were four prints, set square, where a thing had stood. Each was as broad as his palm and deeper at the toe than at the heel, and that depth told him how long. A creature that stands a moment leaves a mark like a seal in wax, and one that stands until its weight has settled forward leaves a mark like these; he judged that it had been there from the time a gate opened until the time an arrow went back into its case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:137:*Subject animal kept station on the party at forty-five yards for something over half a mile, without approach. Halted one hundred and ten yards from the hold's gate and remained until the gate was barred. Withdrew at a walk by the line of its coming. It was waiting upon a signal. The signal was not given. This officer does not know what the signal is.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:151:In the meantime he walked about with it, and listened for the level voice. *What did you see?* The woman of the hold did not ask it. She had watched him go out at noon and she would watch him come in, and she plainly thought that a man's errands at that stone were his own. The archer did not ask. The boy looked at the dust on his boots each evening, and at the direction it had come from, and wrote down, Seln was fairly certain, only that there had been dust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:193:There were both. They did it that afternoon in the cart-yard, the two of them, with the wheel off and laid across a pair of trestles. Brom cut the split timber out of the rim and shaped a new one to the curve with a drawknife, taking a shaving at a time and trying it and taking another, and the carter held what he was told to hold. It took them until the shadows were long. When the wheel was on again Brom spun it, and stood with his head bent, listening; it ran round four full turns and never ticked once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:203:It had been drawn in oak-gall ink and gone over, here and there, with a burnt stick. There were roads on it, each a double line, as a wheel sees a road. Three wavy strokes stood for a ford and a square with a dot in it for a hold. A ring with a bar through it lay on a river in the west, and Cael knew the ferry. Between one square and the next ran rows of small ticks, and he had counted the ticks from the ring to a square at the head of a valley before he understood that they were days. Roads went off the hide's edge to the north, and the south, and the east. Wherever a wheel had no business, he had put down nothing at all, and the skin was bare.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:217:He had not written much at Thornwater, for the first night he had not trusted his hand, and since then he had drawn water, and carried what Brom could not, and helped Karis to pace out a number along a fence. He was eighteen, and for three days he had been a hired man on a grain hold, and it had suited him better than he would have guessed. But there was a scrap of bark in the pocket of Hesk's book now, where two letters had lain, and it seemed to him that the book ought to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:219:*Forty-sixth day past the Line. Thornwater: a grain hold at the head of a valley, two days east of the board. Fourth line, in Pike's hand, copied and brought up by the carter. Nine marks, in his box. Earned, eighteen. In hand tonight, a few coppers and a map.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:221:*On the forty-third, at dusk, a thing the hold calls a stillhound went beside us for the last half of a mile. Five counts and a distance, given to Karis; she has the name and I have the numbers and neither of us has a reason. Nobody declared anything. Nobody drew. I put all nine down at every stride and they kept getting up.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:17:Karis had allowed the foot on her box, and nobody remarked on that either. Cael stood with one hand on the cart's rail. He had discovered, in the last half hour, that a mule's walk was the first pace in five days that suited his leg; he could go eleven strides beside the wheel and ride for a hundred, and the carter had only looked at him once and moved a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:21:"The board I've had out of the dark one," she said. "Forty sacks a day, and I'd not have thought it to look at him. The linen and the thread I'll be paid for. One mark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:41:"There's not. They stop inside, where the tiers are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:43:The woman said it as she might have said that barley stopped at a certain height on a hill. It was a thing about the ground, and nobody had decided it. One healer walked a route, and was not due; the rest of that trade kept to the country where somebody ranked it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:He saw before the mule was in the water that the hold's men had been down. There was nothing at all on the gravel; and along the lip of the rock the white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a yard apart, and did not stop until they reached the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:97:"The hold stands," he said. "For everything any of us carries, on any road, and I'm not changing a word of that." He turned to Seln. "It doesn't cover a tinderbox any longer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:109:"Lit with a flint," he said. He said it as a man reads the mark on a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:149:"It bends there," he said. "So that's where it opens. The rest's holding. I only steadied her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:179:"At Norhold there was a sheet. I lost to a girl that a whole program had built, with all of you at the rail, and when it was over the sheet told me to a tenth of a point how far I was from Silver. I hated it, and I kept it; I've still got it somewhere, in with the certificate." She looked into the flames. "And on the hill there was the ladder. Nobody had ever made me anything, and they made that to my height, with my name on the bottom rung. I knew where I was, every morning. Iron, Rank One, and I could have told you to a rung how far above it I meant to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:187:"That woman said the healers stop inside, where the tiers are. I've had it going round since her gate. It isn't only healers that stop there. It's the sheets, and the ladders, and the ranks. So what am I becoming, exactly, if nobody's measuring?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:191:Brom's mouth opened, and shut again. He had a question of his own that was first cousin to hers, and he had been carrying it since a jug; Cael could see him hold it up beside hers and conclude that it did not fit. Karis could have given an answer by method, with a table in it, and she had the sense to know that it was not what had been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:195:He had the sentence ready; it had come to him at once, whole, and it was the worst thing he could have said to her. *Isn't it better?* He had sat in a bar of red light ten days ago and written that no instrument out here could read him, and that he was free; and he had meant every word, and he meant it still. Not one book in this country had a column for a tier or a rank, or for what he was, and it was the best thing that had happened to him since a brass plate went dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:199:If he told her that it was better so, he would be asking her to feel his relief in place of her own loss. And there was a second thing that he might have said, which was worse: *I saw it. I'll tell you how good it was.* He had counted fighters from the east tier of a yard for two years, and he could have given her a figure for those thirty yards that she would have believed; and then he would be the instrument, and she would have a registry again with one clerk in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:251:"Four marks," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:253:"Four marks, and no dogs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:257:"I walked five roads out of this meadow before any of you had used one, and three more out of a hold, and I lay in the grass above a river. On each evening of those fifty days I have come back and sat down, and looked up." He ran his thumb along the bark's edge. "I keep waiting for someone to ask for my report."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:289:"I've a line to say for you, and I've said it once already. And I've forty marks in that box." He swung the sack onto the plank. "But the ferry's in, and it's mail first. It's always mail first. There's people in this meadow have waited a month to hear their name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:317:"Then that's a fifth of forty," he said. "Eight marks. I'd pay eight for that to a Gold or to a goat, if the goat came back." He dipped his pen. "There's no column for what it was done with. I thought you'd read that off the page the first day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:She had gone up to the eave on purpose to be first. Cael came after her at his own pace, which on level grass was anybody's pace now, and he took his inventory on the way, as he took it at the start of anything. The calf was a leg again on the flat; he had asked it the evening before, on the saddle road, and it had told him nine strides uphill and then shut. Brom's forearm was closed along the whole of its seam and wrapped by Brom's own choice, and it was to pull nothing. Lira had wound a strip of linen round her right ankle, over the stocking, and she walked on the whole sole of that foot. Karis had two pink lines across each palm and held a pencil for a quarter of an hour at a time. The nine fragments lay in him as they had lain since the ford, idle, each with its rent paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:7:There were fifty-two marks in the purse, and the name at the top of the sheet was still up the north water on two holds' slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:21:*The high traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. To be walked with a cart, and mapped. Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:31:"It's the same thing with a wheel on it." Pike laid his one hand on the paper. "Oxhollow's six days north-west of this plank, and Thornwater's two days east of it. A cart from the one that wants grain from the other comes round by my ferry, and it's eight days, and eight home. There's high ground between the two holds. The salt crews say there's a shelf along the top of it, a bench, with a gorge down one side. A mule string went over it once, before I'd this book, and came down in three days." He looked at the horned head, twice. "Somebody's told the ford so. The ford wants to know whether it's true before it sends a wheel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:39:"It's six days of road, and he'd not trust seventy marks to the inside of a carter's hat. I'd not, either. That's what *on the map* means. You put the map in his hand, and he puts seventy in yours, and his mark goes on that paper under my strokes. Then you bring the paper here, and I say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:49:Cael read the slip through once more, for the thing that was not in it. There was no name on it but the hold's. Nobody had asked to have it kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read. There was a fence to be set for a steading on the north water, at five marks, and a wheel to be fetched for a carter on the south road, and hay to be got in at the upper inn for a mark and the loft to sleep in. A string of mules wanted walking westward to the turf fire, and that slip he passed over without stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:59:The first was the plainest: it paid. Seventy marks was more than Pike's book had counted out to them in six lines, and the crew was still four hurts short of whole.

