editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:207:Other ruts came into theirs out of side valleys, one after another, like streams joining; and by the fifth day's noon the track was a cart wide and beaten hard, with the grass gone from its middle. The valley had opened. The red trees stood back on the slopes, and there was pale grass in the bottom, shoulder high, that went over in the wind like a cat under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:343:"All of it. Whatever each of you has. Nobody declares anything on a road, for any reason, until we know what it brings." He kept his voice where the old man had kept his. "Not a Wind burst. Not Iron Skin. Not an Ember point to light a pipe by."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The country came down as they went. The red trees thinned and gave over to a broad pale tree with leaves that showed silver when the wind turned them, which Karis also declined to name, and the river they were following grew slow and wide and took in others. It was warm in the bottom. The dust stood behind the carts until long after they had passed, and there were flies about the gelding's eyes all day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:239:"I had it ready. I've had it ready for a year. *Wind. Iron, Rank One.* I've a certificate for it in this coat, with a seal, and I thought he'd want to see that before he'd so much as write my name." She shook her head. "He wrote my name and went to his dinner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:281:There was nothing to do it against. He lay on his back in scrub that smelled of dust, with the sun climbing to the top of the sky, and the fragments offered him things. Anchor had found the lip of the ledge from the first moment he saw it, and it asked about that edge now, patiently, though the edge was three hundred yards behind him. *There?* He told it that the stones were the hold's, and to leave them. Storm lay in him like a full pail and showed him the air above the river, cool and heavy and sliding downstream, as a lane half made already. Wind wanted the bar. It had watched Lira run that gravel three times and it had her stride by heart, and it leaned in his feet though they were pointing at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:353:That was the part worth a price. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and anybody could tell a Skin by how he came through a door. This one sat like none of them. There was nothing in the set of him to say what he had in his hands, and nothing to say that they were empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:287:"Then it's me, and it's Wind." He had thought about that as well. "There'll be a man on that bank with a bow, and he's her witness; and Wind is the one thing I carry that he'd take for a Path, and think no more about. But I'd as soon it didn't come to a third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:317:He wrote the day while Brom trimmed the wick. *Fifty-second day. Thornwater. Seven at the ford, by Seln's count, and one of them lies on the bar. Karis has a hypothesis, in two clauses, and tomorrow at dusk we test both. If I spend anything it will be Wind, once, where it can be seen. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:151:For the first quarter of a mile there was nothing but the noise, and Cael had not known until then how much noise a quiet valley makes. There were frogs. The bottom was wet, somewhere under the grass, and they were in it by the hundred, a creaking that rose and fell like something breathing. There were crickets, nearer, in a dry steady saw, and some small bird that ticked. Over all of it the wind went through a mile of standing grass with a sound like a hand drawn slowly down a coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:Wind had his feet first. They had leaned for a month, a dozen times a day, and they leaned now toward the rise as if the ground sloped that way. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, slowly, a breath at a time. The Iron read wanted to open and tell him the mass of everything within a pace and a half, which was nothing but a mule; it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. Ember offered a point in the dry grass at the foot of the swell, and behind it another and another, so that he could see how the whole slope would take fire along a line. Anchor had found the wired rim and asked about it. Blade wanted Brom's bill. Storm turned over, once, like a great fish under a boat, and showed him the air between himself and the grey thing as a lane already laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:215:*No*, he told it, and Wind, and Pressure. *No. Rim; his; leave it. No.* He said it to each of them at one stride and to each of them again at the next. They were small refusals, one after another, quicker than he could have put names to them, and none of them was any harder than lowering a hand; it was only that there were nine hands, and that they rose again as often as he put them down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:103:It turned on him faster than a thing of that size ought to turn. Its teeth shut on his left sleeve and went through the cloth of his coat as if it had been paper, and he felt the wind of them on his wrist. Then its legs went from under it, and it was dead before it had finished coming round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:125:It had been easier on the stretch. Nothing had touched him there. Compression had waited all these weeks for a blow, and each time the teeth met in his coat it rose to take the weight and send it down through his heels. Wind begged for his feet. Pressure had begun to gather under his breastbone, a breath at a time, where anything might feel it gathering. He put them down, and they got up, and he put them down again, while a grey thing tore the hem of his coat to ribbons.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:161:The current took the hound's hindquarters. It swept them round and out over the lip, into the falling water, and the river pulled one way and Brom's heel against the rim of his stone held the other, and the animal was drawn out straight between them like a flag in a wind. Its jaws dragged down his arm as the river hauled on it. He let them drag. He kept his shoulder where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:185:Her whole body knew what to do with this animal. She was Wind. The burst sat in her calves like a held breath, and it had sat there since she was fourteen, and for fifty days she had been taking a stride at a time away from it, on a river bar, with a boy telling her how who had been her partner since a lane in Ardenmere. *You say it at the first stride. Then you say it again at the second.* She said it at the snap. She said it again as the thing sprang back, and again as it settled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"I ran thirty yards of gravel at that ford with nothing in my feet," said Lira. "No Wind. No burst. I caught a dog. I've never run like it. I didn't know I could, and I'm telling you it was the best thing I've ever done on my two legs, and it cost—" She stopped. Her hand had gone toward the stave, and she took it back. "It cost what it cost. And there isn't a person alive who can say whether it was good. There's a man with a bow who lifted two fingers. There's a book that says nobody died."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:229:"You're the only one of us who can stand at that crank and brace. Lira can wind, sitting. If I come up limp, somebody with two legs will have to get me over the curb." Cael was tying a loop to sit in. "And a leg's no use to anybody on a rope."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:235:"I shall count," she said. "Aloud, by tens, and you'll answer every ten. If you miss one, they wind, and I shan't call it a second time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:447:*The nine: held. Wind leans, Anchor asks at every door of the inn, and Shadow lifts at dusk and is told no. I pay the rent on all of it and spend none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:85:*Wind, from Lira. It wants the first stride of anything, and my feet lean a dozen times a day before I've decided. If I ever spend a burst, the landing locks for one count, and that count has never got shorter.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:249:He had walked the east road on the ninth evening, within an hour of stepping off a ferry, as far as a saddle and a little beyond it, and the north before light on the tenth. The south road had cost him two mornings, since it forked. The fourth was the road they had all come in by, and he had gone back along that one too, three miles, to see it as a person sees it who is arriving. This was the fifth. It left the north road at a dead tree and climbed, and the meadow called it cold; he learned why at the first turn, where a wind came down off bare ground that had no business in such mild weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:277:The case knocked against his hip. He turned at the crest, where the wind was, and went down.
[bow]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:217:"*Cleared*, and a colon after it," she said. "He's never used one before, and everything after the colon is what *cleared* cost. And *five confirmed*: he won't write seven, and he won't write that two went down the river, because nobody counted those with a bow; he writes what somebody stood over." She looked up. "The last noun has changed again. *Nothing lost* was casks, and *no losses* was a cart and a mule. *None lost* is us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:151:The mule came up out of the shallows beside their cairn and stood dripping. The woman on it was fifty or so, brown and spare, with a lip that had been split a long time ago and had healed a little crooked. Her hat had the brim cut away on the right side. A hatchet hung in a loop at her saddle-bow, edge outward, where anybody could see it, and behind her lay two flat leather bags with long flaps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:29:It took them the better part of an hour to work out how. The boat was a scow, square at both ends, with a rail, and it hung on the cable by two running blocks; a second rope went from its bow to a round shed on the far bank, open at the sides, and inside that shed something walked in a circle. When it walked, a drum turned and the rope came in. When it stopped, which it did every few turns, a man's voice came across the water, patient and tired, and after a while it went on again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:15:The woman with the grey braid stood in the gap, as she had stood there once with a lantern, and counted them through with her lips: the dark coat, the two abreast, the small pale one, the large one. She shut the gate behind Brom with her shoulder and dropped the bar. Up on the platform the bowman was looking down the valley over their heads, with his bow hanging at the length of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:287:"Then it's me, and it's Wind." He had thought about that as well. "There'll be a man on that bank with a bow, and he's her witness; and Wind is the one thing I carry that he'd take for a Path, and think no more about. But I'd as soon it didn't come to a third."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:255:He came slowly, flat-footed on the green rock, with a bow held up out of the wet. He had been told not to come down, and had told them so himself. He stopped at Brom's stone and stood looking into the deep water under the lip. Then he came up onto the gravel and went about it with the tip of his bow, touching each one as a woman touches loaves on a board: the first, at the far landing; Lira's, half in the river; the one that lay beside the boulder. He walked the thirty yards down to the tail by himself, and walked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:341:"Five dead," said the bowman, from the door. "I counted them with the bow. Two took to the water and went down with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:247:That came to her in the middle of the run as well, and she had no time to do anything about it. There was no chalk. No slate hung on any wall. A man stood on a bank with a bow who might or might not have been able to make her out at that distance. She was running the best twenty-five yards of her life for no one at all, and she noticed that she minded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:259:She was two strides from the edge with all that speed in her, and she did what she had spent a month on a river bar failing to do, and one morning under a platform doing at last while a man lifted two fingers from a bow. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:201:In the middle the river had built itself an island. It was a bar of gravel, a long narrow heap of stones that sat on the ledge like a boat run aground upon a weir, with its blunt bow upstream and its stern trailing away below, between the two dark places, to a point. The road came up out of the water onto the bar and crossed it and went down into the water again on the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:There was a platform beside the gate, on the inner face of the fence, six feet up, with a ladder to it. A man stood there. He had a bow in his left hand and an arrow on the string, and Cael had seen enough people threaten him to know that this one was not doing it. The bow hung at the length of his arm. The arrow pointed at the planks between his boots. He was looking out over the top of the posts, down the valley, at something a hundred and ten yards off beside a white stone, and he did not once look round at the people who had just come in under him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:79:Brom's left arm took it. The knuckles went white and the shoulder stood out through his shirt, and the ladder bowed like a drawn bow and came back; and when he had found the rung with his foot he went on up, more slowly, and set the sack down in the loft before he looked at his own hands. They were brown, both of them. Iron Skin had not come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:93:The bowman had been watching her come. He was a lean, weathered man of no particular age, who seemed to live on that platform, and Cael had not yet heard him use his voice. He looked down at Lira standing under him with her hands on her knees. Then he lifted two fingers from the stave of his bow, an inch, and laid them back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:Down in the yard Brom was spinning a wheel again, for the pleasure of hearing nothing, with a row of geese drawn up behind him like a jury. Lira sat on the well's coping, binding the hilt of her knife. On the platform by the gate the bowman stood as he always stood, with his bow at the length of his arm, looking down the valley at a white stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:303:He did it without turning his head. He had done it so for longer than the boy had been able to lift a pack, at every door and gate and ford, and he no longer knew that he was doing it until it was done. The spear was on his left, half a pace back, grounded. The bow was on his right and a full pace back, standing on the good leg, as the bow always stood when there had been a day's walking. The boy was between and behind, where he had been told to be, and by the sound of him he was already looking for the table with the food on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:51:Up on the platform the bowman stood with his bow at the length of his arm. He lifted two fingers from the stave as the cart went under him, and Lira raised the ash stick an inch from her knees, and set it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"I ran thirty yards of gravel at that ford with nothing in my feet," said Lira. "No Wind. No burst. I caught a dog. I've never run like it. I didn't know I could, and I'm telling you it was the best thing I've ever done on my two legs, and it cost—" She stopped. Her hand had gone toward the stave, and she took it back. "It cost what it cost. And there isn't a person alive who can say whether it was good. There's a man with a bow who lifted two fingers. There's a book that says nobody died."
[row]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:Karis had the field archive open, by the light. She had written five words across the head of a clean leaf, in her small exact hand, with a ruled line under them. Now she went back along the row and drew a second line beneath one of the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:117:It made three shelves in a row, as tall as her shoulders when she sat, with no wall behind them and a barn all round; and a hen walked along the top of the middle one and was removed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:277:He put no question to them. He let down the tail-board, and then he walked out along the rock by himself, in the last of the light, as far as a white stone that lay a little out of its row; and he picked it up in both arms and carried it to the lip, and set it down at the end of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:He had been awake a good while, as Cael knew by the gelding, who had been watered and picked out and now stood with a full net in front of him, and by the tailboard itself, which had been swept clean. On the clean oak Brom had set down four small heaps of grain in a row and a fifth by itself, and he waited until all of them were looking before he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:He saw before the mule was in the water that the hold's men had been down. There was nothing at all on the gravel; and along the lip of the rock the white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a yard apart, and did not stop until they reached the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:71:He had brought fleece down from the high fold. Cael remembered the slip, for it had hung on the plank the day they came: eleven bales, at a mark the bale, delivered dry. The big man laid it down with a slap. Somebody at the landing had put a row of strokes on it for a witness, eleven of them, and through the last a short bar had been drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:It took him perhaps a minute: two lines for the banks, and a bar across them for the rock, and on the bar a long shape like a shut eye, lying with the stream; and below the rock, on the near side, he set a row of dots and stopped it five dots short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:141:"Forty," he said, and walked them along the fence to where a stretch of it had gone at the butts and leaned outward like a row of drunkards. "Cut them in the coppice. A stride apart, and deep. There's an axe." He turned back toward the house, and the dog stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:19:Nobody was at it, and the book was not out. The slips hung in their row along the tarred oak, and the boy looked down the whole length of them without reading, as someone looks who has no letters and has been told what a thing is like. He found it by the right-hand post. Cael watched him put up his hand and lay one finger on a piece of real paper, low down, on a thorn twig with a wavy line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:195:Above the ledge the river was a long slow glide. Below it there was a row of white stones, each the size of a loaf, set along the rock's downstream edge at intervals of a yard; and beyond the white stones the water was a different colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:199:Cael saw. The white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a dozen of them, and stopped; and for the last five yards before the middle of the river there were none. One lay on the rock by itself, a little out of the line, where somebody had set it down meaning to come back to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:233:Nothing remained of the forty-ninth evening except an iron bar, lying on the gravel where it had been put down, and the one white stone out of its row. At the tail of the bar the stones had been raked up in long scars, as the sand of a ring is after a bout, and something had since gone over them and smoothed them partly down. It might have been the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:Down in the yard Brom was spinning a wheel again, for the pleasure of hearing nothing, with a row of geese drawn up behind him like a jury. Lira sat on the well's coping, binding the hilt of her knife. On the platform by the gate the bowman stood as he always stood, with his bow at the length of his arm, looking down the valley at a white stone.
[minute]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:Every name on the board in Ardenmere had carried letters after it, and a number: *Cu 3*, and the like. They had been ratings, borrowed from the registry's tiers and kept in somebody's private ledger, and a stranger could not have stood in that yard for a minute without being told where he stood among them. He had learned to read a card by them before he learned anything else; and here there was only a job, and whoever wanted it done, and what they would pay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:9:It was an old habit, and he had it from the hill he had left a week ago, where somebody had always been set to watch him. For a year there had been two men at the foot of a road with a tin cup between them, and a third on a tier with a pencil, and a mild clerk at a copying table who had turned out to be the best of the four; and he had learned to find each of them in the first minute of any morning, as a man feels for his purse. He ran the count now over a country he could not see. He took the ridge behind them first, and the scree, and the black shoulder to the north where a night bird kept its two notes, and then the gulf in front, in sections, from the near ground outward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:39:*No*, he told it. He ground his heel in the gravel, and the stones answered him, loudly. He did it again a minute later, to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:297:"You called before the candle did," said Karis. She was kneeling by him, and her voice had the brightness it took on over a clean result. "By a minute, I should say. What told you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:331:A carter came up the south fork with two mules while the last bucket stood on the curb. He stopped them at the edge of the halting-ground and stood watching Brom, plainly waiting to learn whether a large young man would drop where he stood. When a full minute had gone by he came and dipped his own cup, and then he scratched a wheel on the slip with the point of his knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:213:"I timed myself," she said. "From when the canvas went out of sight. I looked at the river again in less than a minute." She took a cord out of her coat, with knots in it. "I believe that I'm improving. Will somebody pace for me? I want it in yards, and mine aren't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:It took him perhaps a minute: two lines for the banks, and a bar across them for the rock, and on the bar a long shape like a shut eye, lying with the stream; and below the rock, on the near side, he set a row of dots and stopped it five dots short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:39:"Every time you do anything at all, until it's worn in. I had a book for it, on the hill. What to take late, and what to give. I refused something or other every minute of every Fifth-day for two years, and I thought it was the price of being let stay. I didn't know I was learning a trade."

